      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS,</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS,</p>
        <p>Because</p>
        <p>WHY is love like a map of the world? It embraces a great deal. A SUBURBAN cobbler displays the following notice :—"All sorts souled and healed, 35," 'Tis better to have a slip 'twist the cup and the lip than to have one 'twist the nose and razor. YOUNGLY—" Love often compels people to do very silly things." Cynicus—" Yes, makes them marry sometimes." LITTLE ELSIE —"Who was Shakespeare, pa?" Scribblems (the playwright)—" One of my pre. decessors, child. • PENELOPE—" Why did you refuse him ?" Per. dita—" He had the audacity to buy the ring before he knew whether I would accept him or not." BARNES said he was not asleep in chunch. 'Well, what was the sermon about?' said a friend. "About an hour and a quarter long," replied be. WITII regard to Purgatory," said a Popish writer, "I will not say a great deal; but this ranch I think, that Protestants may go further, and faro worse." SEEM-COMIC, log.— -c` My dear, what splendid time you keep in your skirt dance! How do you manage it ?" " W ell, you see, dear," replied her sister artiste, "I always wear clocked stockings l" TIIACK PRAY was at St. Louis at dinner, when one waiter said to another :—" That is the cele- brated. Mr. Thaclceray." "What's he done ?" said the other. "Blessed if I know," was the answer. "No, my eon; people do not argue to get at the truth of a matter. They simply wish to prove that their position is the true one, even if they have to lie to prove that they are telling the truth."</p>
        <p>CECIL: "No, Weggy ; you must not tempt me —weally you must not. I pwomised hr I would smoke Imt three eiguwette3 a day." Reginald: CYI, Cecil, deah boy! how you must love her r</p>
        <p>"I ITEAR you are going to marry Miss Bullion. I should think you would marry Goldie Sterling; she is just as rich, and much younger." "Yes, my dear, but Miss Bullion's papa is much older." ONE of the best kinds of training for a'pedes. trian is for a young man to have his young woman live about 20 miles from his home, with no railway or tram communications, and the horses all busy. A GENTLEMAN, while panting with asthma, was almost deafened by a brawling fellow who was selling oysters. "The extravagant rascal," said he, "he has wasted as much breath in an hour as would have served me for a month."</p>
        <p>EDINBtTRLI MIN : "Aberdeen has a hundred thousand odd inhabitants, hasn't it?" Dundee Man : "Odd is no name for it. Crazy, mad— that's what they are." Vox: "My love, why ever do you put another woman's hair on your head?" Mrs. Velox : "For the same reason, I suppose, that you put the skin of another calf oa your hands."</p>
        <p>" WHY, Bridget," exclaimed the housewife, "I can write my name in the dust here." "Deed, ma'am," replied Bridget, admiringly, "that's more nor I can do. There's nothing like education afther all, is there, ma'am?"</p>
        <p>WuEN a woman says to you "Go away! don't want to see ytu again," you may live in hope. If, on the other hand, she says: "I shall always be glad to see you," you may take your hook.</p>
        <p>HE (just after the proposal) : "I'm so delighted, my darling, that you do not object to smoke." She "Oh, no, I like it ; but mamma can't bear it." He (softly): " You are my first thought. I will smoke continually when we have our little house."</p>
        <p>ABLE and dirty•bodied beggar (to Corporal of Horse): "Give us a penny, capting. Can't get no work—starvitf." C. of H. (g(ving a penny): "Why dcm't you enlist, then?" A. and D. B. B. (pocketing money) : "On I I ain't come as low as that yet.'</p>
        <p>Alacz (aged 7 years): "Papa, were there any live.rebels after the Battle of Bull Run?" 'Father : " Why, of course, my Child. Why do you ask that ?' Alice: "Uncle George told me about the battle last night, and I thought he killed them all."</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE WORKER: "And what caused your downfall, my good man'" Horrible Example: "It was this stage realism, mum. I was acting the drunkard in a temperance play, and the manager insisted on my using real whisky, mum."</p>
        <p>SHE : "You seem to be the best man at all the weddings. When are you going off yourself ? " He: "Oh, there's as good fish in the sea as ever came out of it." She: " Yea ; but don't you think the bait is getting rather stale ? " "You must wake me at the usual hour in the inorning," said he to his wife on the eve of the Bank Holiday. "You are not going to the office, are you ?" "No ; but I want to have the supreme satisfaction of rolling over and going to sleep again.'</p>
        <p>Aunlcurrrumrsr (to season ticket-holder in the train) : " You don't have no ticket?" Ticket- holder: "No; I travel on my good looks." Agriculturist (after looking him over) : " Then probably you aint's gob' far." General smile. PATIENT: "The examination seems to have de- lighted you, doctor. I judge from your happy countenance that you can save my life." Dr. Saw- bones : "I cannot promise you that; but we must perform a number of most interesting operations on you." " WomAN is a creature of moods," said the" young man as he toyed with a note he had just received from his best girl. "You are right," replied his friend ; " and after you are married you'll find that it is usually the imperative mood."</p>
        <p>INSTRUCTOR IN LATIN.—" Miss 8., of what was Ceres the goddess?" Miss B. : " She was the goddess of marriage." Instructor: "Oh, no ; of agriculture." DI iss B. (looking perplexed) : "Why, I am sure my book says she was the goddess of husbandry."</p>
        <p>MAMMA, reprimanding her three-year-old son who is flagrantly disregarding table etiquette: "If we were at another's table I should be -so ashamed of you I should not know where to hide my head." Young Diogenea, not at all abashed: "You could put it under the table." Fr is related of Sydney Smith that on entering a drawing-room in a West End mansion, he found it lined with mirrors on all sides. Finding him- self reflected in every direction, he said that he " supposed:he was at a meeting of the clergy, and there seemed to be a very respectable atten- dance.'</p>
        <p>A DIAN, beleiving himself in extremis, sent for his enemy, and said—" The quarrel between us is all over; give me your hand." As the other was leaving, after shaking hands, the sick man Cried ont—" If I should not die this time, mind, all this goes for nothing e' "WELL, Mrs. Brown, how does your daughter get along on the piano ? ' "Well, Mrs Jones, you knew I ain't n9_ musician myself; but I did hear her-teacher say only yesterday—' Emma, my child, you're quite ten bars ahead !' so she must be making some progress mustn't she ?" Marrnx : "I don't know what I am going to do I" Ethel: "Why what is the matter ?" Maude : " Why, Mr. Feuer of Paris was talking very earnestly to me is French last night, and I didn't quite understand him, and ho spoke so impetuously I replied ' Oui, oni,' several times ! It has just occurred to me that perhaps he was proposing !" "BEE here, Mr. Yorick," exclaimed his neigh- bour one day; "I get tired of having old tin cans thrown into my garden, so this morning I went and pitched into your wife about it." " Well, well," replied Yorick, "I always heard you were no slouch, but I really didn't think you were dare- devil enough for that." Ma. JONES, being on a visit to a friend in a Texas town, and having a good voice, was urged to sing in the local choir. He at last yielded. "I hear you have at list consented to sing in our choir?" said a lady on meeting him. "Yes, I have at last yielded. I believe that when you are at Rome you ehould howl with the wolves." AT a meeting of a certain local vestry lately, it was debated whether or no a chandelier should be purchased for the town hall. "Well, gents," at length put in one of the economy members; "it's all very well for you to talk 13- wasting the ratepayer's money on a chandelier, but what I want to know is, when you've got it, who's a-going to play on it?" He thought it was a species of piaso, apparent'</p>
