        <head>Z I , + – , j 'J . .1\ . .., ;' r• i , , ~,,m),....,:, ! N – Wil: ' ' . -. + . tAr. :40.1PAki ti • . ex, „ . a, la, : • ',.. : ! ! • i-.. i 't\Nl 1 1 i ;' I- '4't f- fi l' ,i ;.1 , • , . , .-- • • js- ) • THE FXAMig' ER. 'ISAIIIS. IlliA Y. , . SEI;FEN.II3-ER; ) 9 – – : J ; -i ,</head>
        <p>Z I , + - , j 'J . .1\ . .., ;' r• i , , ~,,m),....,:, ! N - Wil: ' ' . -. + . tAr. :40.1PAki ti • . ex, „ . a, la, : • ',.. : ! ! • i-.. i 't\Nl 1 1 i ;' I- '4't f- fi l' ,i ;.1 , • , . , .-- • • js- ) • THE FXAMig' ER. 'ISAIIIS. IlliA Y. , . SEI;FEN.II3-ER; ) 9 - - : J ; -i ,</p>
        <p>mushrooms, Sze., are sufficiently cooked. Strain the soup through a hair sieve, boil up, and add the mush- rooms, cut in slices, just before serving. Fresh mush- rooms, when cleaned and peeled, may be set in an earthenware vessel with some stock.and a piece oi butter as large as an egg, covered and allowed to cook slowly for half an hour. Add a teaspoonful of potato-flour or of pulled flour, pounded quite fine, a dash of lemon-juice, and salt to taste. Let the soup simmer for some time, then stir in the yolk of one or more eggs, and serve. A Novel Swedish Dish. One pound of round steak, one pound of pork steak (chopped fine), one onion (chopped fine), one white cab- bage (boiled in boiling salted water ten or fifteen minutes, or until tender). After you have chopped the beef and pork steak, also the onion, very fine, add one egg (well beaten) to the mixture, season well with salt and pepper, add about half a pint of milk, and half a cupful of biscuit cymbs ; mix well. Take a tablespoon- ful of the mixture and brown it in butterto see if it is of the right consistence—if it holds its shape it is right. Take the cabbage from the stove; drain it, take one cab- bage leaf, put a tablespoonful of the meat mixture iuto the leaf and tie it up; then take another leaf, pu's, an- other tablespoonful of the mixture in the leaf and tie up; keep doing this till all the cabbage leaves and meat are , used up. Take a very large spoonful of butter and put it in your meat-pan on top of the stove. When the butter is melted, put in the cabbage leaves which you haw tied into the pan ; brown on one side ; then turn and brown On the other ; when all are brown, add a little hot water, and cook slowly on top of the stove for twapty minutes. When ready to serve, dissolve one large tablespoonful of flour in the same quantity of milk, put it into the meat- pan, stir until smooth, then:add one cupful of cream or milk.</p>
        <p>. Charcoal. Charcoal is of great value in keeping store rooms and food sweet. In milk-rooms and in rooms where food is kept set dishes of charcoal. If poultry or birds are to be hung they should not be plucked. Force some pieces of charcoal into the body and under the wings, and hang up. Small birds, livers, kidneys' sweetbreads, &amp;c.. may be wrapped in paper, and then be buried in a bed of charcoal. For keeping large pieces of meat and poultry here is a simple device : Have a large barrel or hogshead half filled with charcoal. Put meat-hooks in a strip of joist and place across the top of the barrel. gave a netting to pread over this. This barrel may be kept in a cool place, and pieces of meat be hung on the hooks. The charcoal will keep the atmosphere dry and sweet, and the netting will be. a protection against in- sects. Shoold there be danger fiom rats or mice, use wire netting. Fresh fish may be;rpbbeci with salt, wrapped in paper, and bailed ma bed of charcoal. Of course, the charcoal in boxes and barrels liipuld be changed at least dace s month. It 'cab be used for lighting fires, or for broiling meats or fish, If, however, it is difficult to get a good supply of charcoal, the old can be purified by putting it into the stove With a few lighted chips and allowing it to, burn unfirred hot. At this stage operi all the windows, to let the gas pass off : then close the draughts of the stove, remove the covers and leave the roem. When the charcoal becomes cold it will be ready for use again.</p>
        <p>Teach Girls Housekeeping. It rests generally with the mothers, the one most in- terested in the future welfare of the daughters, to teach them arts and household duties that may prove useful to them in the years to come. It is a good plan, therefore, to let daughters take the entire management of the household for a few weeks at a time. For the first week or so the mothers might give a hint now and again, but the girls themselves should give orders to the trades- people, and so on. The girls, on their part, should be particular to see that the • meals are nicely cooked and served up at the proper time, and that all other house- hold duties are performed in the same way as if mother had the control. A good practical knowledge of house- hold matters are among the things that all girls should understand. Since the advent of the cleanly gas stove, with all its numerous jets, which can be turned up and down at will, regulating the work in hand to a nicety, ladies are doing a great deal more of their own cooking, Sm., than formerly, and this means to them a sav- ing of money. In a household of grown-up girls, the total of the weekly washing is to some mothers a serious item. There is nothing undignified in a young lady washing and ironing some of her laces, silk neckerchiefs, and handkerchiefs, &amp;c., and we give elsewhereja useful article on the subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>I LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>I LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, Alpacas are more popular this summer than they have been for many a long day, and for travelling and hard knockabou'.; wear they are really invaluable, for not only are they light and cool, but their smooth surface repels dirt and dust. Grey alpaca, trimmed with rows of black satin ribbon, and made with a long basque bodice, with satin belt and revers, has a very nice appearance; but black alpaca, with the skirt simply trimmed with a couple of cross-way tucks and a loose coat, is still more serviceable, and can be worn with a variety of blouses, those of check or Paisley patterned silk looking particularly well. The cross-over blouse, by the way, is going rapidly out of fashion, and simple, full-fronted ones with broad satin or corded ribbon belts with fanciful buckles, are now considered decidedly better style.</p>
        <p>Fashion favours large hat shapes in millinery at present. The newest ones are mado of a course straw, but lace and other transparent shapes are still worn, later on velvet and felt will come in t again. Floral trimmings are always one of the prettiest for hats, and these have been, and are still much worn. Coloured ribbons quilled up into small rosettes are treated in such a way that they become quite a copy of posies of flowers without foliage. These rosettes will be found very service- able, as they do not fade and spoil so quickly as flowers. Wide black satin ribbon is also much used to trim large hats. It is made up into very ample bows, through which a couple of stiff quills are placed. Sailor shapes in white and light tan Panama straw, with slightly turned up brims bound with ribbon, are also much worn, and are trimmed simply or elaborately according to taste. Some- times a box-pleated ruche of net or lisse is arranged round the crown, and a "mercury" wing set on either side, or the trimming consists of rosettes o chiffon and ospreys; but, in my opinion, a band of ribbon and a bow form by far the most suitable adornment for such a hat. White lace veils are favoured by many fashionable women for driving and country wear.</p>
        <p>Of course it is too early to talk of autumn fashions just yet, with the sun blazing upon us with all its summer ardour, but unquestionably walking dresses which shall not be mad, of the airy dehtine, alpaca, or soft silk, demand our im- mediate attention, for, as we well know, at the seaside, or, indeed, for the matter of that, in the country, the afternoons become chilly when August is a well established fact. A useful dress is made of a very thin blue serge with'a blouse bodice overhanging the belt of white, braided in black. The vest matches this, while the revers are of plain white. The sleeves are of the Garibaldi order, very loose and comfortable, and, indeed, the whole costume may be voted equally suitable for lounging, walking, or the more ener- getic exercises of golf or tennis.</p>
        <p>A fashionable toilette is made of fancy spot beige, trimmed with silk or shot or broche diagonal would be effective, as would also plain or shot bengaline, and for these either silk or velvet trimmings may be used, or contrasting material of the same kind. The gored skirt is trimmed at the foot with two silk bands with a strip of narrow gimp between, and two more bands arranged in the same manner are slipstitched 15 inches above it. The round bodice is fastened invisibly with hooks in the centre of the front, which is trimmed with silk epaulette revers tapering to a point at the bust. A high collar finishes the neck, and a narrow silk band neatens the waist. The gigot shape sleeves are trimmed at the wrist with two silk bands. The silk cape is composed of two frills one deeper than the other; both are gathered at the neck together with a lace frill which falls below them. It is fastened in front with hooks, and is finished with a Medici collar lined with lace.</p>
        <p>A brown holland coat and skirt, the former made with the fashionable " butterfly " collar is very pretty, and the cost of this costume is only 183. 9d., while its counterpart in serge can be pur- chased for a guinea. A stylish tippet of velvet and jet is easily made at home, and may be copied in moire silk, satin, or cloth. It consists of an almost circular-shaped frill, slightly pointed at back and front. The fulness is arranged in box-pleats, six pleats in each cape ; this brings a pleat on either shoulder, and gives the "set-up appearance, which is a necessity. Quantity of 24inch velvet required is two and three-quarter yards. This cape must be lined throughout with sarcenet, and interlined with stiff muslin.</p>
