      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Yours affectionately, JENNIE.</head>
        <p>Yours affectionately, JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Cheese Straws.</p>
        <p>Mix together two tablespoonfuls- of flour, a pinch of salt, a very little cayenne pepper, and three ounces of grated Parmesan cheese. Add the beaten yolk of an egg, and then enough iced water to make a very stiff paste. Roll the paste out on a board into a sheet an eighth of an inch thick. Cut the paste into strips an eighth of an inch wide and five inches long, and bake them about ten* minutes in a very hot oven. They should be a very light brown. If you have any scraps of paste left, roll them into a sheet, and cut it into small rings. Bake them, and slip little bundles of the straws through them.</p>
        <p>Mushroom Soup. Steep half a pourh, 01 urieu nalurootns in tepid water for three hours. When they are soft, wash them care- fully, and place them in a saucepan with carrots and leeks, cut into pieces. Add a bunch of parsley, thyme, and bay leaves, salt and pepper, and boil in sufficient stock, or, if maigre soup be preferred, in water, until the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>TALE uF A DOG.</head>
        <p>A TALE uF A DOG.</p>
        <p>A NEWTON POLICE CASE.</p>
        <p>Two colliers named James Holland, and John Twist, of Pocket Nook, were charged at Newton I'etty Sessions, on Saturday, with stealing a terrier dog, valued XI, the property of Thomas Dutton, Great Sankey. Prosecutor said that on the sth of the present month at about half-past eleven he saw the two defendants loitering about the lane near to his house. He suspected their intentions, so he said lie would watch them. He did so. Ile saw them walk along the road to where his terrier dog was frisking. One of the defendants deliberately picked the animal up and put it into a bag. Then they began to move on. Witness approached them and said that the dog was his, he thought they ought not to take it—at least not without his sanction, and he said he would have it,back. One of the colliers said he could have the dog back willingly, providing he would take it off them and then he would have to follow them to Pocket Nook, two or three miles away. "Very well" said Dutton, " I'll do it." And then as he told the magistrates be followed the defendants till they got to the canal bank, one of them then said " Dutton old man, if you coma any further after us we Shall put you in the canal and you will be able to bathe." To this Dutton said be replied "All right, I shall follow you if you duck me,"and then they moved on. He followed them to Pocket Nook and obtain( d their names and addresses from a youth. In the meantime they had pulled the dog out of the bag and allowed it to walk. Once at Pocket Nook it refused to follow the de,endants across the bridges. The wit- ness picked it up and after laying information with the police he went home. The defendants said they did not steal the dog. Twist said they were going along the load when the the terrier began to follow their dog. It cams with them fr a quarter of a mile and then he said to Twist : " Jim, this poor dogie brat, blessed if it isn't. Let's take him," and then they put him into a hag. When prosecutor told them subsequently that the dog was his they put it back on the floor and it followed them again. They had no intention of stealing the dog. The case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>Ibsen. who s now staying at Christiania, is naturally e. silent man. It is not easy te draw him into conversa- tion on general topics. The subjects which seem to attract him most are those dealing with his own prac- tical pecuniary interests, his business relations with his publishers, for instance, and the sums of money which he receives for his various works. YOUR ATTENTION. PLEASII _ _ _</p>
