      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>DARKWOOD.</head>
        <p>DARKWOOD.</p>
        <p>fi</p>
        <p>1(' °A TALIr,OF LONEMOOK.. BY Mn. HARRIET LKWIS. Author of "Her Double Life," "Old Life's Shadows," "Lady Kildare," etc. Cilarraft HER ANSWER STILL DEFERRED. , . . Upon returning'l6 'Beechrnont, Lord Chilton found that no tragic% whatever of Miss Winter had been discovered. While he had been south and north in his search for her, the agents of Miss Norreys and Lord Darkwood, working separately, had made thorough investigation nearer home, but with equally futile result. The missing girl had not been seen. at Shrewsbury. • The hotels were carefully searched, but no girl had come to them on foot about the time Gwen would have appeared there. The porters at the railway station were carefully interrogated; but they remembered no- thing that could throw light upon the case. ) No one so far as the most earnest inquiry could elicit, had seen Gwen after twelve o'clock upon the 'tight of the festivities in the castle ruins. 4 Miss SOrreys came privately to the conclusion that, after Gwen's interview with her lover, she had returned to her own room and brooded over the shame and mystery of her origin, and that she had thought herself not fit to be his wife, and had determined not to marry him, and not even to see him again, lest hehlMuld shake her resolve. And it this point Miss 'Norreys wavered between two final conclusions. g. Sometimes she thought that Gwen, weary of a"life that had been &amp;Mull of dis- appointment and oporrow, had committed suicide. Again, rememberins,the girl's strength of character —her grand, hermonature—she felt convinced that she never even dreamed of suicide, and that she had gone away to some remote spot, there to begin life anew, bearing her burden meekly and resohitely, however, deep her anguish, Lord Darkwood; too, entertained that theory of suicide. He had the lake in the park dragged and Dark. River also, MO, of equrse, the task was vaint He extended his search to every town, village, and hamlet withiioa Fradins of tvientir, iulles. He Caused advertisements to be inserted in the London tiewspapers, artfidry worded, of the nature to ittract Gwen's confidence and elicit a reply there- to.; and as his 'efforts continued, fruitless, he be- came more and more settled in the conviction that, in some morbid condition of mind, she had des- troyed herself. The marquis thought of Gweit by day and night. She was to him ari embodied peril. So long as she lived he could never know absolute peace and rest. He grew pale at the remembrance of the months she had spent under his roof; while he had been so un- conscious of her identity. Yet, while so absorbed in thoughts of her, he was scarcely less occupied with his matrimonial scheme. 'He was anxious for the arrival of tlae- aay upon which Miss Norreys had promised to give an answer to his 'suit. At (mei moment he became elated with the cenviction that she meant to accept him,' and again he became gloomy and full of evil forebodings. Upon the appointed day, he dressed himself with unusual care and drove to Beechniont. He was ushered into the drawing-room which was deserted. The sounds of girlish laughter came from the gar- dens below, the windows being open and the day springlike, and the notes of the piano floated softly, from the distant music-room.</p>
        <p>Miss Norreys entered presently in a sombre attire of heavy black silk, made with open corsage, and sleeves cut short at the, elbow and heavily tiimmed with lace. But the impression made by her dress was that of half-mourning, and the mar- quis's face gloomed over at the sight. This was not the dress in which a betrothed bride would desire to,meet her lover. She must mean to refuse him!</p>
        <p>The change in her face struck him peculiarly. Her olive complexion was far paler than usual; there were bistrd shadows under her velvety brown eyes, and there was a gloom in the eyes themselves brooding sorrow—a strange hopelessness—that seemed to him very singular. It occurred to' him that she must have lost a friend recently, and his manner became sympathising. "ion are looking ill, Miss Norreys," he ex- claimed. "I am very nearly ill," she answered, sinking wearily into afauteuil. "lam nervous and uneasy continually. But how prospers your search for Miss Myner, Lord-Darkwood Have you learned anything new !" "Nothing," he replied. "I am inclined to think that she is dead.</p>
        <p>Miss Norreys shuddered. _ "She is a very spirited girl, proud as a duchess," observed the marquis; "one of the sort to feel her social inferiority and that sort of thing. And she might have thought •herself unworthy of Lord Chilton and decided to end her troubles after a, rordantio fashion. I am persuaded that we shall never see her again." Miss Norreys was silent. "If she should return," continued the marquis, "1 aliould not be willing to place my giddy Georgina in her charge longer. This escapade will mar bliss Myner's success as a teacher. I have opened negotkitions with the proprietor of an excellent school for young ladies in a northern suburb of London, and shall send Georgina to school in the course of a few days." He.did not say that he was about to rid himself of his daughter that his home might be pleasanter to his expected wife; but this was in his thoughts, and Miss Norreys knew it. "Is the Lady Georgina aware of your intention?" "I told her to-day. There was a scene' of course; but Georgina finds that lam master. She is very anxim.J about Miss Myner," he added. "I didn't know that Georgina liked her half so well."</p>
        <p>Miss Norreys was again silent. The marquis had dreaded to approach the chief point of his visit, but now made a bold plunge, saying, abruptly— • "I suppose, Miss Norreys, that you know what day this is !" "Certainly—it is Tuesday." "And Tuesday—to-day—was the day upon which you promised to give me my answer to my proposal of marriage." said the marquis ardently. Sicily, is that answer to be what I desire 2 Will you be my wife?' • He bent towards her, eager, anxious, half hoping, half despairing. Miss Norreyr toyed with her fan. She was cer- tainly troubled, and ill at ease. Lord Darkwood's hopes fell; his face gloomed over again. "Am lan unwelcome suitor 1" he asked. "Do you mean to refuse me ?" "I don't know what answer to give you," said Miss Norreys, in a low, calm voice. "I am not prepared with ray answer to-day, Lord Darkwood, I tell you frankly that, upon many accounts, a marriage with y.eu would be pleasing to me." " Thanks thanks !" he cried, rapturously. "Then I am not obnoxious to you !" She shivered a little. Had he seen the expres- sion in her eyes he would have known himself obnoxious to her—as a cobra would have been And yet she was actually considering the pro- priety of accepting him as her husband! "Give me another week," she said slowly. "I have not been well during the past week, and have not been able to give the matter proper considera- tion. Another week my lord unless you are tired of the delay," she added, with an attempt at arch- ness, "and choose to withdraw your proposaL" "I would rather grant you a year for considera- tion than have you decide against me," he responded. So long as you do not refuse me out- right, I shall hope." "You may hope," she replied, with an effort. "And I think that I can give you your answer a week from to-day." He thanked her warmly, and kissed her hand. He was too wise to urge the matter further at that time. She had certainly given him great encourage- ment; she had bidden him hope. It was very clear that she 'was endeavouring coquettishly to enhance her value in his sight, and that she meant ultimately to accept him. The murmur, of voices sounded nearer, and Miss Milly Kenright and Miss Ensor strolled into the drawing-room, followed by Sir William Ensor. The conversation between Miss Norreys and Lord Darkwood was broken up, and he presently took his leave in excellent spirits, quite hopeful in regard to the success of his matrimonial scheme. When he had departed, Miss Norreys returned .bo her boudoir. She had scarcely seated herself in this private apartment when a servant entered, announcing the return of Mr. Barsby—her bailiff, whom she had sent to Sicily and Corsica upon a secret mission, and whose startling telegram to her we have given. "Show him up," she commanded. The servant disappeared. "Mr. Barsby's clear brain may help to solve those riddles that so oppress me, she thought. "I am glad he has come. And now to hear all (hat ltelaa.t&amp; BAY filbollt I,9_rd PArkWqocl-"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>k.t.vogir--) 4ii , ! -,N4‘),, Iv ~ , 1893. ,' ! –</head>
        <p>k.t.vogir--) 4ii , ! -,N4‘),, Iv ~ , 1893. ,' ! -</p>
        <p>The bailiff was ushered into her presence.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER LXIL • A PLAN RESOLVED UPON.</p>
        <p>The report of Miss Norreys' bailiff coincided with that which Lord Darkwood had given of himself to Ms successor. in the library Upon that fatal night of his lordship's return to Dunholm Castle. Mr. 13arsily had explored the vicinity of the wreck of the Sylphicle. He had discovered the fishermen who had rescued the marquis, and had . heard from them how they had found his lordship floating at sea upon the morning after the storm and wreck, buoyed up by a life preserver. The bailiff had visited the humble home to which Lord Darkwood had been taken, and in which he had</p>
        <p>been nursed through a long illness back to health and strength. There was no possible room for doubt. Mr. Barsby declared, that the rescued man was Edward Charteris, Lord Darkwood. "I traced him to Marseilles under his rightful name and title," said the bailiff. "At Marseilles he'probably came upon the obituary notices of him- self in the English newspapers, _for, after leaving that town, he called himself Mr. Chart, and so registered himself at the quiet hotel in Paris where he'. stopped one night. There can be no possible doubt that this Mr. Chart, was Edward Charteris, Lora Darkwood. The personal description is the same. He ,came on to England, and must 'have stOrted for Shrewsbury without stoppinii, in Lan- don." "What do you think of that l" asked Miss • Norreys. pursued my investigations all the way home, madam," said the bailiff. "At Shrewsbury Station, where Lord Darkwood was thoroughly well known, aetprniped, to. question the porters. One of them anticipated-me. The crowd had departed when I went bank,' and this potter who knew me very well, drew me'aside, saying that be had something on his mind that had troubled him for months. He told me, aitei- some deprecating preamble, that he believed that he had seen a ghost; that one dark, unpleasant night in November last, a man, muffled _closely, was let out of a first-class compartment of one of the carriages, and, as this man passed under a light, he—the porter—had recognised him, and helievediliim to. be Lord Darkwood, who had been reported dead. His lordship—if it were he—seemed 'to, wish to escape recognition, • and quitted the station on foot. The porter was positive for a day or' two that he ' had seen Lord Darkwbod ; but hearing nothing to confirm this, be gradually per- suaded himself that be bail been deceived by a chance resemb/a.nce. Of late, however, his doublts had returned to him, and he bad begun to.entertain the fancy that he had seen a ghost. ,"If his lordship were muffled, how could he . .</p>
        <p>recognise him?" "The porter had known him well. Lord Dark- wood was very tall and, commanding, with a peculiarly stately carriage. The word kingly describes him thproughly. • He err ried his head in )r. haughty faihion, which I never saw in another 'mem He was fair, with ,bloncle hair and beard. If you had ever seen him once Miss Norreys, you would have known him years afterwards,' had yen met him in the farthest corner of the globe. The porter judged by his entire appearance rather than by his face. The rocognic.ion would not satisfy a court of justice; but, taken in connection with what I learned in Corsica, and the fact that Lord Darkwood was alive at that time, and on his way home, it quite satisfies me." "And me also," said the lady. "It is clearly woven. then. that Lord Darkwood arrived at Nix months ago. What became of him? Why did he never appear at Dunholm Castle? Where is he ? " The bailiff looked staggered at these questions. "He must have walked home from Shrewsbury," said the heiress. "He arrived at the station by the night train. It would have been near midnight when he arrived at the castle. No servant gave him admittance, for the castle servants were devoted to him and would have raised a glad outcry of welcome. The question narrows itself to one or two convictions. Either some harm happened to him between Shrewsbury and the Castle, or he eneountered the present Lord Darkwood on his arrival home." "So much is clear." "I think no harm happenectto him on the way," said Miss Norreys, "because, if he had been assaulted, or robbed his body would have been found. His total disappearance is de to the present Lord Darkwood.' " You think tnat the present marquis killed him?" asked Mr. Barsby, lowering his voice to a whisper, and looking around him, as if fearful that the very walls might hear him. "I think him capable of such a crime," said Miss Norreys, "under the circumstances. He was Captain Tollish, encumber.id with debts, poor, harassed, and on the point of selling out of ,the army to satisfy his creditors. He became Lord Darkwood, a noble marquis, with a princely revenue, master of Dunholm Castle, with a train of dependents and honours were heaped upon him. Could he go back to the slough from which he had escaped? Could he return to the old harassing life, and leave these new splendours, which must have become so dear to his soul? Ido not believe that he could, if any way of escape presented itself. He has not borne an excellent reputation cif late years, notwithstanding the fact that he has re. mained in the army. He is capable in my opinion, of killing a man who stood in his way, as the returned marquis must have done." "You think his cousin dead, then ?" Miss Norreys hesitated. She looked steadily at the honest face of her bailiff. She motioned him to draw nearer, and said in a low tone— "Mr. Barsby, I have waited your return to take action in this matter. I have not dared to confide, my suspicions to any one. Upon the day I sent you on your mission, Lord Darkwood and his daughter were here. The Lady Georgina spoke of the castle being haunted, and said that she Irad heard the ghost cry out in the dungeons. Ido not know why her foolish words should sink so deep in my heart. Perhaps it was because of the look her father gave me. At any rate, I sent you abroad, and I was full of wonder at my own folly in doing so.</p>
        <p>"There was a providence in it." "Last Wednesday— a week to-morrow—my guests and I visited the castle ruins. The marquis refused to open the dungeons ; hut he was prevailed Upon to do so. My suspicions were keen and ter. rible. I separated myself from the remainder oi the party, and ran through the passage shrieking at the top of my voice. Mr. Barsby, there was an answering ory ! ' "An echo, perhaps ?'' "Lord Darkwood Called it an owl ! It was no echo, no screech owl, no ghost. It was the shriek of a man for help—of a Mull in utter despair !" "Great heavens! You are sure ?"</p>
        <p>"I believe the cry was the utte-ra. nee of a man, but I am not ,o sure that I would dare procure a search warrant and constables and search the castle dungeons. My belief may be unfounded, you know. The marquis inig,it have been killed be- tween Shrewsbury and .11nnholm, although that is not probable, or he may be now dead and buried, having been killed by the present lord. Another horrible possibility has also kept Inc silent, if the cousin of Captain Tollish lives, and if Captain Tol- lish were to get wind of my suspicions even, he would put his cousin out of the way. You see, tberefore, that 1 have not dared to do anything."</p>
        <p>"We have grounds for believing the worst,' said Mr. Barsby ; "but we cannot openly accuse the present marquis of a frightful crime without more conclusive proofs of his guilt. So far, we nave no pi oofs that Lord Darkwood ever reached Danholin Castle. If he be yet alive, and we get out a search-warrant to discover him in the castle, the present marquis will be informed of our move- ments in time to rid himself of his rival. Toe task you have undertaken is full of difficulties, madam.'</p>
        <p>_ " I know it; but we can overcome them all. We dare not work openly. Let us work secretly. Let us search the castle dungeons at night---" "And lay ourselves open to be shot for burglars, or arrested for burglarious entrance into a private residence !"</p>
        <p>" I have alluded to the report, which I have heard many times since I came here, that the ser- vs.nts and retainers of the former lord adored him. Are there none among the household we could trust to aid us ?"</p>
        <p>" The butler could be trusted. Mr. Sutton, the Dark wood lawyer, came down upon the same train with me and is now at the castle. Of course, I said nothing to him of my recent discovery, but we travelled together, and he talked a great deal of the dead marquis, lamenting his early death." "I will send for Mr. Sutton. He is a lawyer, and will advise us wisely. He can inform the butler, if he thinks best. Tell no one where you have been, Mr. Barsby. Be very cautious still. You can now go home and refresh yourself after your journey, but come again about an hour hence. My doubts and suspibions shall all be satisfied to- night I" She dismissed the bailiff, and wrote an urgent message to Mr. Sutton. requesting him to come ts</p>
        <p>Beechinont immediately, on business of the utmosi importance. She begged him to inform no one of her letter, or of his proposed visit to her, should he comply with her request. She dispatched a mounted groom with the latter, and feverishly awaited its result. Mr. Sutton had arrivcd at Dunhclm Castle in response to Lord Dark wood's summons. Prepara- tions had been completed for the entrance of the Lady Georgina into an excellent school, and the marquis designed sending his daughter thither the next day in the charge of the old family lawyer, his lordship being unable to leave home in the present state of his affairs. _</p>
        <p>The marquis had returned from his wooing to find Mr. Sutton at the castle. In the exuberance of his delight, he informed the old lawyer that he was betrothed to Miss Norreys, of Beechmont, and received Mr. Sutton s congratulations. The lawyer made some inquiry into the disappearance of Miss Myner, and was informed that she had probably committed suicide in consequence of some vinfortn= nate love affair. Lord Darkwood then retired to his rooms to make some more change in hisloilet for dinner—it will be remembered that he had, be. come as fond of dress as any fine lady since his acceslion-to fortune—and Mr. Sutton went out of doors to smoke's cigar and meditate. '</p>
        <p>He was so engaged when the Beechmont groom rode up. The groom. being a Dunholin man, knew the Darkwood family lawyer ; and dismounted and approached him, giving the missive into his own 'hands.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>lawyer read the letter itiastonishment. _ _</head>
        <p>The lawyer read the letter itiastonishment. _ _</p>
        <p>What Can Miss .Norr..ys .want of tn-e 3" he thought. Has, she heard of the marquis's former irregularities, and does she desire to question me in regard 'tb them? Does 'he tush twspeak of her marriage settlements? Ah, it is probably about this!missing girl, she desires to egnsult me ! Miss Mytiet visited at her lichise, they say. I can't assist ita the search, but I will obey M 1613 Norrey's summons. I would like to see the future Marchio- ness of Darkwood."</p>
        <p>ife.fiqng away his cigar, crushed the letter into his pocket, and went to the stables. A horse was saddled for him, and lie • rode away without men- tioning his destination. • • Tbe groom had gone on in advence:. Mr. Sutton rode briskly, and, fifteen minutes later, dismounted at the great porch of Beech mount, giving his horse into the hands of a. waiting gfooni, and ascended the • steps. The door flew open •at• his •approach. Sounds of Music and langhter came from the draw-:: 14-room and music-roidn, which were upon the floor et the entramie. A servant met the visitor at the threshold and conducted him up the great stair. case, and ushered him int? Miss Norrey's boudoir. Mr. Sutton hail barely time to notice that the room was a very gem in its exquisite tastefulness and luxury when the lady of. Beechmont, in her long black robe .and magnificent beauty, entered kis presence. He stood up, bowing low before her, and think- ing that in all his life he had not seen en a woman as this. He was fairly dazzled by her superb and brilliant lovelinesss and his honest old face showed his admiration.'</p>
        <p>"Mr. Sutton," said the heiress, bending her stately head, "I am Miss Norreys. I must apolo- gise for having sent for you so .unceremoniously, but I know that you will pardon me when you hear what I have to say." "No apology is needed, madam," replied the old lawyer, gallantly. • "Lord Darkwood has just informed me that you have honoured him with your affection, and that you are soon to become Mar- chioness of Darkwood. lam grateful to you for Anticipating the time when my service will yight-, filly belong to you,.mAdam." "Lord Darkwood is premature in his announce- ment," said • the heiress, haughtily. "I am hot betrothed to him. I have promised him an answer to his proposal a week from to day. I may give it sooner—and it may be a negative I" Mr. Sutton'looked surprised. " It occurred to me, madam," he said, "that you might have heard reports derogatory to his lord- ship's .character before his accession to his present dignity, and that you might desire to ask me questions ik regard to them. I am Lord Dark- wood's family lawyer. The honour of his name is only less precious to me than that of my own. I cannot, therefore, arlwer any questions the answers to which might tend to make a rupture between you and his lordship." _ . _ _</p>
        <p>"You misapprehend me. I have not summoned you here to ask you to give me a history of Captain l'ollish's life. You are the Darkwood family lawyer —therefore you are shrewd, discreet, and able. Sib down, Mr. Sutton. I have something of the utmost importance to say to you." The lawyer obeyed. The lady took a chair quite near to hise,and her manner was very grave, as she said—</p>
        <p>"I do not apologise for my interference in the Darkwood, family affairs, Mr. Sutton; neither can I explain to you the causes of my action. I have formed certain suspicions. If they should be verified, I should leave Beechmont, possibly for ever. But enough of myself. Were you attached to the marquis—he who perished in the wreck of the yacht Sylphide in the Mediterranean Sea last year ?" Mr. Sutton was startled at the abrupt change of subject and at the question. "Attached to him, madam 7" he exclaimed. "His death was one of the greatest sorrows of my life. Every one who knew him, loved him. He was noble, brave, with a great truthful nature--" Miss Norreye's lip curled. "Spare me your eulogy of him," she said; "what I have done has been with no regard for him—yet he is less base than his cousin. Mr. Sutton, I did not speak correctly when I spoke of the late mar- quis as having perished. I should have said that he was supposed to have perished." "He did perish, madam. Captain Foster—" "Was mistaken. I had my suspicions aroused in regard to his loss, and I sent Mr. Barsby, my bailiff, to Corsica and Sicily to inquire if his body had been found. Mr. Barsby returned to-day. Mr. Sutton, prepare yourself for a great secret. Lord Darkwood was not drowned !" "Not drowned !"</p>
        <p>"No. He wore a life-preserver, and floated on the water until morning. Then he was picked up by a fishing vessel. The fishermen carried him home. He lay ill for weeks, was nursed back to health, and he started for England!" " Impossible !" "it is true. He was seen at Shrewsbury railway station. He set out to walk to the castle. It was one dark night of last November. After he left the station he was not seen again.' "Miss Norreys, you have been deceived. This is impossible 1" cried the lawyer, in a great agita- tion.</p>
        <p>"It is true 1" again asserted the lady steadily. "I believe, Mr. Sutton, that he arrived at the castle about midnight—that the servants had retired—that Captain Tollish heard his knock and went to the door. And I believe that Edward Charteris, Lord Darkwood, was either then and there murdered by his cousin, or that he was taken down into the dungeons beneath the ruins, and that he is now at this moment a prisoner in those dungeons !" The lawyer leaped to his feet. . " Madam," he cried, hoarsely, "do you realise that you are accusing the present lord of a revolting and awful crime ?"</p>
        <p>"I do T And I declare him guilty !" "Merciful heavens !" ejaculated Mr. Sutton. "It cannot be ! You must not dare to make such accusations against the marquis—" "Then you decline to test the truth of my sus- picions—even when Mr. Barsby declares to you that Lord Darkwood returned to England and arrived safely at Shrewsbury station ?" A cold sweat broke out upon the lawyer's fore- head. The charge againstTord Darkwood seemed absolutely monstrous. But Miss Norrcys seemed convinced of the marquis's guilt. The proofs were strong. What Was to be (lone? "If I prefer this terrible charge of murder against Lord Darkwood," he exclaimed, "and if he be not guilty, what will be my position? lam his trusted adviser, his counsellor, his friend. The entire country would justly denounce me for treachery to my master.' "I do not wish you to prefer any charge against Captain Tollish. Let the matter be investigated secretly. If no trace is found of Lord Darkwood in the castle dungeons, we will keep silent. But for the sake of the master you loved, you must probe this matter." . . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Sutton acquiesced in this decision. He could not rest unless the matter were investigated. But he was horrified, stunned, and utterly bewil- dered.</p>
        <p>"I will assist in the search," said Miss Norreys, and Mr. Barsby will join us. Also Lord Chilton, whom we can trust. And you should inform the butler, and see that he obtains the keys of the dun- geons. They are kept in a cabinet of the library. We will make a secret expedition this very night to the castle ruins—we will search the dungeons. if we find nothing, we will be silent. If we find him, let him be restored to his rights. Mr. Sutton, I a= rely woo Ytitl .?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>"You may, madam," said the lawyer, sternly. "It shall be as you say. We will make a secret investigation of the ruins to-night. And heaven grant that your suspicions prove true, and our noble master restored to us. But I fear the worst —I fear the worst!" J 1 (To be continued.)</p>
        <p>T 'l7 •</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WATERWORKS COMPANY.</p>
        <p>TTIE LAST ANNUAL MEETING. An extraordinary annual meeting of the share- holders of the Runcorn, Weston anti tialton Water- works Co., was held lathe •Ruticorn Public Halton Monday afternoon., Mr. J. Brundrit, J.P., chairman of directors presided, and there were present Messrs. W. Whittaker, J. Simpson, R. Foulkes,and Wi Salkeld (Secretary).</p>
        <p>. , • REPORT AND BALANCE SHEET. The Directors in their report state that the revenue account shows a balance of £1,201 175. 4d. including £l9 7i brought from the previous half-year, out of which they recommend a -dividend of £5 per centum Per •annum, free from income tax' payable on and after the Ist day of October. This will absorb £750, leaving a balance of £451 17s. 4cl. to be carried forward. The capital account stands at £53,400 16s. id., Made up of 3.000 shares at £lO each. £30,000 in preference stock, £l,OOO depreciation fund, £2,408 debenture stock, and 163. id. balance. The gross expenditure to June 30th last was £53,238 175.,- and new boiler account £l6l 19s. id., total, £53,400 163. id. The revenue account shows a balance from last half-year of £l9 7s. ' • water rents and fittings received March 31st, £1,150 7s. 7d. .• ditto June 30th, £1,258 10s. 3d. ; owing for water sold by meter, for fittings and meter rents, and for water rates due on June SOth, £BlO 103.•11d. ; old materials • flolel; £2 93. sd. ; transfers- of stock fees and weighing for tarmerec-E1 Bs. 3d. ; coal in stock, £2O ; t0ta1,*;3,262 13s. 104. The credit side shows amount owing for water rents and fitting, taken into account last half- year, £650 163. 7d. ; interest on debenture and prefer- ence stock. £469 18s. 6d. ; salaries and wages, £363 4s. id; coal, £l7B ss. ld. ; rent' and wayleave, £ll. 14s. 2d.; oil, tallow, packing, Stc., £3B as. 11d. ; station- ary and printing, £l4 83. 3d. ; rates and takes' £156 33. 9d. ; service pipes, fittings, taps, meters, and fitters' wages, £134 14s. 91. ' • repairs of machinery,£2o 135.6 d.; Sundries and stamps, £l4 19s. Bd. ; interest and com- mission, £7 Iss. 3d. ; balance, £1,201 175,. 4d. ; total, £3,262 13s. 10d. The general statement gives assets as balance due from capital account, 163. id.; cash in hand, £9 18s. 46. ; in Parr's Banking Company, £3l 12s. 7d. ; awing for water and fittings, £BlO 103. lid.; stock of goods and meters on hire, £284 17s. Bd. ; stock at pumping stations, £7O ; extra buckets, valves, pipes, 'add fittings, MO Os. ad.; coal, £2O; t0ta1,£.1,395 16s. 40. The liabilities are-Balance due to revenue account, £1,201 17s. 4d. ; owing for coal arid, to tradesmen. • £47 103.,7d. ; owing to Parr's Banking Company, £7 15s. 3d. ; suspense account, being dividends ordered to be retained, £ll7 103. ; cheques running, £ll 35. 26. ; total, £1,395 16s. 4d. CHAIRMAN'S SPEECH. The Chairman moved that the account be passed and ' . the report adopted, and that a dividend at the rate of five per cent, be paid. He did not know that he need. '4 say much especially as there were no shareholders pre- sent outside the directorate, or who were not in some ' way connected with the drawing up of the accounts. At the same time it might be of interest to state that ' this was the last annual meeting of the Company that would be held. Some of them had been connected with , the Company for many years, and had seen it pass through many phases of prosperity and otherwise, and - they were now approaching the end. The time had nearly arrived wimps the company must pass, into the bands of the Runcorn Improvenient Commissioners. It might seem to some that they were carrying forward a larger balance than usual, but there were certain ae- • counts not yet come in with regard to Parliamentary costs, and connected with repairs to boilers and engines, which the Company undertook to do before they closed the proceedings: They had returned this money for the purpose of defraying these expenses. 'lt would be necessary before the final conclusion ot the company that anOtber meeting should be called for several purposes. of which due notice would be given. This was the last annual meeting at which he would have the pleasure of addressing the shareholders-he might say the reporters particularly-and he hoped that the company when it did pass into the bands of the town would be such aS would meet with the approval of the whole Of the rate- payers. Although some of the ratepayers rather criti- cised the amount paid for the undertaking, yet he felt sure that when they came to see as they would in a short time, the advantages that would arise from the- purchase, they would be perfectly satisfied that the com- missioners had not by any means made a bad bargain. He thought the Commissioners were to be congratulated upon the very easy terms upon which they had bor- rowed the purchase money. That in itself it seemed to niin would ensure success, because they would have so much less to pay as interest which would go a long way towards defraying any extra expense they, might be put to in connecting with the Vyrnwy scheme, which. he believed the Commissioners proposed to do. He could only say in conclusion that he should be sorry for many things to have to give up the counpany because be had been a director from the commencement in 1865, and he like many other shareholders would feel like a fish out of water, when there was• no hale yearly meet- ings ot the Runcorn Water Works Co. to attend. Mr, W. Whittaker seconded the motion which was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENTS AND THANKS. Mr. Simpson moved that the retiring directors Messrs. Brundrit and Whittaker bore-elected. Mr. Brundrit, he said was one of the two original promoters and directors of the Company, who were still left, and if there were no other reasons why they should not make any charge in the directorate, he should not like the idea of Mr. Brun- drit not being in at the death. (Latlghter.) They knew what kind of a business man Mr. Brundrit was, and he bad made a capital chairman ever since he was appointed.—lf they had searched Runcorn through they could not have found a better. Mr. Whittaker had not neen quite so long a time a director as Mr. Brundrit,buG he haw attended most attentively and diligently to bin duties.</p>
        <p>Nlr. R. Foulkes seconded the motion which was carried.</p>
        <p>In reply the Chairman thanked Mr. Simpson for the kind manner in which he had spoken about Mr. White taker and himself. The Company had been not only sourse of pleasure in meeting gentlemen in the transac- tion of business, but it had also on many occasions been a source of anxiety. But they had steered safely through many shoals until once more they had got into deep water, and he hoped when they put the vessel alongside the stage and handed her over for somebodY else to do the work, she would be found in good condi- tion and that they might always look back with pleasure to the time when they transacted business together as a company. He further hoped that no town would benefit by the purchase and the ratepayers be satisfied that they had made a good bargain. (Hear, hear.) The Chairmart then proposea the re-appointment of Mr. Robert Foulkes as one of the auditors of the Coal- pans. Mr. Whittaker seconded. Carried. Mr. Foulkes moved a vote of thanks to the directortir and the motion was seconded by Mr. Salkeld and carried. The proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>RACE OF THIEVES.</head>
        <p>A RACE OF THIEVES.</p>
        <p>• An experiment which might to have a special interest for Mr. Galion and others who have faith in the doctrine of heredity is now in practical operation at Fatehgarb- It consists in the endeavour to train up in honest habits the children of the Sansia race, who have been knoWn, in India as thieves and depredators for ages past, anu who are said to regard felony as a matter entirely apart from any suggestion of sin or wrong-doing, so long as it 18 thoroughly carried out. The real sinner in their eyes is the bungler, the lad who • fails ither from lack of courage or lack of skill. The boys are set to agricei" tural and gardening work, and some of them to tbe making of shoes; the girls learn weaving and spinning,t knitting and sewing, and the elements of cookery. Both boys and girls appear, it is said, to like the life, and t° do their allotted work with cheerfulness and with eS much energy as can reasonably be expected in the Tropic of Cancer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PROSPERITY IN IRELAND.</head>
        <p>PROSPERITY IN IRELAND.</p>
        <p>The statistics of Irish:banking and railways this Y":„ go to show that the country has been prospering. note circulation at the end of dune last was £123,000, or about 2 per uent, more than a year ago, which is a SIO of large transactions. The railway tragic returns for the June half-year,sh.w an increase of nearly 4 per cent.upon the corresponding figures of last year. It was, perhaPS; natural, pending actual experience of Home Bute. tha„ deposits in Irish Joint-6tock Banks should be dra°°— upon, although as a matter of fact there is a slight In: crease in these deposits compared with June of last also an increase of £86,000 in Post Office Savings in Ireland compared with last year, Trustees' Savloge Banks, on the other hand, showing a decrease ot £102,000. The country appears to be making Tea progre 83.</p>
