        <head>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</head>
        <p>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Douglas ITyde, whose "Love songs of Con- -uialcht " have been received by the Press with such stinted Praico, is the son of a Connaught rector, ato has lived most of his days under his father's ;Irf at French Park, Roscommon. He is tall,i ~,,I•litehwY, and dark as a Spaniard. He speaks English' the accent of one not to the manner born, and 43'ech is full of the Irish turns and twists, as tVIIRII, distinguished University man as he die, be. 5,,1.,h1ts in the Irish. His expressions in prose are Zueed strange and beautiful. He has verily trans-1 "4144 the Irish into the English tongue.</p>
        <p>Re • -- 4m OIS a many-sided person, being a mighty bunter ccasionally a trafficker in beasts a-, the West- It"' fairs, and on the other side of him a scholar, a /28,tic, a poet, a patriot, an enthusiast. He is an of Trinity College, Dublin, and a double gold iedallist. He is to be married in October to Miss r:l,rtz, of Liverpool, and after a tour abroad will /rill% to French Park to go on with his studies of facT; Poetry, and folk-lore. He is a line Irish polar, and is [wrhaps more in touch with the h "131 e nearer to the earth traditions, than any we Itve ever had.</p>
        <p>Le GaMenne lives at Hanwell, beyond the, ZreMe west- n suburbs of London: With his mild features, his large eyes, his long silky hair, 1,7 is extremely handsome. A lady once said that looked like a nun ; but he rather like a pensive 'very all-there angel in a Botticelli picture. He set", rY Popular. Even one who at first sight has a onlbey resentment of this somewhat feminine ItutiPtV, is drawn insensibly by the quietly cordial to`' convincing manner. Mr. Le Galienne has had, atota,lce some hard hits, fair and unfair, in his day nas returned them without rancour. He is a litmus and appreciative critic.</p>
        <p>aas nearest poetical neighbour is Austin Dobson aling• Mr. Dobson's house is in Eaton Rise, a inytotonous avenue of houses that climbs the hill, iiiBOle Harrow direction from. the railway station. thh.°use is very much like its neighbour and has pc;,, InR to tell that there those beautiful studies in ka',lloll literature, or English of Popeian era, are Ike". Mr. Dobson, alas writes verse no more,and Cal„Illis8 his poems, which had the lightness of 'erly, with a heart and spirit infinitely finer.</p>
        <p>t2l,jlrBll,°n Calthorp's personal estate amounts to ~_ het) The rumoured bequest to Captain Mae. 3,ea ",a8 not been made. One of the features of the neBrs.S been the large fortunes left by peers. Wit- i the Casee of the Dukes of Bedford and Bather- Earl ot Derby, the Lord Stratheden and</p>
        <p>liar° lef„ n rfrench, who is one of the peers who will i the }tome Rule Bill in the House of Lords, 4 nert al• nf nIY qualified to speak of the requirements too reland with a good deal more authority than his class. He has the exceptional qualifiC- Wk..' °I having spent his life in the country. He 8 called to the Irish Bar more than fifty years 11p().11t14 was a resident magistrate for Co. Tipperary r 'nu years ago. Lord Ffrench is now in his 8t Year. towe. _</p>
        <p>where the Comte de Paris is very quietly 1341 rating his fifty4lftli birthday, is a magnificent ttleite, which was at once the glory and the ruin of Lextinct Dukes orßuckingbam. Here the father Q"e late Duke received the Queen end Prince die 4 shortly after they married, and here he 4tPed seriously into his.excbequer by the splendid IvheB which he gave in trmor of Louis Philippe irnr„en tile latter visited Stowe. The house is PiCeensP. It consists of a huge central pile with a usted Corinthian portico and steps, and two wings Cenre same style, which are -connected with the The re bY long galleries built at a lower elevation. tree,reartile hall is a lofty domed circular apartment ver; tWhich corridors strike out to the sides. A dee'', "Ile apartment is the dining-room, which is Psh-ri,ted in the style of Louis Quinze, with a liot,"f'd and painted roof. The approach to this Ike i 8 very striking, lying through an undulating tli "x'llich is well-stocked with red deer and 104 and cattle. The house belongs to Lady Kin- hie"us daughter of the late Duke, and the Comte lest,"lY a Abort lease, being unwilling to take a Asia t?ne• Last year, after many years, the (peen "sr second visit to Stowe.</p>
        <p>_ The • %a bottom has been completely knocked out ,or thu,,,tnaeh-vaunted Welsh petitions against the the ,`"1 Suspensory Bill by the evidence laid before keth`2ternittee on petitions as to the crooked 5"44 resorted to in order to obtain signatures. ot tlBl4 been shown, for instance, that the hawkers e Petition, in submitting it to Welsh people tentof English, have induced them to sign by ettlest 'Nu gross misrepresentations of its purport, etrn to the extent of describing its prayer as con- quite other things than the Established 104' Signed declarations to this effect were tete "Ming in numbers sufficient to convince the , prinnlittee that, extending over a large area of the VieteiPantv, the whole business constituted a corn- Use of the right of petition.</p>
        <p>uni„ ~tes °nee was the House respectfully filled on iliti.ttlaY, and that was for the half hour that John „ Qoaclie„°oonpied in a comprehensive criticism of the kid ',l"c'ti of labour in the Government arsenals 1• ."o.kVards. John was exceedingly vigorous tiCe"ing in his appeal to the Government to offi- th4dopt the eight hours day, and he prophesied th'` theY did so they would reap a hundredfold ki4 e next General Bleed= in the shape .of a solid sent4uBiastic labour vote all over the three s, Joh, .. • Cy - mcidentally amused the House with a little . lad eh at the expense of the front bench men,who eed %lye were amongst the loudest laughers them- St the member for Battersea's racy interlude 411,pepri„.13tive of the mantle of omniscience that is aeoilr'"d to miraculously descend on the favoured tl -era of a member on his promotion ton seat a co le front bench. Mr. Asquith and Mr. Acland. tailLll34; of recent recuits to the front bench, sicaul- Pletrr 1Y blushed and smiled as John Burns de- the transformed new Minister vaingloriously tlit'laing that "the hub of the universe passed %Rh his front doorstep." Ife SUlliVan is not the only verse-maker in rd":l2 /.1 Parliamentary party. On Tuesday Mr. tbseip,",had occasion to purchase a marble slab for nis Place of business at Enniskillen. and the , rrenc „ gave rise to a good deal of chaff, some of kateteaffnes insisting that the real object of the • hzl for South Meath was to ensure .or himself oh,tickfereM. e tombstone. Mr. Pinkerton was po itat:l9lth the idea that he dashed off an ." Silent at last lies our friend Jerry. He 'was a man both wise and merry; And e'er the sod he lay beneath He represented Clare and Meath. NVis know not what he is or where, wot's net of the state of Clare.' He leaves no child to bear his name, alas ! to him 'tis all the same. No wife—he never could afford 'un— A long farewell to Jerry Jordan." • rally Hewitt, who has just passed away. one time the leading authority on female , plainth in this country, and had a very large 4, 13, _lucrative practice in the best circles, while his 44k:eases of Women " was a text-book in the pro- • °P• Of late years, however, he had been in qh4 'Riling health, and had to cartail his work, leo Younger men have come to ,the front. He 68 a widow, who is the sister of Col. Hollis, of</p>
        <p>Nortt.ampton ; a son who has been called to the Bat but has developed a literary bent and has published a novel which has been favourably received; and three daughters, of whom the eldest ie the wife of Mr. Whttehorn, Q.C., and the youngest is,the wife of Dr. Tuke.</p>
        <p>-- Lord Rothschild, whom Mr. Gladstone has just been visiting, sets an example to less fortunate financiers in business regularity. He Most frequent= ly travels to town by the train leaving Tring at 8.43 in the morning, is at Easton at 9.35, and thuS reaches his desk comfortably by ten. He is there- fore breakfasting 'quite as early as the majority of his clerks. On Tring railway station he is, of course, very well known. But at .Euston his identity is less obvious, and he may sometimes be observed seeking anxiously for a cab, with his little black bag in his hand, which, outwardly at least, does not serve to impress Jehu with a sense of the importance of the fare—until he receives the direction where to go.</p>
        <p>Fully one-third of the entire number of Wesleyan ministers, with their families, are this week leav- ing old for new spheres of labour. The exact num- ber of changes this year is 637, which' is rather more than the average of the past few years. With next Sunday the Methodist year commences, and in about a fortnight all the District Synods will , assemble for the autumnal session, when special business relegated by the recent Wesleyan Confer- ence will be considered. As regards London Metho- dism, the outlook for the opening year is said to he exceptionally encouraging,</p>
        <p>The circumstances under which Professor Crouch, .the composer of "Kathleen Mavourneen." came to settle in America are not perhaps generally known. It was as far back as 1859 that Crouch left England for the States with the avowed intention of gather- ing the material for a great work on "Life in the West." A little later the war broke out, when Crouch immediately enlisted as a private on the Confederate side, and took an active part in the campaign, fighting in all the various engagements of the Northern Virginia army under General Lee till the historic end of the struggle, the surrender at Appomattox Courthouse. The close of the war found Crouch in such reduced circumstances that he was forced to accept the position of gardener in Richmond; at 75 he returned to Baltimore, where he has since fought a bard struggle to make both ends meet. He has given music lessons and sold autograph copies of his songs, and has even polished pieces of furniture which relic-hunters have been willing to pay for as momentoes of the composer of "Kathleen.</p>
        <p>BRASS BAND CONTEST 1N BANK PARK.</p>
        <p>In order to raise funds for the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary, a brass band contest. promoted by the Workingmen's Committee, was held on Saturday afternoon in Bank Park Recrea- tion Ground, the use of which had been granted by the Town Council, for the purpose. Forty pounds had been offered in cash prizes by the Committee. Seventeen bands, some of which were prize brass hands had entered the competition and a capital contest was anticipated. A shower of rain fell in the morning, but the afternoon was fine, although the air was rather chilly. The attendance at the time appointed for the commencement was disapppinting to the promoters, but by three o'clock the spectators had considerably increased in number. The attendance was, however, far from being as good as the object deserved, and the ven- ture was not a success financially. The Recreation Ground is well adapted for the purpose. A few reserved seats had been enclosed near the band stand, but they were not very well patronized. Close by was a small tent of conical shape.in- which the judge of the contest,Mr.J. 0. Shepherd, of Liverpool, was comfortably ensconced. He was enclosed in the tent so that he was unable to see the bands and also unable to know which band was playing. He was zealously guarded by the representa- tives of " law and order " and the spectators were kept at a distance by means of ropes. A refreshment tent had been erected on the ground by the British Workman Publichouse Co., where refreshments were obtainable at reasonable prices. The test piece to be played was " Marino Fabero," by Donizetti, a piece bristling with technicalities which require careful treatment on the part of players. The result of the draw for order of " going in "was as follows, the names of the band and the con- ductors being given :-1, Cliviger, Mr. T. German ; 2, Widnes Subscription, Mr. W. Riminer ; 3. Ashton (North), Mr. G. F. Birkenshaw ; 4, Eccles (Borough), Mr. W. H. Ellwood ;5, Shelmersdale (Temperance), Mr. W. Rimmer ; 6, Leigh (Firs-lane), Mr. W. Wood ; 7, Gor- ton (M.S. and L. E 1,.), Mr. T. German ; 8, Mossley, Mr. Edwin Swift ; 9, Warrington Borough Band, Mr. R. Rimmer ; 10, Eagley, Mr. T. Swift; 11, Pendleton (Old) Mr. F. Durham :12, Manchester (Onward Temperance), Mr. C. Smith ; 13. Warrington League of the Cross, Mr. J: Mullins; 14. Warrington (Methodist Free Church), Mr. J. Richardson ; 15, Verdin Adelaide, Mr. J. Rider ; 16, Crewe, (Temperance), Mr. F. Durham. The only absentee band was the Stalybridge (Old). Immediately after the last band had finished playing, the judge- mounted the band stand to ,y,ive his decision. He said the piece had been very well played by the competing bands. ' His awards were as follows: let prize, No. 4 band, Eccles (Borough) •, 2nd, No. 8. Mossley • 9 3rd. No. 10, Eagley.; 4th, No. 11, Pendleton (Old) ; sth,No. 7, Gorton (M.S. and L. Ry,) ; 6tb, No. 5, Skelmersdale (Temper- ance).; 7th, No. 1, Clivi,ger. Mr. John Roberts then distributed the money prizes. He was extremly sorry to say that they had net been so successful that day as they expected to be. When they decided to have that band contest, they anticipated that they would not only clear all expenses but be able to hand over a balance to that noble institution, which he was very proud to say a large number of working-men took an interest in. Re- ferring to the Infirmary extensions he said there was a debt of about £1,600, and the annual expenses would he increased aboue£2oo. The workingmen's committee felt it to be their duty to make an effort to raise a sub- stantial sum towards this debt. A band contest was thought to be the best means of attaining it. They had now had a trial and done their best, although he be- lieved it had proved a financial failure, but he hoped for a more satisfactory result on more future occasions. The day's arrangements were carried out by the Work- ingmen's Infirmary Committee, of which Mr. John Roberts is chairman, Mr. John P. Taylor vice-chairmatt, Mr. Thomas Burton treasurer; and Messrs. Joseph Tay- lor and David Faushaw, hon. secretaries. _ _ _ . . . .</p>
        <p>_ The rcmarks of the judg,eiri the contest were received by Mr. David Fanshaw, one of the secretaries, on Mon- day. Mr. Shepherd in his reference to the Warrington Borough Band, of which Mr. R. Rimmer is conductor, says :—" Allo Maestoso : Coarse opening, but soon im- proved. Soprano poor from bar 16. Tone fair, attack, &amp;c., all right. End of movement very rough' cadenza fair. Larghetto:: Bad start, no bass on first beat of bar. Tone of cornet rather thin. Movement fairly well played. Alto Vivace: Opening rather rough; soprano and cornet not well in tuna together from bar seven. Molto Alto: Rough and rather wild; recit. all right. Andte Mod.: Euphonium fair tone, but does not measure triplers correctly, his tone fails as lie goes .n ; cadenza poor; movement fairly accompanied. Alto Mod.: car- net made a bad-start, and was unsafe throughout move- ment; sGprano also. , Trombone subject rough. Band tired out at Mosso, tone poor. Vivace: Rather better from here, a good finish, but rough. Warrington League of the Cross, mnductor, Mr. J. Mullins.—Allo Maestoso : Opening out of tune and basses make wrong•notes ; precision, &amp;c., fair; cadenza poor. Larghetto : Out of tune; cornet and soprano very :unsafe ; band better in tune from the forte, but not right. Allo Vivace Opening not precise, but hand better in tune now ; style and precision not good,' Motto Alto: Again out of tune; euphonium very quiet nt rocit. Andte Mod. : Band better in tune now ; euphonium improving, but his tone is not satisfactory, especially in cadenza. Alto Mod. : Style of cornet bad, not good play- ing; Pin Mosso rough and wild ; second horn bad at last two bars. Vivace: Coarse and wild, basses not neat ; , fair finish. Warrington Methodist Free Church, conductor, Mr. 3. Richardson.—Allo Maestoso : Very much out of tune, and very rough. From bar 16 very poor' no soprano going ; cornet cadenza poor. Larghetto : Cornet play- ing as though afraid ;„soprano just audible, trying to do something, but not right ; all out of tune and very uninteresting. AlloVivae,e Spirited, hut very coarse, and several wrong notes played, ton many to enumerate ; much out of tune. Motto Alio; Band still out of tune, euphonium similar to last one, in recit ; chords not pre- cise. Andte Mod : Poor playing, no life or expression, and all out of tune cadenza poor. Allo Mod : Baritone just audible, comet also, all very poor; soprano omits his important parts altogether in Pin Mosse ; baritone rather better at repeat. Vivace Dreadfully outof tune, cornets especially. A very poor performance. the worst vet, evidently a young band, handicapped by the piece. Don't be discouraged, stick together, and practice hard, and success will come ultimately. In announcing the above awards Mr. Shepherd said he did not want to judge the same piece any more by these bands, as the playing was so uniformly good. He had heard one gentleman make a remark to the effect that the judge could hear the name of the band which was playing. He wished to assure the audience that that was not true. Had he known the band which was playing it would have made no difference to him. lie got his tee whoever got the awards. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>PUBLIC NOTICB.—Not one lent If UNDREnS of people 01 both sexes with wrecked and debilitated coustitut.ons are yearly restored to sound and robust health again by Messrs. Wilkinson. 3 and 4, Fitzallan Square, Sheffield. Write to them for advicefree when suffering from weakness. loss of flesh. appetite, disturbed rest, pimples, blotches, indigei. tion, pallid skin. nervousness, and general debl Sty. Posi- tive cure for all Blood and Skin Diseases, Required or eon at tutional. Sixty years' experience. One address (as above) for mrer 30 years. The" A 1 Mixture," 4s. 9d. the case. The Nagle Mirror In envelope, four stamps. Sufferers &amp;sap polated with Electric Bait/could write tons at once.—Ado</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 2. 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 2. 1893.</p>
        <p>Bead, Grappenhall, from jobn Hewitt to John AAley was allowed.</p>
        <p>A STRANGE DILEMMA. The Birmingham City Council find themselves in a curious position owity, to the action of the licensing justices. The Bench have refused to renew the license of a public-hiuseof which the Corporation are the owner. The municipal body have given notice of appeal. They will have to employ a barrister to proceed with the appeal, but they will also be obliged to fee another counsel to oppose the appeal in the interests of the licens- ing justices, and however the case ends "The costs on both sides must come out of the city exchequer." The incident is grotesque enough for " The Mikado."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>I THE 'LICENSING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>I THE 'LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>NEWTON BREWSTER SERRONS. At tho annual licensing'sessions held ab the Town Hal Newton, on Saturday, the following report was sub- mitted by Superintendent Brassington for the Wigan division :—" I have the honour to lay before you the usual returns relative to the business in connection with the general annual licensing sessions. and to state that there are within the thernships of Golborne and Haydock which form the Wigan portion of Warrington Petty Sessional Division; 32 licensed persons, viz., 18 inn- keepers, one spirit grocer, and 18 beersellers (13 on and five off the premises), being the same as compared with last year. _There have been 176 persons procaeded against for drunkenness, and drunk and disorderly con- , duct, being an increase of 23 as compared with last year. ' The houses generally speaking have been well conducted and I have no objection to make to the renewal of any of thelicmses." Superintendent Higginbotham submitted the following report for the Newton division :—" I have the honour to lay before you my report and returns relative to the business in connection with the general annual licensing session. The number of licensed persona is the same as last year :—Licensed victuallers, 45 ; beersellers (on premises), 16 ; beersellers (off premises), 5. There are two applications for new licenses. One licensed victualler (Jane Hayes, Ship Inn, Newton) was sum- moned on the 13th August, 1892, for permitting drunk- enness on 15th July, 1892, and was fined 10s. and costs. Generally the licensed persons have conducted their houses satisfactorily, and there have been few com- plaints." AN APPLICATION WITHDRAWN.</p>
        <p>An application for a new off-beer license which had been entered by Mr. James Ramsden, Common-road, Newton Common, was withdrawn.</p>
        <p>REFUSAL OF AN OFF-LICENSE.</p>
        <p>Henry Goodler, Brook House Cottages, grocer, Great Sankey, applied for an off-necr license.—Mr. Segar, barrister, Liverpool, represented the applicant, and pointed out that Mr. Goodier desired the license for the convenience of the farmers and labourers in the district. The nearest publichouse was three-quarters of a mile away, and the other two miles away. There was a pop- ulation of 580.—Mr. El. Ireland, Brook House, testified to applicant'a respectability.—The application was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE BACKDOOR QUEtTION.-STATEMENT BY THE BENCH.</p>
        <p>The annual licensing session for the borough of St. Helens were held on Tuesday, the magistrates being the Mayor (Mr. Sinclair), Colonel W. W. Pilking- ton, Alderman Cook, Councillors J. Forster and 11. Martin, Messrs. Oppenheirn, Cotton, Dromgoole, Sharpies, and Dr. Gasket'. The Mayor said it would be convenient if he first an- nounced the order of business. The Bench proposed to ask the Chief Constable to read the black list, and a list which be has prepared by order of the magistrates as to backdoors in licensed houses. After those had been read the magistrates wonld retire to consider them, and then announce their further proceeding. The Chief Constable (Mr. James Wood) then sub- mitted his annual report, which stated that the popula- tion in 1891 was 71,288, and that there were 147 public- houses and 1.38 beerhouses. 'lhe population to each public-house was 484, and to each beerhouse 524. Dur- ing the past year 397 persons had been summoned for drunkenness, and 655 arrested for drunkenness, making a total of 1,052, against 1,268 in 1892, 1,354 in 1891,an0 1,216 in 1890. Five publicans had been proceeded against in the past year, against 12 in 1892, 8 in 1891, and 3in 1890. The number of publicans convicted was 3in 1693,7 in 1892, 4in 1891, and none in 1890. The number of beersetters proceeded against in 1893 was 3, against 7in 1892, 2in 1891. and 4in 1890. Of these two were convicted in 1893,4 in 1892, 2 in 1891, and 1 in 1890. There had been a decrease of two neerhouse licenses during the year, making a decrease of seven in the four years. The number of public abuses remained at 147. 'I here were eight grocers who had licenses for dealing in wines and spirits. The black list consisted of three publicans and two beersellers, viz., James Wool. fall, Prince of Waleli Hotel, convicted of permitting drunkenness, but the conviction was quashed at the Quarter Sessions ; William Peet, Union Hotel, for being drunk ; William Whittemore, Bird-in-Hand, for being drunk in charge of a horse and trap; Thomas Clubb, Railway Inn, Parr (present tenant James Johnsen), for opening his licensed premises during prohibited hours ; and James Gerraty, Farmers Arms, Windle, for opening his licensed premises during prohibited hours. Mr. Wood said he objected to the renewal of the last two licenses. He also referred to a spirit license which was taken out twelve years ago by the late Mr. Burchtill, for premises at the corner of Ormskirk-street and King-st. Mr. Burchall died four or five years since, and although the licensed had been renewed yearly to his widow, she had not carried on the business. He asked that it not be renewed. The list contained three public-houses and seventeen beerhouses, which were reported upon in re-; ference to backdoors. The Mayor: Do I understand that at every one of those houses people can get drink without going into any public street whatever. Chief Constable Wood : Yes, that is so, and the police have no access whatever, except by going through private houses, and that they have no right to do with the exception of the Clare Vaults, in College-street, and one in Appleton-street, where there are no doors on the passages. The magistrates then retired. On returning in about forty minutes the Mayor said the applications of William Peet, William Whittemore, James Johnson, and James Gerraty, against whose bonsai or against whom there had, beeo convictions, would be adjourned to the 25th September. As to the list of houses which they found had access from private houses to public houses without going into the public streets, the magistrates looked upon it as most objec- tionable, and thought there ought to be no means of access from licensed houses other than through public roads. The Bench was very strongly convinced of the, impropriety of such arrangements, and would do all in its power to prevent its coutinuauce. They hoped this statement of opinion would lead to a great many of these objectionable, entrances being closed before the ad- journed session, and in that hope they decided to ad- journ the consideration of all these cases. The license of Mrs. Burchall, who had not carried on the business, would be adjourned, with a view to its not being re- newed. The Mayor then mentioned houses which had entrances in aifferent streets; one of which had shc of such entrances. ;The magistrates would'. not say any- think that day in the way of decision, but wanted to in; timate that they were most objectionable. The only , other point was that in applications for temporary licenses or transfers of licenses during the next year, they proposed to ask the applicant his position as to whether he was a bona fide tenant to the premises. That was not a decision that day, but it was what they in- tended to do. With that explanation, the other licenses would be renewed.</p>
        <p>• , • NEW APPLICATIONS. ' The business of the St. Helens Licensing Sessions was resumed on Wedossday, when new applications were heard. The justice a present were the Mayor (Mr. Sinclair), Alderman Harrison, Alderman Cook, Coun- cillor J. Forster. Dr. Gaskell, and Mr. Sharpies. . . .</p>
        <p>_ Mr. J. 0. Swift appeared for John Webster, licensed victualler, Peaslu Cross-lane, awl asked for provisional grant to new prillnises- to be erected in that lane; and also for James Sharpies, 81, Newton-road, who asked for provisional grant to premises to be erected on that road. The application ot John Webster, whose license was renewed on Tuesday, was for thc rebuilding of the Stanley Inn, which house, either by the getting of coal underneatipor the vibration of trains which pass close to the premises, had been rendered unsafe. Some few months ago the owners deemed it necessary to take a portion of the house down, anti erect a temporary build- ing in which to carry on the business. Mr. Swift then explained the circumstances connected with the application of James Sharpies, whose license was also renewed on Tuesday. The Engine Inn, Newton- road, during the last eighteen months had become unsafe, and the upper portion had to be ta.lien off, for fear the wilt le of the building would tumble down,in consequence of the caaahnining underneath. It. was proposed to erect a new house on the opposite side of the road, nearer to St. Helens. Both houses belong to Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co. whose at ehiteets, Messrs. Hiram, of Hardshaw-street, and Mr. James Gandy, of Hall-street, described the plans which had been prepared. In reply to the Mayor, Mr. Swift said all Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co.'s houses were occupied by. &amp;ma fide tenants under an agreement. Chief Constable Wood said he had no record against either applicant. Their conduct of public houses had been satisfactory. Thomas Jenkins, of 75, Charles-street, potter, applied for an off beer license for such pa...anises. Mr. Frodsham, solicitor, appeared for applicant. He believed it was the intention of the trustees of the Gotham Estate, on which the premises were situated, not to allow any ; licensed house on the estate, but they would allow an off license if the magistrates would grant one. The magistrates granted the applications of Webster and Sharpies, but refused to grant the off license to Jenkins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DARESBURY BREWSTER SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY BREWSTER SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>The annual Brewster Sessions were held before Messrs. William Long, (chairman), H. Neild, and C. 11,1albot.—Superintendent Atherton submitted his annual report with reference to the conduct of licensed victuallers and beer retailers in the Runcorn division of Cheshire for the past year. There are 23 licensed vic- tuallers. two beer sellers, and four others who sell off I the premises, a total of 29, which gives an average of. 326 of the population to each license. Twenty-four per- sons had been proceeded against for drunkenness during the year, 21o! whom had been convicted, as compared with 44 persons proceeded against, and4l convicted dur- ing the previous year. The application of Mrs..Sarall Joynson, beer seller,for a full license of her premises in Grimsditch-lane. Whitley enferior, was granted, and tn transfer of the Ram's '</p>
        <p>. 11.11W1</p>
        <p>a;?•re</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS HORTICULTURAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS HORTICULTURAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL BROW;</p>
        <p>The eighth annual exhibition of fruit, flowers and vegetables, in connection" with the above society was held in a field adjoining.the'Newton Nurseries, kindly lent by Mr. J. E. Pardey, on Saturday,under very favourable circumstances. The morning opened threateningly but as the day advanced, the clouds cleared away, and but for a cold wind, which blew across the ground, the afternoon was fairly favour- able for a good attendance. The position of the tent has this year been slightly altered, and was fixed across the field. The staging of the material was commenced early in the morning, but it was past noon before every. thing was properly arranged, under the supervision of Messrs. Wilson, Taylor, Hill, Anderson, Boardman, Bcown, Williamson, Liptrott and Jackson. The benches were closely packed and the exhibits ! appeared to be quite as numerous as on any previous occasion. Ile absence of such exhibitors as Mr. 'r. J. Gillespie, Mr. A. C. McCorquodale, Mrs. Jones, and Mr. T. D. Grimke Drayton was noticeable, but the reduction in the open class was fully made up in the amateur class, in which the competition was exceptionally keen. The exhibits in every class were remarkably fine, and bore evidence of the successful season. Two groups of miscellaneous plants were shown in the open class, and were ranged with considerable artistic effect, that shown by Mr J. Smith taking off the first prize. Some very tine stove plants were staged, Messrs. Hilton and Randall awl Mr. G. F. McCorquodale sharing the honours. The cut flowers were of a healthy class and well selected. A fine let of vegetables were exhibited, Mr. E. Last being as usual pretty well to the front. Amongst the amateurs Mr. T. Howard did exceptionally well, his home grown potatoes being very fine specimens of their kind. The attendance of the public during the afternoon was fairly good, and much interest was taken in the exhibits. The judges were Messrs. Kipps, Webster, Parks, and Bounds, and their 'decisions gave every satisfaction. The cottage garden judges were Messrs. Wilson, Randall and Jackson. The whole arrangements were carried out under the supervision of the general Committee, of which Mr. T. Wilkinson is secretary..</p>
        <p>The following is a full list of the Prize winners :- CLess 1.-OPEN; PLANTS-Group of miscellaneous plants, arranged for effect, space not to exceed 48 feet, 1, J. Smith, 2, Hilton and Randall ; six fine foliaged plants, 1, G. F. McCorquodalo, 2, J. Smith ; six table plants (pot not to exceel 61n.), 1, Hilton and Randall, 2, J. Smith ; six ferns, 1, G. McCorquodale, 2 ,J. Smith ; three ivy-leafed geraniums, 1, Hilton and Randall ; six begonias (any variety) 1, hilton and Randall ; three I pots !ilium (any variety), 1, Hilton and Randall ; six coleus (distinct), 1, G. McCorquodale, 2, J. Smith ; six fuchsias, 1, T. Rutter • six zonal geraniums, 1, Hilton and Randall, 2, G. McCorquodale; one stove or green- ' house plant (in bloom), 1, G. McCorquodale, 2, J. Smith ; one fern, 1, U. McCorquodale, 2, J. Smith; one palm, 1, J. Smith, 2, G. McCorquodale • three cocks- combs, 1, J. Smith, 2. G. McCorquodale; six stocks in pots, 1. T. Rutter. 2, Hilton and Randall. CUT FLOWERS.-Best boquet, 1, Hilton and Randall, 2, G. McCorquodale; twelve dahlias (double), 1. Hilton and Randall, 2, G. Clarke, (Great Sankey) ; six dahlias (double), 1, G. Clarke, 2, Hilton and Randall ; twelve varieties dahlias, single, 1 of each, 1, H. Collingwood, 2, J. E. Pardey ; six varieties dahlias, cactus, 1, G. Clarke 2, Hilton and Randall; six spikes gladolia (dis- tinet);l, Hilton and Randall, 2, G. McCorquodale; twelve roses (distinct), 1, G. Clark, 2, Hilton and Randall ; twelve asters, 1, G. Clarke six carnations, 1, Hilton and Randall, 2,J. E. Pardey ; twelve pansies (6 I show and 6 fancy), 1, G. Clarke, 2, Hilton and Randall; twelve distinct varieties cut flowers, outside (not more than 3 stems or blooms of each), Hilton and Randall; 2, Rev. H. Monk ; twelve zonal blooms (not less than 6 distinct varieties) 1, G. McCorquedale, 2, H. Coiling- wood.</p>
        <p>FRI:RT.-Six dishes of fruit, 1, G. McCorquodale; two bunches of grapes (black), 1, J. E. Pardey, 2, J. Smith two bunches of grapes (white), 1, G. McCorquodale ; one dish of peaches (6), 1, U. McCorquodale, 2. .1. Smith ; one dish of nectarines (8), 2, G. McCorquodale; one melon, 1, H. Collingwood, 2, G. McCorquodale; one dish of apples, cooking (6), 1, S. Boardman, 2, E. Last, 3, Hilton and Randall; one dish of apples dessert (6), 1, S. Boardman 2, J. E. Pardey; one' dish of pears (6), 1, S. Boardman:2, J. L. Wood. Veowramas.--Collection of twelve varieties, 1, E. Last, 2, T. Rutter; six varieties of potatoes (6 of each variety), 1, E. Last, 2, J. E. Pardev ; one dish of pota- toes, 6 tubers (round), 1, E. Last, 2, J. E. Pardey; one dish of potatoes, 6 tubers (kidney), 1, Fl. Tayleur, 2, J. E. Pardey; two cauliflowers, 1, H. Anderson, 2, H. Col- lingwood ; two cabbages (white), 1, R. Anderson, 2, A. Cotterill; two cabbages (red), 1, R. Anderson, 2, W. Boardman ; three sticks of celery (white), 1, W. Board- man, 2, A. Cotterill; three sticks of celery (red), 1, A. Cotterill, 2, W. Boardman; one dish of onions, spring (6), 1, W. Boardman, 2, B. Anderson ; peas (20 pods), 1, E. Last. 2, 5. E. Pardey; broad beans (12 pods), 1, Hilton and Randall, 2, T. Rutter ; scarlet runners (12 pods), 1, J. E. Pardey, 2, R. Anderson; French beans (20 pods), 1, Sergt.-Major Strange, 2, H. Collingwood ; two cucum- bers, 1,.1. Rutter, 2, Hilton and Randall; one dish toma- toes (91,1, Hilton and Randall, 2,1. Butter; two vege- table Marrows, I, H. Collingwood, 2, G. Clarke (Ivy• Bank) ; six turnips 1, El. Collingwood, 2, S. Boardman; six carrots, 1, A. turnips, 2, W. Boardman ; two let- tuces, 1, W. Boardman, 2, H. Collingwood ; six leeks, 1, T. Rutter, 2, H. Collingwood ; one dish of shallots (12), 1, W. Boardinan, 2,5. E. Pardey; three red beet, 1, T. Rutter, 2. J. E. Pardey. Crass 2 -Alum-Jas. - PLANTS. -Three greenhouse plants (any variety), 1.5. Mussell, 2, J. Naylor; three tochsias, 1. J. Naylor, 2, H. Tayleur; two zonal gerani- ums (double), 1, C. Rutter, 2, J. Naylor; two petunias (double), 1, J. Mussel!, 2, J. Naylor; two coleus, 1, T.. Rutter, 2, R. Barton rtwo ferns (exotic), 1, J. Owen, 2, It. Barton; two ferns (British), 1, J. Naylor, 2, J. Owen; two begonias (any variety, 1, H. Tayleur, one pot liliurn lancifolium, 1, E. Last one hydrangea, I.; J. Naylor ; two ivy-leafed geraniums 1, T. Rutter 2, R. Barton ; one ornamental foliage plant, I, R. Barton, 2, T. Butter; one greenhouse plant (in flower), 1, J. Naylor, 2, J. Mussell ; six stocks (in pots), 1, T. Rutter, 2, S. Board- man. CUT Flowens.-Six roses (distinct), 1,1.1. Mlissell, 2, S. Boardman ; twelve pansies (show and fancy), 1, .1. Lowe, 2, Rev. H. Monk ; twelve asters, 1, H. Tayleur, 2, S. Boardman ; six dahlias (double). 1, J. Lowe, 2, Rev. EL Monk ; six dahlias, cactus, 1, Rev. H. Monk, 2,5. Owen ; six varieties dahlias, single (1 of each), 1, J. Owen, 2, Rev. H. Monk ; six marigolds (French), 1, W. Boardman, 2, J. Houghton ; six carnations, 1, Rev. 11. Moak ; six antirrhinums (spikes in pots). 1, J. Owen, 2, I'. Rutter ; six phloxes (serummondi), dis- tinct variety, not more than three stems of each, 1, J. Naylor, 2, J. Mussel! ' • six phloxes (herbaceous), 1, S. Boardman, 2, Rev. El. Monk ; six gladioli (not less than 3 varieties, 1, E. Last, 2, T. Rutter ; six distinct varieties cut garden ri owers (outside), not more than three stems or blooms of each, 1, S.Boardman,2, Tatutter ; six zonal blooms (not less than 3 distinct varieties), 2. Rev. H. Monk ; six zinnias (distinct) 1, B. Last.2,Rev. 11. Monk. Fism.-One bunch of grapes, 1. R. Barton, 2. J. Owen; one dish of :Apples, cooking (6), 1, E. Last, 2, S. Boardman ; one dish of apples, desert (6), 1, S. Board- man, 2, G. Clarke. Ivy Bank ; one.,dish of pears, 1, S. Boardman, 2, E. Last. VENETABLES.-Collection of potatoes (4 varieties), 1, T. Howard, 2, E. Last ; dish of potatoes, 6 tubers (round), 1, R. Anderson