 T. Howard; one dish ' of gooseberries, 1, T. Howard ; one dish of currants, 1, , E. Last. ; VEGETABLES.-Collection of potatoes (4 varieties), 1, J. Lowe, 2, T. Howard ; six potatoes (round or kidney),</p>
        <p>7 1, A. Cotterill, 2, T. Howard ; one cabbage (white), 1. A. Cotterill. 2, J. Houghton ; one cabbage (red), J. Houghton, 2, W. Boardman ; one cauliflower, 1, It. Anderson, 2, J. Dean ; six onions, 1, J. Aspinall, 2, W. Boardman ; dish of peas (12 pods), T. Howard, 2, A. : Cotterill ; dish of broad beans (6 pods), 1, J. Lowe, 2, W. Boardman; dish of French beans (12 pods), 1, J. Aspinall. 2, W. T. Strange; dish of scarlet runners (12 pods), 1, R. Anderson, 2, T. Howard ; two lettuces, 1, A. Cotterill, 2, W. Boardman ; three leeks, 1, J. McLaren, 2, A. Cotterill ; twelve shallots, 1, T. Howard, 2, J. Aspinall ; two sticks of celery (red), 1, A. Cotterill, 2, J. McLaren ; two sticks of celery (white), 1, J. Lowe. 2, W. Boardman ; one vegetable marrow, 1, A. Cotterill, 2, J. Houghton ; six carrots, 1, J. McLaren, 2, W. Board- man ; six turnips, 1, W. Boardman, 2, J. Lowe; three red beet, 1, T. Howard, 2, A. Cotterill. CLASS 4.-FARMERS.-Ten varieties of potatoes (6 of each variety), 1, J. E. Pardey, 2, E. Last ; one dish of potatoes, 6 (round), J. E. Pardey, 2, E. Last ; one dish of potatoes, 8 (kidney), 1, E. Last, 2, T. Abbot ; two Scotch cabbages, 1, J. E. Pardey ; three Swede turnips, I,C. Pennington, 2, J. L. Wood ;three mangold wurtzels (red), 1, C. Pennington, 2, J. L. Wood ; three mangold wurtzels (yellow), 1, T. Abbott, 2, C. Pennington. CLASS 5.-EXTRA. PRIZES.-Members only, paying not more than £l5 rental, with or without small green- house, can compete for this prize.) Best kept cottage garden in the Society's district to be judged on or about Saturday, August 19th. Entries to be made before the 15th August. 1, A. Cotterill, Lowton Junction ;2, W. Boardman, Vulcan ; 3, T. Howard, Ashton-road: 4, J. Houghton, Vulcan ; second prize given by Dr. Patterson. Bouquet of wild flowers, gathered and arranged by children under 14 years of age, within the Society's dis- trict. Entrance free. 1, May Barton; 1, M. Dean ;3, I H. Randall; 4, M. Wilkinson; 5, A. Barton; 6,8. Rand- all ;7, C. Haynes: prizes given by Mr. R. Barton. Best I collection of cottager's window plants, four in number. I 1, copper kettle, given by Mr. H. Hibbert, 2, R. Andor- 1 son ; 2, 55., given by Mr. J. Fazakerley, G. J. Cawley. 1 The show was closed at eight o'clock for the removal of exhibits, and the tent was afterwards made available ' for dancing. During the afternoon the Earlestown brass band played several excellent selections of music, and also played for dancing.</p>
