      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>COAL TRADE CRISIS.</head>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>TEIE COAL-OWNERS DECISION.-NO SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>- -- The decision of the coalowners at their Conference in London on Tuesday, was awaited with much interest. 'Through Mr. Thomas Ashton the Miners Federation promised that if the masters would withdraw the notice of 25 per cent reduction in wages the men should return to work and not ask for another advance till coal prices reached the level of 1890. The colliery proprietors, after considerable deliberation, announced that they were unable to accept the proposal—that they could not withdraw the notices, inasmuch that the state of trade could not uphold the existing rate of wages. They also declined to entertain the suggestion that the masters and the pitmen should combine to raise the price of coal to an artificial level, asserting that such a course would be disastrous to both employers and employed. They expressed regret that the miners had refused the offer of arbitration, which was still open. MR. SETON-KARR AND THE ST. HELENS MINERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seton-Karr, M.P. for St. Helens, who it was alleged had in conversation with several miners said, "D— the strike, why don't you accept 10 per cent. or 15 per cent, reduction and not ruin the country 2" has sent the following reply to Mr. Faram, of the St. Helens Conservative and Liberal Unionist Working Men's Assoc- iation :</p>
        <p>Inversnaid Lodge, Loch Lomond, N.B. Aug. 27,1893..</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Faram.—ln answer to yours of the 25th, which I have only just received here, I can only say that my reported conversation with miners at Rainhill seems to have received more attention than it deserves, and to have been made the subject of some romance. My own version is as tollows :—Walking down from Mr. Swift's house at Rainbill to Thatto Heath on the morning of Saturday. A,ugust 19th, I met several miners, some of whom asked me to subscribe to the "strike funds." I told them I was unable, on principle, to do this, as I did not think it right to identify myself with either side in a strike. Subsequently I had some friendly conversation with the miners, during the course of which I expressed my regret at the occurrence of the strike and the hard- ships it must necessarily inflict on the men and their families as well as injury to trade in general. Whilst disclaiming all knowledge of the merits of the strike as between masters and men, I further remarked that I hoped there would be some give and take on both sides, and mutual concessions whereby a speedy settle- ment could be arrived at. The above is, to the best of my recAlection, a summary of the short and casual con- versation between the miners and myself to which you refer in your letter. You may judge for yourselves how much truth there is in the report you quote. I have also received a telegram (reply paid) from the "Express " office, Liverpool, informing me that at a meeting of St. Helens miners a letter was read stating that I, at Rainhill, in answer to miners' requests for sympathy, replied," D-- the strike; why don't you accept a reduction and not ruin the country ?" and ask- ing me to wire reply. The reply I sent ran as follows: "Report of my Rainhill conversation interesting but untrue." I await with interest further romantic and startling versions of a casual roadside conversation. The whole point ot these reports of my conversation is, I presume,to endeavour to show that I have no sympathy with the miners, whom I represent, in their trouble. I feel convinced, however,that my St. Helens friends know me well enough to know there is not ihe slightest found- ation for such a supposition. As the political represen- tative of a large industrial borough, I am prevented, in the event of a strike, from taking any position but a neutral one. lam the political representative of both sides. I know nothing of the real merits of the dispute. Masters and men, as everyone knows, must decide these questions between themselves, until some adequate tribunal arbitration has been established by law. Ls a matter of fact, if I have personal sympathy with either side, it is not on the side of the masters but on thp side of the men, that my sympathy is enlisted : and for this reason, that the men are far and away the greater sufferers of the two in the event of a strike. They have no reserves of capital to draw on, and cannot, as a general rule, afford to wait the time or pay the price necessary for a successful strike. If they do succeed, the victory is almost as dear as a defeat,and the increase of pay they may gain does not compensate them, and never can compensate them for the time and pay lost whilst on strike. This is the obvious result of relentless and unalterable economic laws. There are other wider ' considerations ; a trade strike means trade injury and possible permanent loss of trade to this country. The greatest sufferers from such loss of trade are first and foremost the working-men themselves. Employers as a last resource, can remove themselves and their capital, or what is left of it, to other localities or other indus- tries, and possibly to other countries ' • but what pos- sibility is there for the bulk of working-men to make such a transfer? Permanent loss of trade means so many men thrown out of employment, and a permanent reduction of wage-earning opportunity. Transfer of capital to foreign countries means employment of foreign labour in place of home labour. For these general rea- sons, and in the interests of the men themselves,l would always use whatever influence I possessed, as oppor- tunity offered, to endeavour to bring about the termina- tion of a strike on any reasonable terms. Strikes may occasionally be a disagreeable necessity, but the leaders or promoters of a strike, incur.in my opinion,a tremend- ous responsibility. I look upon the present growing tendency to strikes in our great home industries as a matter of serious national concern and danger, which I trust the solid good sense of the country will before long find means to mitigate or avert.—Yours truly, H. SETON-KAREC. To Mr. F. Faram. . . _</p>
        <p>P.S.—This letter has extended a greater length than I intended when I sat down. You may make what use you please of it. U.S. K. The four miners who made a statement regarding Mr. Seton-Karr stated on Wednesday that they adhere to their version, and are prepared to meet Mr. Seton-Karr on a public platform. One or two of the four men are Conservatives. THE POSITION AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The decision of the coalowners not to withdraw the notice of reduction has been received at St. Helens with considerable equanimity, and although the union funds are practically exhausted the men declare they can hold out for another month. Mr. Glover, the agent, has addressed a letter to the more wealthy inhabitants of the town in which he says the question which should be considered at this moment is not whether the men are right or wrong, but that of assisting the starving women and children. Councillor John Forster, J.P. has sent the following:—" August 20th, t 893. Mr. Glover, —I quite agree with you that, in face of the distress in the town, it is net simply a question of the strike being right or wrong, but the duty of all to help the suffering people as far as they are. pAssible. In this spirit both my wife and myself have trued to do our duty, and I here- with enclose a cheque for £2 for your fund. I hope there will soon be it satisfactory solution of the difficulty —Yours truly, John Forster." Councillor H. Martin on Wednesday, sent Al to Mr. Glsver, to be added to the fund. Hotpot, loaves, and soup, are being supplied to women and children from various centres, and many tradespeople have liberally responded with food, &amp;c., to enable this to be done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TRAIN REDUCTION AT ST. EIELENS.</head>
        <p>TRAIN REDUCTION AT ST. EIELENS.</p>
        <p>The London and North-Western Railway, in conse- quence of the paralysing effect of the coal strike, on Wednesday issued a list of nearly one hundred passenger trains which will be discontinued on Thursday, and orders have been issued from Euston that drastic mea- sures must at once be taken to reduce mileage expendi- ture. A St. Helens correspondent states that in that district about fifteen signalmen have been temporarily stopped, and that some of the mineral branches are practically closed. Few people are travelling, and even , commercials find their visits to the town almost fruitless. Railway servants are alarmed at the prospect, and work- men on aU grades fear the results on all industries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CHEMICAL TRADE.</head>
        <p>THE CHEMICAL TRADE.</p>
        <p>The news published on Wednesday that there was no prospect of an immediate settlement of the coal strike caused considerable uneasiness in the Widnes and Run- corn districts. The situation in the chemical trade in Widnes and Runcorn at present is very serious. The whole of the chemical works are practically stopped, and ' 3,000 men are idle. The stock of coal is now almost ex- hausted, and it is considered highly probable that, rather than pay the high price demanded for coal, the United Alkali Company will entirely close their works. In fact, one of the chiefs of the firm in an interview some days ago stated that would be the course pursued. Nine thou- sand men and youths would be affected. The gloomiest forebodings are entertained, and tradesmen complain bitterly of the dislocation of trade. " KNOBSTICKS " AT ST. HELENS. On Wednesday night some lively scenes were witnessed at the Bold Colliery, St. Helens. It got to the ears of the miners on strike that about a dozen day wagemen, and others had gone down the pit. They met with a lively reception on emerging later on from their clan- destine labours. A large number of colliers were wait- ing for them, and ofter a moral lesson on the degradation of " knobsticking " some of the younger and more ardent spirits unceremoniously threw one of the offen- ders into the brook. About five o'clock on Thursday morning a couple of hundred colliers assembled at the pit mouth to prevent any men going down. Only two workers, however, dared to put in an appearance, and they were easily persuaded from going down. - ^^</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SITUATION IN SOUTH WALES.</head>
        <p>THE SITUATION IN SOUTH WALES.</p>
        <p>In South Wales on Wednesday there was a still further increase in the number of men who returned to work at the local collieries ; whilst some of the larger pits, which have been idle hitherto, resumed operations. The secre- tary of the Associated Coa!owners estimates that as many as 40,000 miners are actively following their occu- pation in South Wales at the present time. Processions and mass meetings, formerly so popular, are on the de- cline, although some gatherings took place on Wednes- day. The bulk of the strikers remain firm, but every- where the desire to return to work seeing to be growing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>DURHAM BALLOTS.</head>
        <p>THE DURHAM BALLOTS.</p>
        <p>The official return of the Durham miners' ballot issued on Tuesday shows that 20.872 votes were recorded in favour of striking, and 19,704 for continuing at work, si vi ng a majority of 1,078 tor a strike. This being below two-thirds,the majority is insufficient to carry the vote, and the men will therefore continu3 at work. The Durham Miners' Executive have asked the owners for an advance ef wages. NO HELP FROM NORTHUMBERLAND.</p>
        <p>The Northumberland miners on Tuesday, by a large majority, voted against any levy being made in aid of the Federation colliers on strike. Proposals to grant donations were also vetoed. FEEDING THE CHILDREN AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the shopkeepers at Parr was held on Wednesday night in the Baptist Schoolroom to consider the best method of dealing with the distress. Coun- cillor Burt presided, and it was resolved to form a re- lief fund for the Pocket-nook district. The subscrip- tions already promised amounted to £7 per week. It is intended to feed 200 children a day, the number which will be increased as money co:nes in. A working com- mittee of ten will watch the district and seek out the most needy cases. It is felt that so far promiscuous relief has been a mistake, and it is intended to divert the relief funds into one channel. Councillor Brown is giv- ing £1 ptr week while the strike lasts. The soup kitchens at the Corporation boiler-house, at the En- gineer-hall and other places still continue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>THURSDAY'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>It was reported on Thursday that the distress amongst the miners and their families caused by the stoppage in- creases daily'. Almost everywhere now the men are de- pendent upon outside help. Relief committees are being organised in every direction, and a ready response is generally reported to the appeals for funds. The Cum- berland miners have now demanded an advance of 10 per cent. Some of the flunslet miners, incensed because they heard that the deputies at the Broome pit were loading coal in the mine, surrounded the dwellings of these officials, threw bricks and stones and smashed many windows. The colliers' wives took a prominent part in the disturbance. Owing to che scarcity of fuel several ironworks have been closed in Staffordshire. A gang of reckless miners went to the Elornthorpe pit, near Renishaw, in East Derbyshire, late on Wednesday night, and, opening the points on the colliery lines, started two coal-laden trucks down the steep gradients. The waggons dashed through the gates upon the Midland mainline, doing much damage. The signal- man on duty in the locality warned the driver of an incoming goods train and stopped an excursion train, otherwise there would have been a serious accident, for the track was strewn with wreckage. Some of the miners marched on the pit banks at Bolsover on Thursday and threatened violence, but were convinced by the manager that coal-loading was not in progress, and dis- persed. From South Wales it is reported that there is a general disposition to resume work except in parts of Monmouthshire in which the English Federation has a stronghold. Already, it is said, something like half the miners have got to work again. The action of the remaining halt will depend to a large extent on the deci- sion that may be arrived at at the mass meeting to be held at Pontypridd on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WILL OF THE LATE JOHN HORNIMAN.</head>
        <p>WILL OF THE LATE JOHN HORNIMAN.</p>
        <p>The will, dated 21st March, 1893, with two codicils, of Mr. John Horniman, late of Coombe Cliff, Croyden, who died on the 12th August, was proved on the the 30th August, by Mr. Frederick John tiorniman, the son, and Mr. Samuel Robert Brewerton, and Mr. Joseph Gundry Alexander, executors. The value of the pers-onal estate amounts to upwards of £313,7132. The testator le.avei to his widow an annuity of £2,000, and his house at Coombe Cliff for her life, the remainder to his son Frederick John liornitnan, to whom he bequeaths the sum of £lOO,OOO. The freehold estates at Bletchingley, Surrey, are devised to Frederick John Ilornitnan absolutely, and male entail for ever. In addition to numerous gifts and annuities to relatives, friends, and ' servants, amounting to over £23,000, he leaves the following charitable bequests, all free of legacy duty The Peace Socinty. £lO,OOO ; Friends' Foreign Mission Association, £12,500; Friends' Syrian Mission Commit.: tee, £ll,OOO ; Friends' Home Mission Committee, £11,000; Friends'Temperance Union, £2,000; Moravian Mission Society £lO,OOO ; North Eastern Hospital for Children, £10,000; Lndota Temperance Hospital, £5,000; Howard Association for Prison Reform, £2,000; Kingston Monthly Meeting of the Society of Friends for those in indigent circumstances, £3,000 ; Bedford Institute First Day School and Home Mission Association, 25,000 ; Friends' First Day School Association, £2,000 , Friends' Christian Fellowship Union, £2,000; to Mr. It. B. Brockbank and others, for the Spread of Friends' Principles in Scotland, £2,000. The residue is left to his grandson, Emslie John Llorniman, and grand- daughter, Annie Elizabeth Frederica florniman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>A. MANIA FOR RAILWAY TRAVELLING.</head>
        <p>A. MANIA FOR RAILWAY TRAVELLING.</p>
        <p>It is an insatiable passion for railway travelling that has brought Mr. Wm. Edward Cartwright, solicitor, et Newcastle-under-Lyne, into the Hanley Bankruptcy Court. Such at least is the bankrupt's account of his position. " I don't think," he °Wei ved, " that a day has passed without my finding myself in a railway train somewhere or other." Hereupon the following colloquy took place ;—The Official Receiver: What for ? Debtor: Oh, goodness knows I don't.—Do you mean to say you took journeys by rail without any object ? Without any object at all.—How long has this been going on ? I should think for the last three or four years.—Why. I really cannot tell you why it was. I have been all over the country I should think.—When you made these journeys did you go and return in one day. ? Some- times I stopped away, and sometimes returned on the same day.—Did you go long distances? Yes : I have been to London for no earthly object at all. It may seem incredible, but it is true.—Did you go alone on these journeys Yos.—Always ? Well, not always. Occasionally I took my wife, abd sometimes the children but not frequently.—This peculiar mania, Mr. Cart- wright stated, had been " on for three or four years, if not longer." FROM THE RANKS.</p>
        <p>The late Mr. Henry Ward, the Blackburn cotton spinner, who died last wen, was a self-made man, born in a humble cottage of the manor of which he afterwards became the lord by purchase from Lord de Tabley. His school life was brief, and at an early age he was appren- ticed to a Blackburn hairdresser, in which connection the wags of the town dubbed his first cotton mill "T Lather-box." By the time be was twenty-rive he had saved £BO, and, having secured a capital helpmate, he commenced the hand-loom weaving of light cotton goods, and gradually blossomed into a hand-loom manu- facturer on a considerable scale. He was on the high road to fortune when a stroke of had luck, in the shape of three big bad debts, brought him up, but in his emergency a friend, impressed by his ability and character, lent him timely assistance, and from this time he went steadily forward. Ile was a typical Lan- cashire lad to the last, plain and homely ot manner, direct and even, blunt of speech. bat rigidly honourable in all his dealings. His invariable rule in business was to strictly abide by his price, and as his goods were always honest make they never failed to command the market.</p>
