      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday evenin; Sir G. TREVELYAN, answering Mr. Hazier, said the Government did not propose to bring in a Suspensory Bill in regard . tothe Church of Scotland. Mr. GLADSTONE, replying to the same bon member, said the Government were in- clined to view with favour the Scotch Church Bill which had been introduced by Sir C. Cameron, but consider- ing the circumstances, they had no intention of proceed- ing with the Suspensory Bill of which notice was given early in the year. Sir %V. FOSTER informed Mr. Macdona that the Medi- cal Officer of Health at Hull had given it as his opinion that the case of alleged Asiastic cholera in that town was merely one of English cholera. The House went into Committee of Supply on tte Navy Estiniates, and the shipbuilding policy of the Government was criticised by Lord G. Hamilton, Sir, Kay-Shuttleworth denied that the Admiralty ha!betfl guilty of any delay or of a reversal of the policy of the late Government, and Sir W. Harcourt said the late Government made a great show and made the present Government pay for it. Sir E. Heed called attention to the dangers attending vessels of the Victoria class and suail ed vti chteoyri awweasre bostiirn dut. o K. ea ay p. ss i ue ttrehewonrthinpjruormjdiseaas that full inquiry should be made into the case of the Victoria.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords a question put by Lord Stanley of Alderley in reference to certain schools itt Holyhead and in Cheshire,led the Earl of Kimberley to remark that his Lordship had "the Vice President of the Council on the brain." The intent ion of the Education Department, added the noble Earl, was to apply the rules of the De- partment with impartiality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening Mr. CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN was questioned by several hon. members as to the qualifications of the Duke of CON- NAUGHT for the command at Aldershot, to which he has been appointed. The right hon. gentleman said his Royal Highness had been selected by the COMMANDER-IN CHIEF by reason of his fitness for the duties and of the fact that he was practically the senior available officer. There were one or two general officers senior to him who in one sense might be available, but they were discharg- ing duties from which it was not desirable that they should be removed. Mr. A. C. Moirrox said he should call attention to the matter on the Army Estimates. Mr. Astiurrw informed Mr. Clancy that the reports of medical examinations which had been made of Dr. Gal- lagher, a convict at Portland, all agreed that the pri- soner exhibited no symptoms of insanity. The time had not come when he could interfere with the sentences passed on John Daly and other treason-felony prisoners. Sir W. HARCOURT, replying to Mr. Noble, said con- sideration should be given to the question whether the Local Veto Bill should be made applicable to pleasure steamers at seaside resorts. .</p>
        <p>The Rouse again went into Committee on the Navy Estimates. In the course of the discussions Mr. Ilortnav- soN said the Admiralty had decided that the new rate of pay for unskilled labour in the dockyards should be 19s. a week, except at Woolwich and Deptford, where the rate would be 205., and he mentioned that the question of establishing a 48 hours a week system was under con- sideration.</p>
        <p>After progress had been reported, Mr. J. B. Roamers moved resolution having for its object such an altera- tion of the proposed Welsh University charter as would enable persons not connected with the colleges of the University, to obtain its degrees after examination. Mr. ACIAND, asked the House to agree to the Vv'elsh University scheme which had been prepared, and he be- lieved the three centres of education which were to be included in the University would be to Wales in a smaller degree what Oxford, Cambridge, and the Vic- toria University had been to England. Mr. Robert's motion was then negatived without a division.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. Mr: GrAnsroive rose at noon on Wednesday in the House of Commons, amid loud cheers, to move the third reading of the Home Rule Bill. The Prime Minister,who spoke for an hour and five minutes, said experience drawn from many other countries showed that they were in favour of a division between Imperial and local affairs ' • indeed the authority of the civilised world as exhibited in permanent literature was with him on this subject. They had spent practically eighty days in dis- cussion on the bill, arid two ends had keen secured. They had passed the bill through the House of Commons, leaving available the rest of the session for Britishlegis- lation. These results had been obtained by the use of 1 the time closure, an evil only to be tolerated to guard against greater evil. the Opposition had revealed a great deal of old English pluck and fortitude worthy of a better cause; and a very heavy price had been paid for the prolonged debate. The time of the House was the treasure of the people; and the Opposition had appre. priated it good deal of it, making 938 speeches, occupy- ing 152 i hours. Their great object had been to prevent the utilisation of any part of the session for British legislation, but in this intention they had been thwarts d by the Government. The pleas of the Opposition were monstrous and hideous falsehoods. The bill was com- plex because it was moderate, and was a sincere attempt to solve the Irish problem. Our treatment of Ireland had stained the escutcheon of England. Much had been done in the way of reform's'', I conciliation, but the relations between the two countries were far from being an honour to the warm and generous heart. He denied that there Was a polit'cal genius of England or to her her awn da so n tl-1 the encouragedlrish in h race ; his work. ohrak. d faithnd looked theirk e d e, h l triumph. Mr. COURTNEY moved that the bill be Hmoeusemberrsosehrt twenty minutes to six o' early of the bill through the House as indicative of an afte and severalti clock. spokenread tddaeybas six wmaosn adjourned,ths . THURSDAY. In the House of Commons on Thursday evenivg, Mr, MUNDELLs, auswcrilfk a question, said tha claim of landowners to the foreshores adjacent to their properties were not le cognised without careful investigation by the legal advhers of the Board of Trade, who are prepared to receive authentic information of encnachments. Since 1867 coastguard and Customs officers had been under instructions to report any attempted encroach- ments. Sir E. GREY, also in reply to questions, said Her Majesty's Government had received no information that the French Government contemplated the termina- tion of the Convention with this country fgr the govern- t ment of the New Hebrides and there had been no with- drawal on the Dart of the French Goeernment of its assurances on the subject of Siam. Lord Dovexixte,added Sir Edward, was to return to Paris almost immediately. Mr. GLAnsToNE announced that the Government had decided to adjourn Parliament at the close of S9pply until Thursday, November 2, for the purpose of finishing the business of the sessio:a. Mr. ARNOLD MORLEY, questioned about the arrange- ment with the National Telephone Company, said the agreement would be placed before Parliament as soon as it was signed. The debate on the Premier's motion for the third read- ing of tte Home Rule Bill and Mr. Courtney's motion for the rejection of the measure was resumed. The ATTORNEY-GENERAL said the Duke of Devonshire had said in Yorkshire that this bill would be rejected in the House of Lords because it had not been sufficiently dis- cussed in the House of Commons. Ile (Sir C. Russell) repudiated this right to introduce a new doctrine which meant interference with matters which were in the judgmentoio Amongst t the subsequenteoitr ol v of arkheersHwoeurseemro.f Mr. DILLON, and Mr. T. RUSSELL. Mr. JUSTIN M'CARTRY was speaking when the hour for the adjournment of the D. rmttrsoan A, rrs. , debate arrived.</p>
        <p>-- The House of Lords by 50 votes to 27 determined to Insist on their amendment—disagreed with by the House of Connunns—striking the betterment clause out of the Loudon County Council's Street Improvtanents Bill.</p>
