      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>LOCAL _</p>
        <p>- - FELONY AT RUNCORN. On Thursday morning, at RUDCOrn Police Court, Ann Gillespie, of no settled residenoe, was brought before Meesrs: James Handley and P. Speakman, on a charge of stealine, a hat, value is., the property of Samuel Bailey, Regent-street, between the 25h and 20th August.—She was committod to prison for ten days. When in want of a CHASTE and ELEGANT WEDDING or MaTTIDAY PRESENT go to PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-st.—.Autet. WIDNES AND THE COAL STRIAE. There are about 4,000 men out of employment in Wid n, „es throueb the coal strike. Considerable distress prevails and yesterday about 500 people were served with soup at the Town Hall. Similar provision will be made to-day Councillor G. I. Neil has arranged to give a free dinner daily to 150 children in Matins Hall, on the line adopted by Mr. Liptrot, ot St. Helens. He will commence his operations to-day, Mr. Couacillor Shuttleworth has provided a ton of potatoes. Joscerr PRITCHARD, 51. High-street, Runcorn, keeps SPECTACLES to Suit all sights, front a shilling a I.air.--4drt RUNCORN TONIC SOL-FA SOCIETY. The annual general meeting of this society was held on Monday evening, in Ellesmere-street schoolroom, mi- lder the presidency of Mr. Burrows. The statement of I accounts showed that the income was £ll3 odd, and the ' expenditure £ll5, leaving a debt on the year's working of nearly £2. The increase of members was from 74 to 85, and the number of h merary members had risen from 39 to 106. It was considered that the choir having in- creased their expenditure by £2O or £3O during the , season, the result was highly satisfactory. Mr. Speak- man, J.P., C.C., was elected president. JOSEPH PRITCFIARD, WRlobMaker and Jeweller, 51, High ' st., Runcorn.—Adrt. A PHOTOGRAPHIC ASSOCIATION FOR WIDNES. On Wednesday evening a very successful meeting was held in theWidnesChessClubroom,for the purpose of ' forming a photographic association for Widnes. It was decided to establish a society, the following officers be- ing appointed :—Mr. V. C. Drillield, president ; Mr. G Warner, vice-president ; comtrettee Messrs. J. S. Sin- clair. W. Priestnall, Arthur Wareing, and H. White, hon. secretary and treasurer, Mr. G. Reay. The first meeting has been arranged for September 13th, when an address will be delivered by Mr. Driffield. Go to PRITcTIARD's, 51, High-street. Runcorn, for the best FivE GUINEA WATCH a workingman can have.—Arivt. SUDDEN DEATH OF A CAPTAIN AT RUNCORN. Mr. J. E. Worsley, coroner, held an inquest at the L. and NW. Hotel on Tuesday morning, on the body of George Crowder, aged 57, captain of the flat Florinde, of Bedford Leigh. The deceased's boat was lying in Run- corn tidal basin on Sunday evening, and the boat of his sOO-in-law, Robert Parker, was alongside. After having tea, he went on the latter boat and was in the act of lighting his pipe, when he fell over and died. Dr. Robinson, who was called in, informed the jury the deceased had the appearance of a man who might die suddenly, and apparently suffered from earthy degenera- tion of the veins. The immediate cause of death was failure of the heart's action.—A verdict to that effect was recorded. If you want your WATCH REPAIRED by skilled workmen take it to PRITCHARD'Svt ASSAULT ON A RUNCORN POLICEMAN. On Saturday night a navvy, named Thomas Giblin, was lying across the footpath in High-at., and using most offensive language to the passers by. Police-con- stable Ridgway, finding he was drunk took hint into custody, and with the assistance of Police-constable Gregory, took him to the police-station after a long struggle. He several times threw him to the ground, and kicked Gregory twice en the stomach, and kicked both officers many times on the legs. Giblin was on Monday brought before Mr. DAVieS and Mr. Speakman, at the Runcorn Petty Sessions on a charge of being drunk and disorderly, and of assaulting the police. He was committed to goal for thirty-five days' hard labour, in default of paying. a total fine of 259. and costs, IBs. Bd. PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test for fifty years, and quality and first-class workmanship are still guaranteed.— Advt. TREAT TO CATHOLIC CHILDREN AT RUNCORN. On Saturday afternoon, the children attending the Catholic Schools of Runcorn and Weston had their annual treat. In the first place they paraded tho town, headed by the band of St. Edward's League of the Cross , under the conductorship of Mr. Corcoran. Among those who walked were the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. Richard Clinch, J. P. Quinn, M. J. Redmond, M. Fahy, James Loughlan, and J. H. Leigh (Wolverhampton),the school- mistresses and teachers. After the procession, the chil- dren, numbering over 300, partook of a substantial tea, dispensed by the Misses Commerford, Miss Eva Done, Mrs. and the Misses Redmond, Misses Gleeson, Mrs. Done, Mrs. Peacock, Mrs. Connell, Mrs. Clinch, Mrs. Quinn, Mrs. Nixon, Mrs.ldoxworth, Mrs. Costtlo, Miss Harrison, and Miss Lacey. After tea the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, gave a short address. Mr. Hoxworth presided over the subsequent proceedings. Songs were contri- buted by Mr. Tierney, Mr. John Durr, and Mr. Sunder- land (St. Mary's, Widnes), selections were rendered by the band ; and a magic lantern entertainment was given by the Chairman. On the motion of Mr. Clinch, seconded by Mr. J. P. Quinn, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the ladies who had provided and dispensed tea. FUNERAL OF Mn. EVAN WILLIAMS, OF RUNCORN. On Sunday afternoon the remains of Mr. Evan Williams, Salisbury-street, Runcorn, were interred in the cemetery. The deceased, who was a native of Gwetheryn, Denbighshire, came into this district 20 years ago. For a long time he had been a prominent member of the Welsh Chapel in Rutland-street, where he had occupied the principal offices. He was also an active worker in the Sunday-school. By his consistent conduc:. he had earned the regard of an extensive circle of friends, whe extend their sincere sympathy to his widow, who has been somewhat suddenly bereaved, her husband's illness only extending over a week. He was 37 years of age. His remains were followed to the grave by those associated with the Welsh Church at Runcorn, by the agents of insurance companies, and by representatives from the Post Office. The service was conducted in Welsh, in an impressive manner, by the Rev. Edward Lloyd, of Bootle. and the friends joined in singing the hymn, "Bydd myrdd o rhyfeddodau," which translated means "There. will be countless wonders." On the coffin were several beautiful wreaths, one of which was forwarded from the officials at the Runcorn Post Office, at which the deceased laboured two years ago. P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C.A. On Sandal there was a good muster, and a hearty meeting, presided over by Mr. T. S. Kent, president. Mr. S. B. Stringer gave an interesting address, and two solos were rendered by Miss Cosgrove, viz., ,-43 Rest in the Lord "(" Elijah"), an " Calvary." Mr. F. Hickman ably accompanied upon the piano. A TRAMP AND HIS HEAD. A man named John Wilson, 40 years of of age,and described as a tramp, was admitted to the Warring- ton Infirmary, on Sunday, suffering from scalp wounds. He was found in an unconscious conei- tion by some railway men near Walton Arches, lie was discharged from the institution on Tuesday. lie could not be induced to say how be came by the wounds; he assumed the most reticent attitude when interrogated. When pressed particularly hard respecting the presence of the wounds, he once said to a nurse "They came themselves." BITRTONWOOD SCHOOL BOARD. The usual monthly meeting of the above Board was held in the classroom, Burton wood, on Tuesday last, when there were prcsent Messrs. James War- burton (Dallam) chairman, John Warburton, vice chairman, Thomas Perks, Thomas Norcott, William Fairclough, John Forster (attendance officer), H. Gawthorpe (school master), and Wm. Webster (clerk). The correspondence for the month was read. The included letter from tho Education De- partment stated that by an error too little grant had been paid to the extent of .£4. The clerk re- ported the receipt of the following sums, annual and fee grant £l4O 10s. 2d.; balance of annual grant £4; drawing grant .C:2 Hs.; Technical Education committee £5; and the same were ordered to be debited in the cash book. The annual report was further considered. The new evening schools was discussed, and it was decided not to open an even- ing school at present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <p>TII E EX A MINER,. SAl'l7l DAY, SEPTEMBER 2. 1893. • THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT WARRINGTON SLAT-MELTE tt GRAND GALA AT THE WARRINGTON RUNCORN. 1.1.0 USES. BATHS. THE POLO CHAMPIONSHIP. BY AN OCCASIONAL CORIIESPONDENY. lIL—THE DE IANDS OF HEA.LTLI. It was are extremely successful affair ; and the Swimming gala held at the new baths on Wednes- --- day evening will no doubt have fulfilled the object for which it was organized: to augment the funds of the swimming club. It was eminently success- ful, and the numerical strength of the audience and their appreciative enthusiasm far exceeded the ex- pectations of the most sanguine officials. But it was a success which was well deserved for it crowned the indefatigable efforts of the officials of the club, in their endeavour to help forward both the interests of their club and the welfare of the new baths, for there is no doubt that the interests of both are beentnirrz more and more cemented, owing chiefly to the extensive char- acter of the club. When the latter was first organized there was a doubt as to its future success owing to the uncertainty which existed as to the manner in which the Corporation would receive certain applications for important concessions, and in the second place because of the lethargic state of public feeling respecting any- thing relative to swimming. But the Corporation met the club in a generous spirit, and gladly conceded privi- leges which were advantageous to the club, and at the s one time the officials of the club set about procuring members, and very soon had more than 100 names on their membership books. That the Baths Committee are desirous of helping the club may he seen from the fact that they granted the club the tree use of the baths for Wednesday evening. The advertised time for opening the gala was 7.30, and before seven o'clock the street in the precincts of the baths was crowded by persons eagsr to secure kII entrance. The Committee had made all preparation to meet the exigencies of " a crush," and were thus enabled to accommodate many more spectators than the galleries were intended to WM. Rut even this extra accommodation was by no means inifficient, for at hilt - past seven the building wai so crowded that all late comers were refused admittance. The laves presence"l ladies was very noticeable, and might be taken as a tes- timony of the interest in swiminin, which is being created amongst them. The building had been well Minn ina led, and this fact, toeeiliee with the eon:sten, performances of operatic inu-iic by an excellent band, under the conduotorship of Mr. Whitehead, lent quite a gala aspect to the proceedings. There were many well known townsmen present amongst whom we netieeil : —Alderman Chandler. Alderman Wrielit. Councillors Smethurst. Muir, allll Wilkinson, Mr. J. Lyon-IV hitds (Town clerk), Mr. Lonedin (borough surveyor), Ilev. 11. V. rigor, Mrs. Picot, Dr. and Mr. Kisby, Pr. and Mrs. Peacock, and Dr. liraide. ALDERMAN CHANDT.EY ON SWIMMING. The proceedings were opened by alderman Chandley who act, das Chairman. In the course of a few reins he said,"l wish to say how desirable it is that the gala we are about to witness should have the effect of encour- aging the youth of this neighbourhood to cultivate the art of at least sustaining themselves in the water in the time of danger. In Rome of the midland counties it is now becoming one of the branches of an ordinary educa- tion to teach and practice the act of swimming ; and when we reflect on the sad calamity which took place a short time ago to one of tier Majesty's line of battle ships—the Victoria—it is almost incredible to know that there were many on that unfortunate vessel unable to swim. (Hear, hear.) Now I don't mean to say to the youth of this town that it is necessary for them even to attempt to attain the same proficiency as we shall see to night, but still it is absolutely a duty, with such facilities as are before us to at least be able to sustain themselves for a considerable time in the water, and so save themselves in an emergency ; and chances might occur when such skill might be of practical service in preserving what would otherwise be the lost life of a fellow creature. (Hear, hear). THE COMPETITIONS OPENP.D. The programme was a fairly lengthy one and did not terminate until about ten o'clock. The first item was a race tor youths under 17 and there were three prizss. In this there were no fewer than 16 competitors. The race was run in four heats and a final and was of four breadths of the bath. There was a good race between W. . Crichton and A. G. Bobertsen, two scratches. Robertson had much ths better of the race but, unfor- tunately, owing to making a false start was disqlialitied, leaving Crichton the winner. The next event created csnsiderable interest it being a two lengths members race, in which there were 30 competitors. Of the three prizes given in this race one was a gold centre medal given by Mr. H. 13olton. There were six heats. Amongst the competitors were Warrington's leading swimmers. In one heat it was interesting to see such veterans—not old veterans to do justice to them—as J. Critchley, J. Dixon, ,J. lieswick, and T. G. Grounds. The heat was won by H. W. Longdin, but the fight between the winner and J. Dixon and 4. Passwick was a very tight one. The winners of the other five heats did their performances in good style. They were A. Monks, W. Reid, B. Hatton, G. A. Scotson, and Clough. In the last heat was a negro named A. Smith, described in another event I as A. Smythe ! Great things were expected of him from the eagerness he showed to get into the water,but as his style was that of surf swimming he had no chanse with , the dexterous exponents of the overhand and side exoke. However, there was one noticeable feature in the swim- ming of the home men and that was the utter disregard for position when in the water. This the judge alluded to with some amount of severity, and advised time cul- tivation of swimming in position which he said Was a great principle in the art of swimming. After an interest. ing open four lengths handicap the most interesting feature of the gala came on. This was the semi-final N.C.</p>
        <p>--- During the past month tbe science and art classes and the Evening Continuation classes will be again commenced in Greenway-road School, by Runcorn School Board. Mr. Moss, the clerk, has published an excellent programme of the subjects to be taught in art and science. The list also includes French, shorthand, and dressmaking. Any young man de- sirous of self improvement has in these classes a chance of obtaining a most useful education at a coinparat ively small cost, and it is to be hoped that the youths of the town will encourage the Science and Art Committee, nor only to continue these classes but to extend their operations, by attend- ing in large numbers and persevering in the studies they select to a UCCXBSt ul issue.</p>
        <p>There is one drawback to these classes of which all concerned are aware. The school is meant only For the :.euching of children in the elements of the diree R's and is ill-adapted for the teaching of science and art, but, as a makeshift until we can ,ibtion the Techhical Institute, it answers the pur• pose to a great extent and it would be ungracious to grumble. When may we expect to have this lii- pt ate ? To all appearances our Board of Commis- siont rs have shelved the subject, but only, it is to be trusted, to take action with greater vigour when it is again revived. The Institute was projected during Mr. Taylor's previous chairmanship it would be very appropriate could we see the foundation stone laid during th present year of office.</p>
        <p>-- I spent the whole of Thursday in Widnes, and it was pitiful to see the crowds of men standing about willing and eager to work, all through the coal strike. It is computed that there are nearly :3,000 men out of work in the borough, and should the strike last another fortnight, there is every prospect ! that this number will be nearly trebled. The stocks lof coal at the United Alkali Co.'s Works are now very low, and will probably not last more than another week. It is exceedingly likely that the Company will stop the whole of their numerous works. The outlook is certainly very gloomy for the chemical trade, and the tradesmen are already complaining loudly.</p>
        <p>In Runcorn, if the effect of the strike ig not an disastrous, it is bad enough. There are about OO chemical workers thrown idle, and the labourers at the docks are losing a day a week. At the Spring Works also only 4 days a week are worked. There are some hundreisrof canal boats towed up besides a number of schooners. In fact the strike hai brought about a general dislocation of trade in the district.</p>
        <p>Harrison Ainsworth has invested the poacher with a certain amount of romance in one of his books, but there was very little of the romantic in the appearance of Samuel Lydiate, or in the position in which he found himself in Runcorn Police Court on Monday morning. His case was a good example of the aphorism that a man is known by the company he keeps. There is no crime in walking the streets at one o'clock in the morning, but Samuel was known as the associate of poachers and when In-. specter Cooper and Sergeant Gisling met him and a companion on 1% aterloo Bridge at that hour, they felt somewhat curious as to the business of Samuel and his companion. The latter made good use of his legs and escaped, but Samuel had to undergo tne indignity of a search, and from his capacious pockets were taken a net, several pegs, a quantity of stones, meant maybe for unfriendly keepers, and a rabbit recently Lilted. For his little sport, Samuel is now spending a month in Her Majesty's Mansion at Knutsford, in default of paying a tine of 20s. and costs, amounting to 13s. It would appear that poachers- must not holler when out of the wood; danger lurks in the streets.</p>
        <p>GILCHRIST LEM URES FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the local conimittee appointed to make.arrangements for the forthcoming lectures together with others interested in the same, was held last evening week in the Co-operative Hall, Church-st., kindly lent for the occasion. Amongst those present were the Revs. Canon Wood,who presided, T. Wilkes, and A. W. Riley, Messrs. W. M. Sowerby, F. Wiswall, S. Taylor, T. Riley, W. B. Filth, J. W. Frith, J. Clarke, Ellis Gatley, and the secretaries, Messrs. J. D. Jones and B. Crosby. The Secretary announced that the objects for which the meeting had been convened were to give some in- formation respecting 1. The objects of the Gilchrist Educational Trust. 2. The character of the forthcoming course of lectures. 3. The purpose of the lectures. The meeting was addressed by the Chairman, the Rev. T. Wilkes and Messrs. Sowerby,Wiswall and Taylor and the secretaries. From the various speeches, the follow- ing information was gathered. Dr. Gilchrist (born 1759 died 1841), left the proceeds of his estate to found the trust which bears his name, and which, as the words of his will testify, is one of the broadest educational trusts ever founded by a single man. The words of the bequest are as follows : He directs that the trustee or trustees of his will shall appropriate the principal fund "in such manner as they shall in their absolute and uncontrolled discretion think proper and expedient, for the benefit, advancement and propogation of learning in every part of the world as far as circumstances will permit, in carrying out the provisions the trustees have founded scholarships in various parts of the world, and have also for some years spent large sums in procuring the delivery by distiaguished men of science, of lectures on scientific subjects to working-men in the various towns in England. By their means the working-men of Run. corn, have for the first time in the history of the town, the privilege of a course of lectures (a privilege which the town cannot hope to enjoy again for many years to come), at a merely nominal cost by some of the most eminent scholars in the world. The names of Sir Robert Ball, Rev. Dr. Dellinger, Professor Seeley, and of the other lecturers who will visit Runcorn are familiar names to all science students, and it is to be hoped for the credit of the town that the Public Hall will be filled to over- flowing at each lecture. Each lecture will be illustrated by the optical lantern which will be manipulated by a professional operator appointed by the Gilchrist Trus- tees. In order that the working classes " for whose benefits the courses are specially given " shall have the full advantage, the Trustees direct that there shall be a very limited number of reserved seats, and these shall not be front seats, so as not to interfere with the occup- ation of the best seats by the working classes. The Trustees desire.that these lectures shall lead be some permanent educational result in the towns where they are given, and they feel it important that the local com- mittee should have some specific purpose in view whieh they desire to further through the instrumentality of the lectures. The "specific purpose" so far as Runcorn isconcerned, is the furthering of the Technical Institute scheine,which has been before the Board of Commissioners for so long a time. Almost all the speakers at the meeting deplored the fact that so little headway had been made in this direction, and the hope was expressed that the course of lectures would create a desire for scientific education, and that an enthusiastic spirit would be evoked among the working classes which would greatly strengthen the hands of the Commissioners in the furtherance of their sckeme. The proceedings closed with a vote of thanks to the Vicar for presiding, and to the Committee of the Co- operative Society for the use of the Hall.</p>
        <p>(By "CLEo.")</p>
        <p>I come now treconsider the question in its most important bearing, not ouly because it affects locali- ties and neighbourhoods but because it affects the welfare of the whole community. The prosecution for preparing a diseased carcase in a slaughter house which prompted the publication of these papers,was an example of the dangers to health and of the liberty of smugglipg such stuff upon the unwary. It will be equally as impossible so long as the pre- sent arrangement is allowed tr+ go en to reduce to a minimum the dangers arising from this eource ae it will he to reduce to a minimum the opportunities of smuggling throughout the town of abominable stuff as wholesome food. The Corporation of War- rington are alive to this danger, and it is time the public of Warrington were also made alive to it. The Corporation byelaws relating to slaughter heuees were passed in 1854, and byelaw 15 seems to have been specially framed to facilitate the ad- mission of diseased animals. It says "No carcase or any portion of a carcase of any animal not in- tended for the food of man, shall be allowed to re- main in any slaughter house or knacker's yard, or the premises belonging thereto for a longer period than 24 hours after the same have been flayed." This is a most outrageous byelaw, for on no pretence, or for no reason whatsoever ought a diseased animal to he admitted into the premises, let alone being al- lowed to remain there for a period of 24 hours, which means that it might remain there as long as it stilted time convenience of the owner. Were this byelaw still in operation, it would be as a seive through which/ it would be impossible to catch an evildoer, for, provided he did not dress the carcase after the orthodox manner in which healthy carcases are dressed for human food, prose- cution against him would be certain to fail even if the collateral defence of the 21 hours' limit seas not available. But fortunately that bye-law, although it remain, with the others, is now null and void or as I he lawyers would say it is now ultia vire., as t he I Public Health Act of 1875 over-rides it and makes the admission of diseased animals into a slaughter house an offence which renders the perpetrator liable to fine and itnprisonment, the onus of proving ignorance of disease resting with the defence. Reference to this outrageous and antiquated bye- law is only made to dissipate any lurking notiona that fieshers or their assietante may have, that they are safeguarded by it ; Wiz; no safeguard, they are clearly liable to prosecution for every diseased beast or carcase which may be found on their licensed premises. It is no argument to say that Warrington butchers are a very reepectiOle class of tradespeople and that no diseaseo beast or carcase would be admitted iuto their slaughter houses. I say nothing to the contrary, but a recent case tried before the magistrates showed what can he carried on under their very noses and yet they may be hoodwinked end ignorant of it. On that occasion one of the most respectable and best known butchers in town appeared before the magistrates to answer to the charge of having the diseased carCaso of a calf in his slaughter house premises, the carcase having undergone aconsiderable amount of dressing. It was, however, proved that the car- case was put there by some person or persons unknown to the butcher, who accordingly very properly was able to leave the court with his reputation unsullied. Here again was evidenced an undesirable, not to say dangerous, feature in the working of the present arrangement. Many butchers sublet their slaughter houses or at any rate grant the privilege of using them to other butchers when they themselves are not requiring them. AS many its four or five butchers use the same pre- mises, each of them employing their own servants. The regular slaughter house men may be all that is desirable, but one sample of the odd jobber whom I saw actively engaged in the work fairly shocked me.. lie wits one of the filthiest and most drunken of the many filthy and drunken "hung suckers" that I have even seen follow a brewery dray in the streets of YV arrington, There he was working away with all the deportment of a man of importance yet with all the characteristics of his filthy and dirty hatiis con- spicuous upon his person. He was apparently no stranger about the place. I turned away from this particular slaughter house with a feeling of dis- gust, a feeling I know which must be fully recipro- cated by lovers of cleanlihees und decency, who, I make bold to say, never for one moment thought it possible for such a thing to go on in their midst. I for one would never have credited it, had I not seen it. I may have to again refer to this incident on the ground of cruelty, at present I may say it afforded ample proof if proof were tieeded,ol the necessity of having the occasional jobber as well as the regu- lar slaughter houee men duly registered as they are in other towns. Besides this lement of danger which may reasonably be ascribed to the presence of such characters in the place is it too much to in- fer that thereby a door could easily be opened for any connivance to get behind the back of the real occupiers and convert a slaughter house into an open sesame for the reception and ulti- mate disposal of a large or. small quantity of diseased meat ? Bear in mind that at present the Health Inspectore have nothing whatever to do with the characters of the persons employed at slaughter houses. To utilize any unlicensed pre- mises for the preparation of carcases for human food is a very serious offence indeed and renders the offenders liable to exceptionally heavy fines, and it is not likely therefore that anyone desirous to get rid of shady stuff at a large profit would tisk such penalties when by a little cunning management a legalized establishment would be at his service. A short time ago there were ugly rumours of slink meat being imported into the town via Penketh, which if imported would have had to be deposited somewhere. Ido not say that any traffic of that nature goes on, or ever did go on, and it may be that the two cases referred to above may have been ex- ceptions to prove the rule that they were excep- tions, but they may on the other hand have been ex- ceptions to prove the rule of the immunity of such cases from detection and exposure. litAtlse tnoeetdh ebeso-saciadll.edlstilips enrovirsn reflection tdhoen d healthan officers to say that little is done, for it would be a sheer impossibility with the present staff, and with the slaughter houses scattered as they are here there and everywhere over the borough and worked through the night as often as through the day, to exercise anything approaching supervision. To put it in its best light the slaughter-houses are visited once a week, and to call this.; supervision would be about as reasonable as to call a penny popgun a weapon of defence. It could not be otherwise. The health department consists of the medical officer who does not visit these , places except when specially called upon; the Inspector of Nuisance, and two sub-inspectors. I One of the latter has recently been transferred to the I nigh t soil department so tt at the general inspection I rests with the Inspector and the sule-Inspector. These are the two available and when one thinks that these officials have to supervise the triperies (which would of course be included in an abattoir) fish and fruit markets, common lodging-houees, milk houses, canal boats, and the general cleanli- ness of the town, one readily understands that the chances of a proper supervision of the slaughter houses are few and far between. The detects to which I have here alluded can only be effectually checkmateilby the establishment of a public abat- toir, for the use of all the butchers, and by the con- tinuous and stringent, supervision which would thereby be provided. I am not a medial man, but it does not require a medical man to certify that serious consequences follow in the wake of a dietary on unwholesome or diseased meat. It is not only the very poor that are exposed to this danger, the thrifty home- wi fe, ever eager to get a good bargain, is a ready victim on wham tn palm off stuff of a doubt- ful nature. Were the effects of such a dietary at once discernable, we would know where we are, but the immediate effects are not always seen, for the seeds of consumption which are imparted into the huruan body through partaking of the flesh of a consumptive or otherwise diseased beast, take time silently to woik out their work of death, and only manifest their fruit in the wasting fatal consump- tion so common at the present day. Blown or stuffed meat, and meat affected with tuberculosis, all contein ingredients for imparting consumption, while meat in a more advanced state of unsound- ness would readily act as an irritant poison with serious consequences. Therefore, it is with very good reason that health demanas that the dangers arising from this source should he reduced to a minimum and not continue to be maintained at a maximum.</p>
        <p>POLO CHAMPIONSHIP, between Leaf-street S.O ,Manchester, and Crescent S.C., Liverpool. The result of the game was awaited with interest, but few anticipated it would be of the character it turned out to be. From the moment the hall was thrown into play Leaf-street displaced their superiority. They had the game entirely in their hands through- out, chiefly owing to the splendid performances of two of the half-backs, 11. Carlow, and A. Smith, the champion swimmer of the Mersey, It was sufficient to arouse sentiments of sympathy in the most unsympathetic of bosoms to watch the way in which the Crescent were overpowered. They did not appear to have the slightest chance of scoring. They were beaten by eight goals to nil. The following are the teams.—Leaf-st. S. C. Manchester, E. Moss, goal ; M. Brown, and 0. Goddard,backs ; F. E. Carlow, half-back; a. Carlow, J. Brown, and A. Smith (champion swim- mer of the Mersey), forwards. Crescent S. C. Liver- pool, J. Lowdey, goal ; R. Redoath, and ft. Morgan, hacks; J. S. Gummersall, half-backs; J. Nevin, 11. Condor, and E. H. Lowdey, forwards. The gala was concluded with "a screaming farce." It was called A HORSE RACR of a length. There were three compstitors Dick Scalia- brick, A. Smith (or Smythe), the negro, and Jones (initial not given). They were attired in a full suit of clothes and silk bats. Their horses were barrels, on propelwliich theywith were paddles. Teyt sito were expected t toupon a this;nd hut could not do it. Scarisbrick and Jones could get no further after falling off their barrels, than trying to re- gain their hats and struggling hopelessly to regain their seats on the barrels. Smith who when his horse had drifted nearly to the end of the length, did manage to regain his seat and propel the barrel the remainder of the distance. Thus he was proclaimed the winner. The competition was very amusing and was undoubtedly enjoyed by the audience. The officials were chairman, Alderman ChandleV judges, Messrs. 0. H. Barker, hon. sec. N.C.A.S.A., and H. Bolton; handicappers to local events, Messrs. G. A. Barlow antl Stanley Wilson ; starter, Mr. O. 11. Barker : check starter, Mr. H. Tat ; referee, Mr. W. Ilacdock ; hon. secs., Messrs. T. O. Grounds and Stanley Wilson. THE RESULTS. The following were the results of the competitors. The numbers after the names of the winners are the number of seconds allowed. YOUTHS, Race (under 17 crate of age).—Four breadths of the bath. First prize, silver medal (presented by Mr. E. Warburton) ; second prize, steel case keyless watch ; third prize, silver pencil case. First heat, W. Crighton (11); second, IL Hatton (10) ; third, A. G. Robertson (11) ; fourth, W. Ford (2). In the final the winners were as follows, first prize, W. Crighton ; second prize, W. Ford ; third prize. B. Hattori. A. G. Robertson was diiqua lified. HANDICAP RACE (for members of the W.S.C.), two lengths of the bath, 50 yards. First prize, case of silver meat carvers ; second prize, gold centre medal (pre- sented by Mr. 11. Bolton) ; third prize, steel cased keyless watch. First heat, A. Monks (6); second, W. Reid, (10) ; third, B.:Hatton (3) ; fourth, 0. A. Season, (11) : filth, 11. W. Longdin (5) ; sixth, S. Clough (r). ln the final the winners ran out as follows : first, G. A. Scotson ; second W. Reid ; third, A. Monks. FOUR LENOTITS HANDICAP, GertN.—Distance 100 yards. First prize, valuable marble timepiece ; second prize, field and marineo rl tvg, il(ak9s third, silverrsdt;o coy, ca. ps i,essrh: e(tr1(1w911 )do;d.. sixth,P. rsTiez;eo,. ssrdsillilvpoejyar.: mounted walking stick (presented by Mr. H. Tort). Coupe,if s) "11Satintc fourthi(S P. . D . flpvsr7o, 0 Gym.:erwp winners nl Crescentersin th ,e(lfi9n) a;1 wi'M;)cetrhkee:jl::FsierlpsVtlailarbrrlii:niegin,tY°n. (loupe. Sto ck port ; second J. Isher- wood, Tyldesley ; third, T. Locker, Warn i ngton.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. S. Cooper, ILA., who on Sept. nth will com- plete his 90th year, began his artistic career at the ago of 17 as scene-painter at the II asti ngs Theatre. The Sultan of Turkey is a man of kingly bearing, but with a thin face and colourless eyes, keen as a falcon's. When he appears in public on ceremonious occasions, he rides a milk-white Arabian horse, which he manages very skilfully, and his manner is most gracious as he bows right and left to the people. One of the unique characters attracted to Chicago by the World's Fair is Captain Jack Crawford, who until recently was chief of the Government scouts on the plains. Captain Crawford has been waging war against the sensational yellow-back literature which fires the small boy's breast to attempt deeds of daring In the far West. He is preparing a genuine book of Western bor- der life.</p>
        <p>A 22 stone mart has just died at Dover, and several papers are saying he is the next heaviest man in Eng- land to Thnnias Langley, another Dover man, whe weighs 31 stone. In England. perhaps; but in Cardiff there is a pilot who weighs down everything under 2i stone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>POLISH COLONY AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE POLISH COLONY AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SAVAGE ATTACK WITH A FIRE SHOVEL. Before Mr. 11. Wade-Deacon at the Widnes Police Court on Friday, three Poles named Martin Ucok- iske, 18, Barn-st., Charles Julius, 20, Barn-st., and Thomas Zernus, 20, Barn-st., were charged in cus- tody with unlawfully wounding another Pole named Joseph Revenkus on Saturday night with intent to do him grecious bodily harm. It appears that during a disturbance which took place in Barn-St., between a number of Poles on Satinday night the prisoner Ucokiske was seen to strike the prosecutor on the head with a tire shovel and Julius and Zernus were also seen to deal him blows on the head, Julius had a stone in his hand. Dr. Hutchinson said the prosecutor was brought to him on SAturday suffering from three large wounds on the head varying from two and a half to one and a half inches in length, and penetrating to the bone. His condition was serious, and he was unable to leave the house. He had lost a large quantity of blood, and much violence must have been used to cause the wounds. Prisonurs were remanded until Thursday. On Thursday they were again brought before Mr. Stapleton Bretherton. Dr. Hutchinson said the man was still confined to bed, and would not be able to appear for a week. The pris- oners were remanded for that time.</p>
        <p>The young Emperor of China has as keen a relish for the pleasures of the table as any epicure in his dominions. Eight plates form the staple of his dinner menu. The Emperor insists on having bears' paws, antelope tails, ducks' tongues, torpedo eels' eggs, camel's hump, mon- keys' lips, carps' tails, and marrow bones served at his table every day in the year. The Russian newsboy must be fearfully and wonder- fully made. The following are specimens of the papers he cries out in the streets of St. Petersburg and Moscow: —Wjedomosty Granonatshalstwa, Olonetzkija Goubern- skija, Pskoffsky Gorodskoe Listok, Jekaterinnslawsky Listolc, Wostotshuoje Objaafienij, Estlandskija berrusk Wjedornesty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>POLICE AND PUBLICANS' " DIRTY WORK." STRONG REHARKS BY THE CHIEF CONSTABLE</head>
        <p>THE POLICE AND PUBLICANS' " DIRTY WORK." STRONG REHARKS BY THE CHIEF CONSTABLE</p>
        <p>OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>James Howarth was charged at the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, with beitig drunk on the licensed premises of James Thomas of the Royal Arms, Bank-street, on August 19. —P.S. Cheeney said that on the above date he was in company with P.C.s Clough and Chrimes,when he noticed a disturbance at the Royal Arms, and there found a man who com- plained of being ejected and assaulted by someone inside , the publichouse. The officers took the man home and then returned to find his assailant. When they got inside the publichause, they found it full and in great disorder, and some questionable characters were also in the place. They saw prisoner at the bar putting a pint pot to his mouth, hut witness could not swear that there Was any liquor in it. They discovered that he was drunk, and he had to be carried home, which was near by.</p>
        <p>MP publican, Mr. Thomas, said that he did not know the man was in the house and he was not served with beer.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable asked him why he Allowed drunken persons to remain in the house, as that was a breach of the law, to which Mr. Thomas, answered that he could not he everywhere at once. Too Chief Constable remarked that the publicans of Warrington were too ready to think that they could allow a oruriken man to remain on the premises as long as they (lid not serve him with drink, and that all they had to do was to call In a policeman to do their dirty work for them, and put the man )ut. It was the publican's duty to eject such men out of their houses and only as a last resort to Call in the police. He was •determined that he would not do their dirty work for thetn. %Villa would norms think if Ile was summoned or ermitting such work ?—The magistrat-s inflicted a line of ss. and costs or seven days, amid warned Mr. Thomas no; to allow such things to he done in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ALI2.:GATIONS AGAINST A SERVANT AT WARRINGTuN.</head>
        <p>THE ALI2.:GATIONS AGAINST A SERVANT AT WARRINGTuN.</p>
        <p>THE ADJOURNED INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an adjourned in- quest on Thursday, in the parade room, at the Police Station, concerning the death of Joseph W. Pilkington, the three months old son of a domestic servant, named Martha Pilkingtou. Mary Whittaker, Napier-street, was the first witness called. She said that she knew the mother of the de- ceased. She gave birth to the child on the 15th Feb., and was attended by Dr. Peacocke. She only remained there a short time, as she was removed to the Work- house. She saw the child and thought it a very delicate one and unlikely to thrive. Four weeks after the birth the mother came out of the house and lived with Mrs. Dale for a time and then remained five weeks with witness. While she was at her house the child could not eat much—she gave it " pobs.7 The Coroner : That is a thing commonly given— milk and sugar and bread—which tends to bring on convulsions. _</p>
        <p>ituess continuing said that the child was kept clean. Dr Peacocke said that ho saw the child at birth and said that he thought it was a poor one ; it wasn't a baby lit for a show. It was doubtful whether it would live. When lie saw the child just before it died he was told it had been attended by Dr. Jack. He then advised them to take it there again. The child was wasting away.— The Coroner : is it not a common thing for children to waste away not through neglect, but from wrong diet. —Oh, yes.—l mean it is possibly with cases where the child is well looked after, but dieted wrongly. Alice Cavanagh, a nurse at the Warrington Work- house, said she remembered the deceased being brought with its mother on theist and being discharged on the 18th. During that time the child was under her con- stant care, and she thought it was not a robust child, The mother seemed to be extremely fund of the child. Sarah Andrews, 27, Napier-street, who knew the mother of deceased said she first saw the child on the day of its birth. She thought the child was a very he small one. S saw it after coining from the Workhouse'. She slept at Mrs. Dales in the night and lived at her house in the day. The child seemed very sickly then, but was kept clean. The child would be about three months old then. While time child was at her house it was kept with plenty of milk and also a half-penny biscuit each day. The Caroner : I fail to have found anything against this woman of neglect. It is &amp;lithe other way yet* Witness, in answer to Inspector Spinks, said that the mother wa:, very kind to the child. The Coroner: Inspector Spinks, do you know if there is any evidence to show either the commission or omis- sion of anything injurious to the child. InspectorSpinks : I think not, Sir. There is another witness you have not examined, Mrs. Lomax. Mrs. Elizabeth Lomax, School Brow, was called and stated that the mother brought the deceased to her house and lived there come time. She was very kind to the child and fed it properly. • Inspector Soinks : I believe you had the custody of the child for a week.—Yes, I had.—How did you treat the child ?—I fed it properly and kept it clean. The Coroner (to the jury) : Gentlemen it now simply remains for you to say whether this woman has done anything she ought not to have done to the child ; or did she omit something she ought to have done for the child and thus cause its death P If this woman was sent to the Assizes I think from the evidence given on oath she would be dismissed. The witnesses have all sworn that the nsother was kind and attentive to the child. He would draw their attention to the fact that Dr. Jack who had attended the child would say that the child had died from starvation ;on the other hand Dr. Peacocks who had seen the child at birth,h i ad said that t was delicate and small. Therefore be thought there was 110 clear evidence for them to commit the woman. After a long consultation the jury returned a verdict of " death from natural causes." The Coroner thought that was the proper verdict to return. He agreed with Dr. Jack in refusing to give a certificate and causing the ease to be reported , because !he thought it had been a case to investigate. The evi- dence, however, cleared the mother from any blame. Dr. Jack : I agree with you, after hearing the evidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>COLLIERS IN WARRINGTON. "ALLY SLOPER" TO THE RESCUE.</head>
        <p>COLLIERS IN WARRINGTON. "ALLY SLOPER" TO THE RESCUE.</p>
        <p>----- - Before the Warrington magistrates on Tuesday, John Ilarrison,a collier from l'emberton,was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Mersey-st. on Monday afternoon, and also with assaulting Jessie Shervvood at the same time and place.--Prom the evidence it was shown that prisoner was very drunk, and on passing Mrs. herwood in Mersey-st., he gave tier a blow in the face without any provocation what- ever. Mrs. Sherwood followed him and give him in charge of P.S. Ilowbottnm and P.C. Monks who brought him to tha police station.—Mr. Garnett (presiding magistrate) Can you explain, Mr. Hunt, why there are so many colliers in the town ?—Mr. Hunt: I think they are here for what they can get.—Mr. Garnett: I think they would not be able to get very much bere.—Mr. White (magistrat('s' clerk) : It is quite a sight to see the men lying asleep about Arpley.—Tha Chief Constable: Mr. Diggle was going to call a public meeting of the unemployed on the fairground on the question of the coal strike, but I told him that if ha did so the col- liers of Haydi.ck and round about, would come here in their thousands, and we have enough of them here al- ready. Mr. Diggle had accordingly given up the idea.— After further conversation, Mr. Hunt said that on Satur- day afternoon, lie was walking along Church-st., when he saw a drum and life band, and two formidable men wearing masks (which he ordered to be brought tip for the magistrates to see). Hundreds of woman and children were in the street. He (Mr. Hunt) went to the police &amp;Bee to get assistance, and when they got back, they found the members of the drum . and fife band in the " Marquis of Granby " drinking. The constablea .got the names and addresses which will probably be use- ful in future. It was discovered that they were colliers from Bedford Leigh, and that the hand had been hired from some football club.—The masks were brought into court, and they were found to be monstrous heads of "Ally' Slope!.s and his wife.—Mr. Garnett re-marked that it wills a pity that the colliers did not imderstand that begging was a punishable offence. lie had heard of a particular gentleman who had boen stopped by colliers asking for money and another case where a .rope had been Placed across the path to stop a CORCil.— Che Chief Constable said that was not in Warrington. He men- tioned the case above to let the people know where the money which was collected went to.—Mr. Garnett a - dressing the prisoner said that acts like his destroyed all sympathy and kindly feeling towards men of his class. It was a great pity that he had not more judgment and common sense than to do such a thing. The magistrates were inclined to act leniently towards him as they were sure he would not do such a thing if he were sober. The assault upon the woman however they could not overlook and they would fine him 10s. and costs or 14 days' iin- prisonment in default.</p>
        <p>After the usual husineas of the Borough Court. on Thursday morning, Mr. Diggle came forward and said that in the papers of the previous day, he found himself reported by Mr. Hunt to have wished to hold a meetin,g of the colliers, but he did nothing of the kind. He was : wishful to have a meeting of the unemployed with the colliers he had nothing to do.—Alderman Chandley (pre- siding magistrate) :We are not here as phhanthropists, Mr. Diggle, hut as magistrates ; we cannot help you.— Mr. Diggle: : The reporters are here, and I wish them to take it down. I wrth it to be distinctly known that it is not the colliers I was going to have the meeting for.— Mr.ilunt said that what he said in court he did not Intend to be published. . It was not right that every- thing ho said should be put into the papers.—Mr. Sutton (to Mr. Diggl.?) ; tire your meetings on behalf of the Wl*</p>
        <p>lemployed by reason of the collier's strike ?—Mr. Digg le. : Yes, I passed through the market last night. I found sit- ting upon the stalls a huge number of men out of work. One man had worked at Howley Mills. Ile said that he had been stopped nearly a month, and a large number of families were in distress. some would be on the streets very soon if nothing was done to prevent it.—Mr. Chandley said that they could do nothing. Ile thought that Mr. Diggle's only course was to contradict any- thing that was riot right about him in the papers.—Mr. Diggle then left the court. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WA R RING TON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Owen Watkins who succeeds the Rev. A. Freeman in the Warrington Wesleyan Circuit is a well known figure in missionary circles, and he has been called the" Wesleyan Moffatt." He has had a distinvished career as a missionary in Africa and it is not very long since his return to England. During the holding of the Conference in Cardiff last month, a correspondent writing to one of the Methodist papers, said that on one of the Sundays duting which conference was held, he had gone to hear the Rev. Owen Watkins, and he could say that the circuit which got that minister, would be exceedingly fortunate. Although not put down for Warrington on the first draft of stations, he will without doubt receive a hearty welcome from the Methodists of the town, coming with such creden- tials. At the same time the three years' system in vogue in the Wesleyan connection, seems especi- ally hard when it compels a minister so highly regarded as the Rev. A. Frsetnan, for instance, to leave the tows. That he will have as successful a minister in the Ilkeley circuit as he had in the Warrington circuit, is I believe the wish of every Wesleyan Methodist in Warrington.</p>
        <p>During the past few weeks, there has been quite a number of men caught sleeping under haystacks in Arpley meadows. These meadows seem to be a favourite haunt of" sleepers out," this being to a great extent due no doubt to the loneliness of .tbe ' district, and its proximity to the Ship Canal cutting, a large proportion of those captured being navvies. This class of people are a source of great annoyance to those who rent the fields, as there is great danger of their stacks being burnt down by lighted matches. The buildings in which the cattle are kept have been broken into several times so that it is probable that some of these gentry would not disdain to pick up any" unconsidered trifle" that might be lying about, besides saving their lodgings. Arpley meadows are not very safe just now for the unpro- tected pedestrian to pass after dark. Not many weeks since, a navvy returning from Warrington to Acton Grange was set upon by four men and held upon the ground until his pockets had been rifled, and even his boots taken from off his feet.</p>
        <p>In the course of its long history Warrington has been famed in several ways. A few years back it was claimed in some quarters that the town would soon be honcurably and extensively known as the a pioneer in sanitation. The experiments which were being worked out at Longford were going to solve some problems which had troubled unitarian for a long time. That was, however, only an agree !able dream which has not yet been realised. But still, Warrington is spoken of °broad. I notice that the Mayor of Ashton, while presiding at a licensing sessions, deplored the drunkenness that prevailed in the borough of which he is the chief citizen. He was, however, not sglthout one ray of comfort, and that was furnished by the fact that the borough stood' very well in the scale "as com- pared with—well, as compared with Warrington, Oldham, and Stockport. In regard to drunkenness it appears that Ashton is a ' black spot " but there are blacker spots, still, and Warrington is one of them. Yet, in face of this inenviable notoriety, which the town is acquiring there are people here.. aye, and magistrates, too, who object to a atop being put to the more or less clandestine drinking which takes place at back doors.</p>
        <p>There are some people in the town who waxed virtuously indignant at the resolution of the mag- istrates last week, to close back doors for business purposes. It may, perhaps, ba some consolation to them to know that Warrington magistrates are by no means alone in making a stand against the back door business. All over the country it has ,been. condemned, and in the neighbouring town ol St. Helens the licensing magistrates have made a strong pronouncement aztinst the continuance of back doors, and have adjourned consideration of the licenses of all houses basing such entrances. They have gone further, an 4 lave been asking some per- tinent questions as to the bana-fidss of the tenants of public-houses.</p>
        <p>I see that your neutral contemporary informs the- public that Messrs. Walker and Co., notwithstand- ing the action of the magistrates at the licensing meeting have decided not to close the door leading into their vaults from Ship-yard. Should the licence be refused at the adjourned meeting. to be held on the 21st September we understand that the case will be taken to a higher court. If the an- nouncement be correct we shall have a ram rtlebre In Warrington, for I take it, there can be no ques- tion of the magistrates withdrawing from their position. Public opinion is entirely on the side of their decision; and besides, there is the dignity of the bench to maintain, rho magistrates cannot afford to have their decisions set at defiance.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire magistrates near Warrington are very inaccessible just at present, to judge from the difficulty of finding one when he is wanted. This week a bicyclist named Joseph Lunt was brought to the Occasional Court to be tried on the charge of stealing .£2.5 from, a house in Barriton where be had put up. The witnesses arrived at about ten o'clock in the morning, and the defendant a little later. A diligent search was made fora Cheshire magistrate, and liven the telephone was brought into requisition but not one could be found, and thus a number of people wasted a whole morning. The defendant had to be taken to Northwich to see if he could be tried there.</p>
        <p>MERLIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was bold on Tuesday. Mr. H. Wade Deacon premided, and there were present the Revs. Father Clarke and Dean Finegan, Dr. O'Keeffe, Messrs. Quinn, Barlow, Shaw, Lewis, and Denby (cleric). SCHOOL MANAGEMENT LABOUR CERTIFICATES. The School Management Committer. recommended: That the best thanks of the Board be given to the Res,. J. Wright Williams for conducting the examination of the Boards pupil teachers in Biblical knowledge for the year 1893.—That Eleanor Dwerryhouse and Florence , Joynson be appointed certificate assistantsat Warrington- read and West Bank senior girl's schools respectively. That the Cleric be desired to take such steps as may be ! necessary for arranging of periodical examinations by ELM. Inspector for "labour certificafes," and that notices be given to all the schools, drawing attention to the requirements of the Act, and to the conditions upon which such certificates will be granted. The Committee reported that in the three schcols the drawing examina- tions under the Science and Art department had resulted In eight " excellents," being obtained out of the 12 de- partments. Simms Cross obtained four " excellents," Warrington road one " excellent "and three " good," and West Bank three" excellent "and one " good."—A brief discussion took place with regard to the labour certificates. It was pointed out that hitherto the chil- dren had been examined individually, but now the ex- aminations was to be by sample, and in consequence, children, who had passed the fifth standard and wished to leave school, had to apply for a " labour certificate," and be examined a second time individually.—Dean Finegan and other members thought the new system rather a hardship.—The minutes were passed. A letter was read from Mr. Phelps, stating that he had fixed Monday, the 2nd October, as the day of examination for special laboar certificates.</p>
        <p>A NOLIDAY WANTED A petition was submitted from the certificated teach- ers in the employ of the Board, pointing Out that the teachers in the employ of the London, Leeds, Liverpool. and other school Boards had a week more vacation than the teachers of Widnes. They suggested that the first wgrk in October, which was the week after the CX/thliß- atrone should be a holiday. The petition was referred to the School Management Committee. RUNCORN SCHOOL noann• The monthly meeting was held on Tuesday night. Mr. Pritchard presided and there were present Dr. Mc- Dougall, Messrs. Taylor and T. Handley with Mr. G. Moss (clerk). The head teachers of the Oreenway-road and Mill Brow Schools was appointed secretaries of the banks. There was no business of public interest.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Potter Palmer is protected from the swarm of people who continually wish to ask her quostions by her private secretary, Miss Laura Hayes. Miss Ilayes has been with Mrs. Palmer for two years and a half. In that time she has mado herself invaluable in the Palmer household. However, she is distinctly a private secre- tar7, directing the work of a stenographer and a type:. writer, whom she has to ai. her.</p>
      </div>
