      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>EXTESSIVH THEFTS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Bor.ugh Court on Saturday, Jane trmston was charged with stealing frcm the house of Levi Martin, fish dealer, 183, Mersey-street, on Tuesday, a shawl value 4s. 6d., on Wednesday, a shawl and jacket value 13s. 6d., on the 20th a shawl, two frocks, a pair of boots, slippers, and other articles valued at £1 63. 11d. Prisoner pleaded guilty. From the Chief Con- stable's statement it appeared that the prisoner had bean engaged to the complaint nt as a servant for a few months. During the time she was there, various articles were missing. As the woman did not return to the house on Wednesday night the complainant examined the boxes and drawers and found various articles mis- sing. The matter wes reported to D.S. Monaghan. It was found that the prisoner was in Knutsford, and hearing that the police were after her, she left the town, but was followed, and arrested at Rostherne. D.S. Monaghan went there and received the prismer into custody. She gave him some pawn tickets which showed that some of the articles had been pawned at Mrs. Jackson's, Church-street, and some at Mrs. Hough- ton's, Mersey-street. Levi Martin identified the articles as his property. Eiizabeth Taylor said she pawned the articles for the prisdner, and admitted that she knew the articles did not belong to the prisoner. The Chief Con- stable said had he known that before, he would have had her in the same box as the prisoner. The magis- trates sent the prisoner to gaol for three months' hard labour, and severely reprimanded Taylor for her conduct, in pawning the articles for the prisoner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>_ ASSAULT ON A SHIP CANAL GATEKEEPER.</head>
        <p>_ ASSAULT ON A SHIP CANAL GATEKEEPER.</p>
        <p>Thomas Jones, navvy, was charged at the Daresbury Sessions on Tuesday with seriously assaulting Godfrey Stanton, lock gatekeeper on the ship canal, on the 21st inst. From the evidence of Stanton, it appeared that Jones wanted to cross the locks, but Stanton refused permission. Jones upon this got into a rage and first threatened to throw Stanton to the bottom of the locks, and then lifted up a plank and struck him a violent blow on the head. The magiitrates decided to send prisoner to gaol for two months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>FOND OF TOBACCO.</head>
        <p>FOND OF TOBACCO.</p>
        <p>Catherine Graham of no address was brought before the Warrington magistrates, when on the charge of hav- ing certain half ounces of tobacco supposed to have been stolen.—P.C. Joses stated that on Sunday afternoon, he saw prisoner lying dowis her full leugth on the parapet in Horsemarket-street. He went to her and asked what was the matter. She replied that she was subject to fits. Witness assisted her to get on her feet, when a parcel nt tobacco fell from under her apron. She said that she had had it given to her in Knutsford, but witness brought her to the police station, when it was discovered that there were 39 hslf ounces of rob loco in the pare .4. The prisoner was remanded until Thursday in order that inquiries may be made. Prisoner was brought More the magistrates on Thursday, but nothing further was discovered with re- ference to the tobacco, and she was hound over to ap- pear when called upon. The tobacco was confiscated as unclaimed property, and the proceeds devoted to the borough fund. A SUSPICIOUS CHARACTER.—MISSING BANK NOTES.</p>
        <p>„„. John Edwards, 46, Hind-street, was charged at the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, with stealing a purse of money containing £3 153., the property of Bertha Kilshaw, on May 30th, and also with being drunk and disorderly on Saturday night—From the statement of the Chief Constable it appeared that com- plainant was removing from the Big Horse Shoe Inn on the day in question, and ths purse with the money was left in the dress which was hanging on the wall upstairs. Prisoner was seen on the landing near the room in which the dress was, not long before it was missed. He had never been seen since then until Saturday night when Detective Monaghan arrested him in Buttermarket-st., in a state of intoxication. The Bench were of opinion that the charge of stealing was not fully substantiated, but that it was a very suspicious case. They gave prisoner the benefit of the doubt, but sent him to gaol for a month on the charge of being drunk and disorderly. The Chief Constable said that on January last, a lady corning out of the National and Provincial Bank dropped three £5 bank notes out of her pocket, and they were never found. It was subsequently discovered that prisoner had had some bank notes and had spent them. When questioned on the matter he owned to finding them, and said that he came with them several times to the police station to see if there was any reward offered for them, and when there was none he spent them. He (the Chief Constable) mentioned this to show what a suspicious character prisoner was. The magistrates said they were sorry that there was no conclusive evidence to convict prisoner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>EXTENSIVE ROBBERY OF RINGS.</head>
        <p>EXTENSIVE ROBBERY OF RINGS.</p>
        <p>Charles Oates, U, Wheeler-st., Salford, was brought before the Warrington magistrates on Wednesday morn- ing, on the charge of unlawfully offering in pledge to Edwin Pye, a gold ring, without giving any satisfac- tory account of the same. Mr. White (magistrates' , clerk), informed the magistrates that since the prisoner had teen arrested, it had been discovered that the ring and others were stolen from Victoria Station, Manches- ter, and the railway company had sent Detective Inspec- tor McGee to take prisoner in custody.—The Chief Con- stable in his statement said that the complainants were the L. &amp; Y. railway in whose employ prisoner had been ass goods porter, and at times assistant in the parcel office. On Tuesday prisoner went into Mr. Morton's pawnshop, and offered a gold ring in pledge. Mr. l'ye, the manager, questioned him closely, and as his answers were unsatisfactory, P.C. Amson was called in, but pri- soner had in the meantime hurried off. He was, how- ever, caught before he could get far, and was taken to the police station. when asked about the ring, lie said that he had found it near Central Station. On being searched two pawntickets were found upon him for rings which he bad pledged. There were also two halt return ticket to Manchester and 12s. 11td. in cash found upon him. Some time after he had been put into the cells, the mat near which he had been searched happened to be removed, when underneath were found six pawntickets for rings which prisoner.had pledged in Manchester, Salford and district. Detective Monaghan then went to the cells and asked him what account he gave of the tickets to which he replied that he did not wish them to be found upon him when he was searched. He then made a statement that lie had stolen them from the L. and Y. Railway parcel office last Saturday. De- tective Monaghan upon this went to Manchester and got the assistance of Detective Cox and then went to pri- soner's house in Salford. While searching the house, Detective Monaghan accidentally removed a picture which was hanging on the wall, when a parcel dropped to the floor. The parcel was find to contain 28 gold ' rings, and also 15 pawntickets for various articles.— Detective McGee applied for prisoner's remand to Man- chester, as the parcel had been missed, and several others also.—The magistrates ordered prisoner to be handed over to the Manchester police. and complimented Mr. Pye for his sharpness in causing the apprehension of the prisoner, as had it not been for his action, the thief would probably not have been arrested. A BROKEN HEARTED PRISONER.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, Martin Coleman was brought before the Warrington magistrates on the charge of being drunk laud disorderly in Bridge-street on Wednesday night. P.C. Amson proved the case. Prisoner whose face was covered with tears begged of their worships to let him off this time. He would go home to Hanley and they would never see him again. Mr. Hunt said that prisoner had been assaulted sometime ago, and his a,sailant had been sent to gaol, and immediately aftc-rwards prisoner was sent to prison for being drunk. He (Mr. Hunt) thought three months would do prisoner good.— Prisoner : 0 laws ! do have mercy ! I would go back to Hanley. I have been under lock and key ever since I came here. Do let me go !—The magistrates said they- would be lenient with him and would send him to gaol for seven days. r'rsoner shed copious tears when taken out of the court, and tried to melt the hearts of the magistrates, but in vain. SQUABBLE BETWEEN NE IGHBOURS AND OTHER ASSAULTS. James Hogg, Orford Avenue, was charged at the War- rington Borough Court yesterday, with assaulting and heating Edwin Melling, on August 25. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins who represented the complainant said that the parties were neighbours, and there was some squabbling between the mother of the complainant and defendant. Later on in the so me day. the complainant and defend- ant met in a pass age near the Avenue, and without any ceremony complainant knocked defendant to the ground and struck him in the mouth causing it to bleed, and swell.—A number of witnesses were called in corrobora- tion.—Complainant asserted that he was called a Scotch pig by defendant, and that he only gave him one blow. —Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for six months, and ordered to pay the costs. Thomas Dickenson, 4 Chapel-court. was discharged on the charge of assaulting Walter Hill also of Chapel- court, on August 17. Mary Broderick was fined ss. and costs or seven days' imprisonm,mt, for assaulting Mary Carey on the 21st August. ASSAULTING A SCHOOLMISTRESS AT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>At the Warringtm Borough Court yesterday. Eliza- beth Robinson, 57, Wash-lane. was charged with assault isig Kate Coleman, 331 Knutsford-road, on the 24th August.—Mr. Arthur Browne prosecuted.—Miss Cole- man is the schoolmistresc at St. Mary's Roman Catholic School at Latchford, and the chilu of the defendant . attended the same school. Miss Coleman had occasion to write to Mrs. Robinson concerning the condition of her child's head, and this appears to have put the defen- dant in a rage, for she came to the school during the school hours, and pulled Miss Coleman from one end of the school to the other, until Miss Behan another teacher separated them. The school was in an uproar, and the children were screaming for upwards of three-quarters of an hour. A policeman was sent for and the woman was put out of the school. After the, evidence of two teachers the magistrates ordered defendant to he bound over to keep the peace for six months and also to pay the costs.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED SUICIDE. Annie Webster, of Trinity Place, was charge., at the Warrington Borough Ccurt yesterday, with attempting to commit suicide hy throwing herself into the River Mersey early the same morning.—P.C. Marsh said that early that morning prisoner was creating a dis: urbane° in Bridge-street, and threatening to take her he, and Mr. Tat, of the Roebuck Inn, was trying to persuade her to go home. She went in the direction of Warring- ton Bridge, agd witness followed her, and just caught her as she was springing over the rails into the river. He caught her by her arms and dragged her back on to the bank.—The Chief Constable said that the constable must have had some difficulty in getting the prisoner upon the , bank as her arms were .as black as coal that morning. Her husband was a labourer at a brewery in Warrington, and was separated from her. She came to him (Mr, Hunt) some time ago about her husband. Something ap- peared to have taken place on Thursday night before the attempted suicide. He believed that she was in a better frame of mind that morning.—Mr. Webster. who ap- peared in court, said that his wife left him on May 8. She wanted to see high life, and wished to stop out late every night. She had been led away by drink.—Mr. Toft who was called denied all this and said that she was a very respectable woman, and though he lad known her for many years, he knew nothing wrong against her. The prisoner complained that she was not allowed to see her children. The magistrates decided to discharge prisoner on her promise not to do such a thing again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>,= MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>,= MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>6ATIIIIDAY.—Before Mr. John Burgess (in the chair), Alderman Chandley, and Mr. Lewis Voisey..</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE Cases —Ellen Haves, Egerton-st. was charged with neglecting to send her step-sou Arthur Tomlinson to school. The boy was sent to the ship " Clio " as he was incorrigible. Joseph Gerrard, bricksetter, Hopwood-st., was lined ss. for neglecting to send his son to school. Mr. G. Riley, school attendance offfr;er prosecuted. THEFT Os' A SPADR.—Thomas McGinnis, a labourer, was charged with stealing from the yard of Mr. Robert Isherwood, on Friday, a spade value 24. 6d. From the evidence it appeared that the prisoner offered the spade for sale at Mr. Houghton's pawnshop, Mersey-st., for 23. Mr. Houghton's son notictd that the handle was</p>
        <p>stamped with Mr. Isherwood's initials and refused to buy it. P.C. Corcoran was informed and he arrested the prisoner. Mr. I•ilwr wood said he had missed about three dozen spades during the year. Prisoner was sent to gaol for 14 days' h trd labour. MONDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison. (chairman) Messrs _</p>
        <p>F. Monks, and S. Wright. DESERTION OF FAMILY.—Alfred Barlow no address, was charged with deserting his wife and five children, and leaving his wife chargeableto the Warrington Union. Mr. Garnett represented the Guardians. Mr. Aukland, relieving officer, said that he found tho wife and child- ren in a deplorable condition. They had not seen the prisoner for some time as he had deserted them. They received £1 from the Guardians for relief. Prisoner was sent to gaol for one month. ASSAULT ON TUE POLICE. —Charles Batchford, no ad- dress, was charged with being drunk and disorderly and with assaulting P.C. Bylatt on Saturday night in Wild- erspool-road.—P.C. Rylatt stated that he was called into Mr. Massey's tripe shop to eject prisoner who was creating a disturbance. When requested to leave the shop prisoner struck witness in the chest. Witness took him into custody, and he was so disorderly that he had to obtain assistance of P.C. Bruen and he was taken to the police station. Mr. Massey and P.C. Bruen corroborated and prisoner was fined 103. and costs or 14 days' hard labour.</p>
        <p>IT FELL INTO HIS POCKET.—Joseph Foy, no address, was charged with stealing from the shop.--of Frederick Wm. flaljwood, Buttermarket-st. on Saturday, a tin of salmon, valued Bd. Mr. Hallwood stated that he saw the prisoner pick up the tin of salmon and put it into his pocket. Witness followed him and gave him into custody.—P.C. Cooper (36) said that he received pris- oner from Mr. Hal!wood and took him to the police station. When charged with the theft, prisoner said that he caught the tiu with his elbow and it fell into his pocket. (Laughter.)—Prisoner was sent to gaol for a month.</p>
        <p>_ SLEEPING ol7T.—Oswald Drinkwater, and Patrick Clynes were charged with doing wilful damage to some straw belonging to J. H. Fleming on Sunaay night.— P.C. Bell said that he found the prisoners asleep on some hay which had been pulled out of a stack in Arpley belonging to Mr. Fleming. He took them into custody. —They were sent to gaol for 14 days' hard labour. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—EIiZa Hill, 6 Dakins-yard, Benjamin 'Casket, 19 Orchard-st., Wm. Bailey, 9 Naylor- st., were dealt with for this offence.</p>
        <p>MAINTENANC3 0 D.DEUS. —George Jaundrell, 19 Warbur- ton-st., and John Jones, 45 Gladstone-st., were each ordered to pay is. pr week towards the maintainance of their children at the reformatory. FIGHTING.—Wm. Curley and James Higham, were summoned for lighting at Bridge Foot on August 17. Highain did not appear. Curley stated that he was fighting in self defence and was discharged, A warrant was issued against Higham.—Wm. Ross, 1 Dakin's-yard and Joseph Dobson, 74 Church-st., were charged with committing a similar offence on August 20, in Greenall- st.—P.C. Clark proved the cases, and the defendants were each bound over for the other's good behaviour and each were ordered to pay the costs.—John Smith, 8 Hill at., was discharged with a caul ion on the same charge committed on August 20. TUESDAY. —Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), Mr. E. J. Guest, and Dr. Starkey Smith. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—EIizabeth Bendel!, a native of Cornwall was discharged for this offence on promis- ing to leave the town. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman), Councillor J. H. Fleming and Mr. John Geddes._ _</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON TIM POLICE.--Thomas Yeman was fined ss. and costs or seven days' imprisonment on the charge of being drunk and disorderly in Lilford-street, on Wed- nesday morning, and also for assaulting P.C. Sankey by striking him in the face with his fist. MOUBLESOMR PUBLICIIOUSE CUSTOMERS. James William Bibby was charged on a warrant with issaulting Thomas Partridge, the landlord of the Imperial Hotel, Bewsey-street, on August 12th. The complainant said that prisoner and his brother came into his house on the day in question and commenced to sing but witness told them that there was no singing license for the house so they must stop. They would not do so, however, and they had to be forcibly ejected. Prisoner's brother, against whom a warrant was also issued, struck witness on the head, and prisoner picked up a shoe and flung it at witness. hitting him in the chest. They were not drunk.—Thomas Booth, barman, and P.C. Corbett sup- ported Partridge's statement, and prisoner was fined 108. and costs or 14 days.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY—Before Alderman Chandley (chairman), Alderman Harrison, and Alderman Sutton. ANOTHER ASSAULT ON THE POLlCE.—Patrick McGrath was charged with being drunk and disorderly on the previous evening, and with assaulting P.C. Wheatley and Ainson, and also with doing wilful damage to the door of the Bulls' Head to the extent of 33.—P.C. Wheatley stated that prisoner was being ejected from the Bull's Head, Bridge-street, the door of which he had broken. Witness and P.C. Amson teak him in custody and they had to get the assistance of some of the by-standers to get him along. He struck witness in the chest, and kicked P.C. Amain on the leg.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for 21 days. SLEEPING Our.—Thomas Holmes, a navvy of Acton Grange, was sent to gaol for 14 days for sleeping out in Arpley fields on Wednesday night. SCHOOL CASES.—Mr. G. Riley prosecuted in the follow- ing school cases : John Barton, Elaydock-street, fined ss. ; John Callan, Hardy-street, ss. ; John Dutton, Win- wick-street, 5.5. ; Joseph Abberley, Bewsey-road, ss. ; Henry Morris, Albert-street, ss. William Shaw, Aikin- street, adjourned ; Dania' Rowland, School Brow, 2s. 6d. The son of Peter Kelly who was an incorrigible truant was sent to Father Nugent's school. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman),and Dr. Gornall. ALLEGED DESERVION.—WiIIiam O'Brien was charged with being a deserter from the Royal Artillery stationed at Gosport. After P.C. Jones gave evidence the pri- soner was remanded until Monday until a reply from Gosport arrived.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 2, 1893.</p>
        <p>assaulting William Vale, 83 Bewsey-road, on August 9. —'rhe evidence showed that the two parties were in the Blacksmith Arms, and in some quarrel which ensued, Leigh was striking at some one and hit Vale in the mouth, knocking 18 artificial teeth out of his triauth. His mouth was also cut and had to he stitched.—Leigh was bound over to keep the peace for six months in one surety, and also pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mr. Wm:Long. and Messrs. H. Neild</p>
        <p>and C. H.Talbot.</p>
        <p>PASSION'S AFTERMOUTH. Frederick Simons was charged with having on the 14th of August committed damage to a wall, the property of Win. Bostock.—De- fendant pleaded not guilty.—Complainant stated that the defendant worked for him. On the day in question he was in a temper and broke down a wall which he had previously built up.—Defendant sTated that the com- plainant did not pay him his proper wagcs, and he broke down the wall in a rage. He was sorry now for having done so.—The Bench ordered him to pay the costs of the case, and the damage to the wall. ' SHOCKING CRUELTY TO A HORSIL—WiIIiaM Eastman was charged with having on the 19th of August at Wes- ton cruelly illtreated a horse.—lnspector Dowty, of the R.S.P.C.A.. appeared for the prosecution.—Henry Grounds said that at 9.30 on the day spoken of he saw a horse driven by the defendant pulling a cart lead of bricks. He again saw the animal at 12.30. On the second occasion the defendant was beating it with a stick. The horse could hardly move. Later on in the day he saw the horse in Wilderspool-road dead.—lnspec- tor Dowtv said the last witness came to his house, and told him -of the affair. He afterwards had the horse skinned, and on examining it found several bruises on the body. It was quite evident that the horse had died from the effects of these.—The defendant denied having beaten the animal except with a small whip.—A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed. PREDATORY DEFENDANTS.—Patrick Lynch was charged with having stolen a quantity of lead, valued at 21., from Mr. Warburton' s works at Appleton. Mr. Jenkin appeared for the defendant, who, he said, had no evi- dence, and he was obliged to plead guilty. Frank War- burton stated that they had missed a quantity of lead. Detective Monaghan proved to arresting the defendant at Warrington with the lead in his possession. A fine of 51. and costs was imposed.—Fred Simons was charged with having stolen a trowel on the 14th of August from Mr. Bostock. Mr. Bostock gave evidence as to seeing the defendant take the trowel out of the shop. Defendant stated that the trowel was his, and had never belonged to Mr. Bostock. He bought it from another labourer. The case was dismissed.—James Harris was charged with having stolen a coat, valued at 203., from L. Williams, an engine driver on the canal, and was sent to gaol for seven days.</p>
        <p>SHE USES THE RIGHT SOAP.. HIVISOII.B Soap save her clothes. Anything washed with Hudson's Soap is thoroughly washed. therefore remains much longer clean.—A pure Dry Soap In fine powder.— Adrt • [l]</p>
        <p>The murder of Mr. Hamilton, an officer of the East Africa Company, has been avenged by men from Her Majesty's ship Blanche, who a few days ago recaptured the fort at Turk's Hill. destroyed one or two villages, and killed many of the Soraalis.</p>
        <p>BMIGRATION.—Intending emigrant. to New York. Boron Philadelphia, San Francisco. GlIl4Ol. China, Japan, Ana tralia, or New Zealand, should take ti _lots (lowest rates) at the " Examiner" Office, by Onion, American. Australian Oriental, and other lines.—John Walker, agent,"Examinee Office, Bewsey-it.. Warrinitton.—ildeit. . _</p>
        <p>A LIVERPOOL SHIPPING EXHIBIT AT THE CHICAGO Ex- POSITION.—The White Star Line Pavilion was designed by M'Kim, Mead, and White. of New York, and built by George Fuller and Co., of Chicago. The interior of the pavilion has been designed with a view of giving as far as possible all visitors an idea of the company's ships which, traverse the various oceans of the world, and to illustrate the comfort found on board the ocean steam- ship of the present date. In the main room are placed various models showing the different types of the com- pany's fleet. Following round the building one sees next a section of the dining saloon of the " Majestic" and "Teutonic," in which a table is laid for dinner. The silver ware, crockery, glass, cutlery, cloths, napkins, finger bowls, the comfortable revolving chairs, &amp;c., &amp;c., are precisely the same aeon the " Majestic " and "Teu- tonic." One sees also two of the sp( cial staterooms of the Atlantic steamers handsomely fitted with wide bed- stead, easy chairs, wardrobe, &amp;c., and hung with pretty cretonne, giving a bright and cheerful effect. Opposite the dining saloon section is the section of the smoking room of the " Majestic " and "Teutonic," intended to represent exactly the comforts provided for the lovers of the fragrant weed. In the interior of the building op- posite the main entrance is the office in which the com- pany's representative is to be found ready to give any information that may be required.</p>
      </div>
