      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>r</p>
        <p>41)7, 1</p>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL,</p>
        <p>-- HALF-YEARLY REPORT OF THE SHIP CANAL DIRECTORS. The report of the Directors of the Ship Canal Com- pany was presented at the half-yearly meeting of the shareholders on Monday. It is as follows : near:Cß.—The half-yearly accounts duly audited and certified are annexed hereto. The bill deposited in the present session of Parliament to enable the Company to raise additional loan capital to the extent of £2,000,000 on mortgage of the undertaking at a rate of interest not exceeding q per cent, per annum, and the bill deposited by the Corporation of Manchester authorising the Corpor- ation to lend the Company any additional sums not ex- ceeding £2,000,000, received the Royal assent on the 12th May last. The bills promoted by the Corporations of Salford and Oldham, authorising them to lend the Company any sums not exceeding £1,000,000 and £250,000 respectively, were withdrawn. PURCHASE OF LAND AND COMPENSATION.—The total area of land purchased to date is about 4,520 acres, the purchase money for which, including compensation for interference, amounts to £1,139,354, in settlement of claims amounting to £2,159,519. Applications continue to be received for the purchase of surplus lands, and several plots have been sold by the Company for wharve and works at various points on the canal. CONSTRUCTION OF WORES.—The following is the report of the Executive Committee :—" During the past half year the weather has been unusually favourable for the works of the canal. There have not been any floods of importance. The works have thus made excellent pro- gress, and unless some unforseen contingencies should arise, the canal will be opened for traffic to Manchester very early next year. The works generally have been carried out at a cost under the estimate of Ist June, 1892, and it is believed, judging from the experience of the past twelve months, that the cost of the work still to be executed will be within that estimate. All the devia- tion railways have been completed and taken over by the several railway companies,-and are being worked by them. The difficult and dangerous works in the estuary on the Runcorn section, which the Directors did not think it safe to let by tender owing to the numerous complication, have been successfully carried out by administration. The water has been admitted into the whole of the section, and the works there now are al- most completed. The two new dredgers whicif•have lately been constructed at Manchester have worked satisfactorily. There are now ten dredgers at work cuttirig the waterway and"rernoving dams, aad this work will not delay the opening of the canal. RAILWAY CLAIMS ARBITRATION. The arbitration before Lord Balfour of Burleigh, with Sir George Bar- clay Bruce as assessor, to septje the amount of compen- sation to be paid to the Lorn and North-Western and Great Western Railway Companies for interference with their existing lines has been completed after a sitting of 24 days. Lord Balfour has awarded to the London• and North-Western Railway Company the sum of £63,9a1, to the London and North-Western and Great We.,,tern Com- panies jointly £31,360, and to the Great Western Com- pany in their own right £5,310. The total amount of the awards in respect of the claims which have now been settled by Lord Balfour is therefore £100,661, while the amount which the Company had to deposit to secure the payment of those claims before possession of the rail- ways could be obtained was £383,713. A claim was also made by the London and North-Western Company for the additional cost of the possible widening of their main line to Scotland. This claim was not submitted to arbi- tration, but the sum of £66.287 has been deposited to meet it, if substantiated by the London and North- Western Company within three years after the comple- tion and opening of their deviated line—the time fixed by Parliament. SHIP CANAL TRAFFIC.—During the half year 708,169 tons of merchandise traffic was carried over the opened portion of the Ship Canal, as compared with 423,579 tons during the corresponding period of 1892. Of this traffic 48,132 tons was chargeable with tolls. The traffic receipts have again been sufficient to cover the local working expenses. During the twelve months within which period Saltport has come into existence 214,664 tons of traffic has been handled there, principally con- sisting of building materials for the works, Runcorn traffic (which has been dealt with there during the clos- ing of the Runcorn docks), as well as general cargo, tim- ber, sugar, resin, pig iron, Sm. Considerable progress has been made with the arrangements necessary for dealing with the large traffic expected at Manchester and other places as soon as the Ship Canal is opened. BRIDGE WATER CANALS BNLERTARING.—The net revenue of the Bridgewater Undertaking, after making the usual allowances, amounts to £28,466 103: 11d, for the half year, which is satisfactory having regard to the serious reduction in the receipts causell by the lengthened stop- page of the traffic during the intense frost in the early part of the year and by the prolonged strike in the cotton spinning industries. The Runcorn dock entrances which were temporarily closed during the construction of the Ship Canal between Runcorn and Weston Point, were reopened for traffic in June last,and are again fully employed by the shipping which has been accustomed to use the port. During the interregnum of about twelve months the Runcorn dock trans was successfully worked through the Weston Point docks, which were leased for the purpose, and at Saltport. The through carrying business to and from London and other places via Saltport is now working with smoothness and regularity. PARLIAMENTARY BustmEss.—The bill described in the report dated 26th February, 1892, which, in consequence of the dissolbtion of Parliament, did not pass the House of Commons last year, was resumed in the present Ses- sion, and received the Royal assent on the 28th March, 1893. The bill which was sanctioned at an extraordin- ary meeting of the Company held on the 27th April, 1893, to enable the Company to sell on chief and otherwise deal with surplus lands, has also received the Royal assent.</p>
        <p>ltivEns POLLUTION,—Owing to the winter floods, a large accumulation of cinders and sewage matter was brought down into the cuttings of the Ship Canal, and imposed upon the Company very large additional ex- penditure in dredging. The Directors in March last addressed a communication to the Mersey and Irwell Joint Committee calling their attention to the approach- ing completion of the canal, and stating that unless the crude sewage which was discharged into the Mersey and Irwell and their tributaries was dealt with at an early date the state of the water in the Ship Canal and the docks at Manchester and Salford would be seriously affected, to the great injury of the Company and danger to the public health. The Directors, whilst fully sensible of the beneficial results which have already been achieved by the Joint Committee, and having regard to the possibility of a permanent charge on the undertaking and the probable risk to vessels using the , canal in consequence of such deposit, consider it of the highest importance, in the interest of all concerned, that the Joint Committee should act in the most vigorous manner possible in order to secure the necessary im- provemeut in the state of the watershed. The Directors have taken what steps lay in their power to deodorise the water in the Manchester and Salford docks and the Castlefield basins, but it is unreasonable that this ex- pense should be entailed upon the Company owing to the default of other parties. DIRECTORS.—On the Bth June the Directors received an intimation from the Mayor of Manchester that the following members of the Corporation had been ap- pointed to act as directors of the Manchester Ship Canal Company in pursuance of the Act of Parliament, viz :—Alderman Sir John J. Harwood, Alderman Bosdin T. Leech, Alderman Anthony Marshall (Mayor), Coun- cillor Southern, Alderman Clay, Alderman Cheaters Thompson, Alderman Mark, Alderman Joseph Thompson, Alderman Walton Smith, Councillor M.Dougall, Coup- cillor Pingstone. VISITORS.—The Directors regret that, owing to serious i interference with the progress of the works, t has been found necessary to refuse permission to shareholders and others to visit the 'unopened portion of the canal and docks, and they are gratified to find that tile share- holders generally have supported then this action. PLANT.—On the 29th August and following days the second sale by auction of plant not further required for the works will be held, when it is hoped that a large portion may be disposed of. Issued by order ot the Board this 25th day of August, 1893. EGERTON OF TATTON, Chairman.</p>
        <p>• The half-yearly meeting of the Ship Canal Company was held on Monday, under the chairmanship of Lord Egerton. Lord Egerton showed how the work was , rapidly approaching completion, and confirmed the ' announcement made some weeks ago by Sir John Har- wood, that, unless sZnnethiug extraordinary happened, the waterway would be opened from Liverpool to Man- chester before the date of the next half-yearly meeting. He appealed to all who approved of the canal, whether shareholders or ratepayers, to use their influence in directing, trade to it, remarking that it rested with them to make the concern a financial success. Sir John Har- wood seconded the motion for ths adoption of the report and it was accepted without any discussion. VISIT OF MANCHESTER CORPORATION. The members of the Manchester Corporation visited the Ship Canal works on Wednesday, traversing its entire course from Eastham to Cornbrook. The pro- gress of the works was seen to be very rapid, and strong hopes were entertained of the fulfilment of the predic- tion that the canal will be opened for traffic " very early in the coming year."</p>
        <p>WARR]aNToTON CRESS CLUB.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Chess Club was held at, the Y.M.C.A. on Monday night, when the chair was occupied by the president, Canon Dodd.—Mr. L. Chandler presented the secretary's report which showed that last season there were 16 members in the club. The record of matches played was satisfactory as they had played seven, won three lost three, and drawn one. (Applause.) In the league they went up one point and had not got the wooden spoon this time, they being the next to the bottom. In three league matches they played they lost two and drew one. The total number of games won by the club was 28, against 26 lost. During the season a_ tournament had been held which had created some ex- citement, and it would be of interest to have _something of the kind again. They had several new members com- ing this time, and they,ltoped to have a still better season this year. He hoped they would pull together. He was glad to see Canon Dodd present (applause), and he hoped that he would give such support to the club as he was able during the season.—The Treasurer's report was next put and adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>SPECIAL MUSHROOM TRAIN.</head>
        <p>A SPECIAL MUSHROOM TRAIN.</p>
        <p>--- On Tuesday night the quantity of mushrooms con- signed over the London and North-Western Railway was so extraordinary that a special train had to he made up at Bangor and despatched to Chester, en route for the Laucashire and Yorkshire markets. The special consisted of five milk vans completely filled with ham- pers of mushrooms. In addition a special van full of mushrooms had to be attached to the night mail for Birmingham.</p>
        <p>An Oregon (Ill.) young woman is making a crazy quilt out of the silk ties which have been given her by her devoted admirers. Her pillows are to be stuffed with their love letters.</p>
      </div>
