      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>NEW MEMBEES.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBEES.</p>
        <p>The following new members were proposed and elected on the motion of Mr. Chandler, seconded by Mr. Sulli- van: Rev. Morley Stevenson, and Mes.srs. R. Bond, T. Ward, L. Barker, and Taylor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ELECTION 'OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION 'OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Sullivan proposed the re-election of Canon Dodd as president and Mr. Fleming seconded the motion which was carried unanimously.—Canon Dodd in reply thanked the members for his re-election. He hoped that the Warrington Chess Club would succeed, and be really a Warrington club, and the members be really Warrington people who would be able to hold their own against any adjoining club. He was glad to have been asked to be president of the club and be would be glad to continue, but he must offer his excuses for not being able to play more frequently, as he was a member of three different clubs, and he had a greht many engagements. He would be glad to do anything he possibly could for the club. It. would be satisfactory if the members of the club could themselves play their match( s and win their games with- out external help. He knew the strength of most of the adjoining clubs, and they would have to play well to beat them. The chess clubs ef the district were improv- ing. The game was now followed up with more vigour, and there were men who played extremely well. The strength of play was increasing, Their only chance would be to increase their strength also. (Hear' hear.) He thought himself that all things considered, for the short time the club had been in existence, and the com- paratively few members, the record of the club had been very good. To win three, lose three and draw one, was a very good record. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr. Wilkinson, seconded by Mr. Dunne, Mr. Garland was unanimously elected treasurer. Mr. Garland suitably replied.—Mr. L. Chandler ex- pressed a wish to retire from the secretaryship of the Club, which he had held since its commencement four years ago, and proposed Mr. H. H. Dutton as his suc- cessor. He would still take part as a private member. It was however considered undesirable by the members to lose the services of Mr. Chandler, and on the motion of Mr. Garland, seconded By Mr. Sullivan, Mr. Chandley and Mr. Dutton were elected joint secretaries.—The fol- lowing gentlemen were elected as a committee : Messrs. Sullivan, Fleming, Mason, Dunne, the president, secre- taries, and treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>MISCELLANEOUS.</head>
        <p>MISCELLANEOUS.</p>
        <p>The next question that arose was whether to join the league or not.—Canon Dodd thought it was very desir- able to join.—Mr. Chandler said there were several ob- jections to it, because although they were in the " C" League, when they played the Manchester clubs, they had to meet players who were in the "A" League. it was disheartening to be beaten by a club which was second-rate, and at the same time the players were members of a first rate club also.—Mr. Fleming moved and Mr. Mason seconded a proposition that the club join the League.—Mr. Dunne moved and Mr. Sullivan seconded an amendment that the club arrange fixtures with Manchester, Liverpool, and local clubs, but do not join the league—the proposition was however carried by a largo majority. After some discussion it was resolved that the club meet each Tuesday and Saturday during the season which would commence in October.—lt was also recom- mended to have a trophy to be competed for by mem- bers of the club, and also to have a tournament.—The usual votes of thanks closed the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>NEW WAY TO GET HOME.</head>
        <p>A NEW WAY TO GET HOME.</p>
        <p>At the Ashton Police Conrt on Saturday, before T. Hulme and other magistrates, George Allen Merchant was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Warring- ton-street on the previous night.—Defendant : I don't think I was disorderly. I think if the constable had left me alone I would have gone home. Constable Williamson stated that on Friday night, at a quarter to eleven o'clock. he saw the defendant drunk in Warring- ton-street. Ile went into the Grapes Inn, but he was in such a drunken state they refused to serve him. He was with two other men, who tried to get him home. In the street he commenced to use bad language. lie refused to go away, and in the end witness had to take him to the Town Hall.—Sergeant Irving also spoke as to defen- dant's drunken condition.—Mr. Hulme : Do you know anything against him ?—The Chief Constable : No, sir. lam surprised to see him in the position be is. I know him very well.—Mr. Hulme : What have you to say ? Defendant : If I had been allowed I could have gone home.—Sergeant Irving: He was so drunk that he was unable to go home.—Defendant : Nonsense,ma n. I have gone home on all fours many a time, and you know it, too.—(Laughter.) The defendant was lined is. and costs.</p>
