        <p>A CORRECTION.</p>
        <p>John Birchall, plasterer, 9, Haydock-street, Warring- ton, writes that he noticed in last Saturday's report of the Berough Court news, a case of a young man giving 'the name of John Birchall charged with gambling with cards in Birchall-street and fined ten shillings and costs, and adds that this is a wrong name, and that the person in question lately lived in Bank-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>THE FIVIER HOSPITAL. Sir,—Will you kindly insert the following. I wish to publicly exprzss my most sincere thanks to Dr. Hornell, the matron and nurses for their great attention and kind- ness to my child whilst in the Fever Hospital. I may say, I consider the hospital a well ordered and comfort- able establishment and a boon to the public of Warring- to.—Yours truly. Froghall-lane, Warrington.</p>
        <p>WM. McINNERNEY.</p>
        <p>TUB DECISION OF THF, MAGISTRATES AT THE RECENT BREWSTER. SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I be permitted to state, without fear of contradiction, that in my opinion the Ship Yard " alterations " to the Lion Hotel required the usual ap- plications sought for in the case of a Hew license? As a shareholder last year remarked in the columns of your contemporary (tho " Observer") was the door in Ship Yard a public requirement, or did it add to the then respectability of our oldest hostler! ?—Yours, Etc. CORDON PANITAIRE. August 25th, 1893.</p>
        <p>BUNG HOLES IN BRIDGE-STREET. Sir,—Allow me to direct the attention of the Borough Magistrates of Warrington to the change that has come over the character of Bridge-st., during the last year or two. I have not had any occasion to give special atten- tion to these matters, which have literally forced them- selves under my notice. I have observed the character of the street gradually change for the worse. Rowdyism has become rampant, and on Saturday nights especially the scenes that are to be witnessed from ten to twelve o'clock, cannot be surpassed fer brutality by any other quarter of the town. I have seen people empty the con- tents of their stomachs in the street, and I have seen a drunken man MP at a poor woman at the entrance to Ship Yard, and fell her to the ground as suddenly as if she had been a poor ox struck with a pole axe. I have witnessed several fights of a serious nature, and have heard heartrending cries of " Murder ! " " Murder ! " issue from the crowds. So frequent have these disturb- ances become that we now look for them as part of the Saturday evening's routine. It would be well if the magistrates who have the power of granting the licenses would frequent the public thoroughfares late onSaturday nights, and see the samples of brewer's work that are limping, waddling., robbing, or being carried home. Me- thinks the sights w )uld cause them to hesitate long be- fore they permitted another Bung Hole in Warrington.— ' Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>6 A TRADESMAN.</head>
        <p>6 A TRADESMAN.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC SWIMMING BATH FOR RUNCORN..</p>
        <p>bir,—Through the medium of your columns I beg to make a suggestion to the" Public Baths Committee' of Runcorn, respecting the construction of a public swim- ming bath. Hitherto I understand that the principal bar to this undertaking has been the cost. Would not this difficulty be very easily overcome by the construc- tion of an open-Air swimming bath. Such baths exist elsewhere. Leicester possesses one, and as a proof of its utility I may mention that an amateur cbampionship swimming match took place in it recently. Why cannot a swimming bath of this nature be constructed at Run- corn. There are several good reasons in favour of the scheme. Many people would prefer an open-air swim- ming bath, to an enclosed one with its stuffy atmosphere. The cost, also the working expenses, whieh would be very small, are strong factors in favour of its adoption. As regards the question of a site, I beg to suggest a site, somewhere between the waterworks pumping station and the brine reservoir. This locality possesses two great advantages. First, cheap land ; second, close proximity to a supply of both fresh water and brine. A supply of the latter could no doubt be obtained from the Salt Union at a reasonable figure, and when mixed with the fresh water would produce a very desirable liquid. for bathing purposes. Such a bath as this would prove a great boon to the inhabitants of the town, especially during such tropical weather as has lately prevailed. Now that the town has been cut off tram the river by the Ship Canal there is absolutely nowhere for the youths of the neighbourhood to acquire the important art of swimming. Trusting that my suggestion will receive consideration in the proper quarter.—l am, sir, yours faithfully. Runcorn.</p>
        <p>I' Z."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>LICENSING SESSIONS AND LICENSING BIGOTS.</head>
        <p>LICENSING SESSIONS AND LICENSING BIGOTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ls it not marvellous the way in which year by veer the self-assumed air of superior fitness, and sound judgment, is claimed by our tippling justices and their jelly companions,for all positions relating to the licensing question. It is amusing how the respectable toper prides himself as the only fit, open-minded, elear-heade.i. true-hearted, and worthy judge, who ought to settle all the alarming evils of the drink question. Such lovers of the glass are the only class of beings to their minds able to do full justice to the needs of the masses of the people. Yet, strange to say, they are the very persons who fear to trust the masses of the people with the power to determine by their votes, as is simply embodied in the " Local Veto Bill," the number of public-houses in any given locality. If a person can and will swallow enough of the brewers' alchemy to redden his nose, sharpen his wit, loosen his tongue to stir men's blood, they " speak right out" and tell to all who differ from them in drink- ing and thinking, to look on them, whose right it is to demur against the decision of their soberer-minded friends on the licensing bench.</p>
        <p>A man may be a sa, neglect his family, through intemperance, and be otherwise low and polluted and polluting, yet his evidence in favour of the drink traffic would not be pooh-poohed and sneeringly smiled at, by some of our magistrates as would the evidence of some well-known, useful and pious minister of the Gospel, who is a total abstainer and supporter of some local tem- Iseranee cause. A number of our magistrates too lightly dub such ministers bigoted teetotallers, &amp;c., esteeming the victims of the drink traffic as being the well-balanced In mind ar.d the freest from bigotry. The supreme fit- ness for being held devoid of bigotry, fit to appear before licensing justices as witness, is to lcve the pipe and the pot, or to reap profit out of the soul and body des- troying drink traffic.—Faithfully yours, PRO-BONO-PUBLICO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>TLIE RECENT BREWSTER SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>TLIE RECENT BREWSTER SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Examiner's report of the proceedings at the Wallington Brewster sessions is interesting for the tol- lowing reasons. In the first place, it shows how determined are the brewers arid publicans to maintain their ground if they cannot extend it. The Greenalls and Walkers monopo- lise nearly the whole of " the trade" in Warrington through the abominable system of " tied houses," and although no less than £200,000 of our working mesea wages finds its way into their tills every year they are net satisfied. Again, it is encouraging tofind that even in Warring- ton, which is, according to Rector Willis, one of the most besotted towns in the kingdom, there are a few men to be found battling against the liquor traffic on every suitable occasion. Let every man and woman in Warrington whodesires to put temptation out of the way of the weak do what they can to strengthen the hands of these men. What is urgently wanted is a sound, healthy public opinion against the traffic expressed individually, through the press, the platform and the pulpit, so as to make it impossible for the magistrates to perpetuate the existing scale of things. The justices have full power to close every public house in the town at their own option. It must not be expected that they will do this at the present time, as public opinion is hardly yet ripe enough for such a drastic measure. But let .us with united voice emphatically demand that they shall make a sweeping reduction in the number of public houses. The peace, order and prosperity of the town demand this at their hands, and if they fail in their duty a terrible responsibility for drunkenness, poverty and crime will lie at their door. Lastly, the decision of the magistrates to close the back doors of all drink shops is a step in the right direc- tion, and I trust that they will act up to their announce- ment without fear or favour. If the vending of intoxi- cating liquors is a proper and useful occupation, why !Mould publicans, any more than grocers, bakers, and other tradesmen, require back entrances for their cus- limiers. The fact is " the trade" is under a ban, and multitudes of the higher class of tipplers knowing and feeling this, do not care to have tho public eye upon them when going in for drinks, and so they sneak in be the secret passages. 'I he closing of these modes of in- gress would be a hardship to this class of drinkers, but it would bring the conduct of the publicans and their friends more fully under the eyes of the police.—yours truly,</p>
        <p>LEPROSY AND VACCINATION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>OBSERVER.</head>
        <p>OBSERVER.</p>
        <p>Sir,—My attention has been called to a brief notice in the " Examiner" of my recently published volume, .4 The Recrudescence of Leprosy and its causation," and I shall be glad of space for further explanation. It is seedless to say that the question of the connection</p>
        <p>between vaccination and leprosy is one of urgent and 1 imperial importance, and must sooner or later command the attention of every one interested in the public health and in the well-being of our vast colonial populations. Evidence to show that there is such a connection was laid before the Select Parliamentary Committee on Vaccination as early as 1871, by Dr. Hall,Bakewell, then visiting physician to one of the largest leper hospitals in the West Indies, and vaccinator-general for Trinidad, but the idea was pooh-poohed by the chiefs of the Poor Law Boards of that time (who had probably never seen a case of leprosy amongst them), and the evil was allowed to go ma unchecked until it has reached its , present alarming dimensions. Evidence of a much , wider experience has been laid before the Royal Vaccin- ation Commission now sitting in London under the presidency of the Lord Chancellor. The eminent bacteriologist, Dr. Edward Arning, .of Hamburg, who spent three years in the Sandwich Islands, studying the causes of the serious increase of this scourge, says, in a paper read before the Berlin Medical Congress of 1890, and published in January. 1891, on the transmission of leprosy, that " a very remarkable local accumulation of fresh leprosy cases took place in 1871-72 at Lahaina, on the island of Mani Hawaii, about a year after a general arm-in-arm vaccine ation, from fifty to sixty cases occurred suddenly in this locality, which up to this time had been coinpara- Lively free." Evidence pointing in the same direction has been given, with particulars of cases of invaccinated lepiosy by Professor W. T. Uairdner, 81.D.F.R.5, of Glasgow, Dr. John D. Hillis, of Demerara, Professor Montgomery, of San Francisco, Dr. Roger S. Chew, of Calcutta, Dr. S. P. Impey, of Robben Island, South Africa, and other well-known authorities. Dr. Arning, Dr. Hansen, and Professor Montgomery discovered the leprous bacilli in the virus of vaccinated lepers. At a meeting of the Microscopical Society of Calcutta, report- ed in the V Englishman," of Calcutta, 23rd December. , Dr. W. I. Simpson read a paper on the Bacillus Leprce. Dr. Simpson said he had vaccinated three lepers with vaccine lymph, and had found the bacilli in very large numbers in the lymph obtained from those patients. He had blistered a patient over a leprous tubercle, and got bacilli in the serum from the blister, hut they were not nearly so numerous as in the vaccine lymph. As all bacterial diseases are inoculable, these experiments shovv the danger of vaccination.</p>
        <p>lam aware of the numerous attempts (in my judg- ment most cruel and unwarrantable) to inoculate human beings with leprosy and syphilis, per se, and of the alleged negative results. The late Dr. George Hoggan, who had given many years close attention to the subject, maintained that if the leprous virus is mixed with vaccine lymph, as in the process of vaccin- ation, the transmission is easily made, and this will account for the alarming increase of leprosy in countries where leprosy is endemic, and where vaccination is enforced. During my visit to Hawaii, in October, 1890, several members of the Honolulu Legislature, and other influen- tial residents begged ma to make known the facts con- cerning special danger, and to assist them to get rid of a medical ordinance which was neither adapted to the native race, nor to the climatic conditions, and was decimating the population with the most hideous and destructive of human afflictions. Allow me to conclude by thanking you fer calling attention to the subject through your influential columns.—Yours faithfully. WILLIAM TEBB.</p>
        <p>Devonshire Club, St. James's, London, S.W. August 30th, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Authority was held yesterday morning at the Workhouse. There were pretent : Messrs. William Houghton (chairman),J. Houghton. Mather, Smith and Swinton; Mr. S. Sutton clerk, and Mr. 4Villiam Owen, surveyor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>RIXTON BURIAL GROUND.</head>
        <p>RIXTON BURIAL GROUND.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM OWEN read the following report respect- ing the Rixton Burial Ground. " The Clerk and I at- tended at Hollins Green on the 10th August, and, sub- ject to your approval, settled with Mr. Antwiss for coin- pensation and crops at 0.6. We also settled with Mr. Gibson, tenant of the Mount Pleasant Farm for com- pensation for £l. Since then I have been in corres- pondence with the owners of Mount Pleasant Farm, and have settled, subject to your approval to pay 2s. 61. per annum for wayleaves for drain outlet from the Burial Ground, the agreement to be prepared by the Authority and at the Authority's expense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>LATCHFORD SHIT CANAL BRIMS.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD SHIT CANAL BRIMS.</p>
        <p>Mr. OWEN also reported that at a meeting of the Street Committee held on the 30th August it was re- solved that no action be taken to recommend the Authority to undertake the lighting of the roads and new bridge over the Ship Canal at Latchford, and that the gas manager be told of this decision in reply to his letter of inquiry.—The reports were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>XSTIMATICS.</head>
        <p>XSTIMATICS.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Scrum, clerk, submitted his estimates for the coming half year. He said the rate would be j of a penny in the 2. The probable expenditure would be. £250 up to Lady Day, 1894. The balance in favour of the Authority now was I.32.—These were aviroved of.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>LONGFORD DEPOT QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE LONGFORD DEPOT QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that the deputation, which waited on the Corporation on the 18th August concerning the nuisance caused he the Longford depot consisted of Mr. Wm. Mortimer, J.P., Mr. Wm. Houghton (members of the Authority), Mr. J. C. Sutton, C.C., Mr. Wm. Owen, and Mr. S. Sutton, clerk.—Mr. J. C. Sutton as county councillor for the division introduced the deputation, and pointed out the serious reasons which the people in Winwick and Orford had for complaint avtinst the emissions from the depot. He also pointed nut that pro- perty in the district had depreciated in value: It was further mentioned that unless an abatement was affected legal proceedings would be taken. Alter evidence had ,been given severally by the deputation Councillor Bea- man (chairman of the sanitary Works Committee) stated with other members of the Committee that complaints had been made that the nuisance arose from Mortitner's tannery. This was refutea by the deputation. Finally the Chairman explained that a large expenditure was contemplated for doing away with the nuisance, and amongst other things for making the present 120 ft. chimney a 2001 t. one, with a larger bore. There were otVr proposed improvements which when carried out would leave no ground for complaint.—lt was decided to wait until the Corporation had made their improvements and act according to the result.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE COAL STRIKE</p>
        <p>The prolonged lock-out of the colliers is having a disastrous effect upon the town of Warrington. The streets are thronged with men out of work, and the number is constantly increasing. The smaller works are closing rather than pay the enormous price for coal and the larger ones are for the greater part working only a portion of their men. During the week, the whole of the employes of Monks, Hall, and Co.'s works, have been out of employment,with the exception of some engaged in the tube works and on repails. There is great destitution among some of the men, and the firm have set an excellent example by giving soup to these. It is possible that a mill will commence work on Wednesday,hut no more puddling will be done until the colliers resume work. At Bewsey and Mara forges, 1000 men were taken on again on Monday,after a week's play, and there are about three-quarters of the men working. The engineering department, however, is closed. The men will probably continue work next week. The mills and forges ot the Whiteeross Wire Company have been stopped during the pre- sent week. lioughtons Wire Works are also seri- ously affected by the lock-out. If the present state of things continues much longer, the smoke of which Warrington is at present renowned will be conspicuouarby its absence, as already some of the works have the appearance of being almost com- pletely closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT OF THE HEALTH OF</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT OF THE HEALTH OF</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During the week, which was the 34th of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :—Births : Warrington, 35 ; Latchford, 4; total, 39. Deaths: Warrington. SO ; Latchford, 2, total 32. The excess of births over deaths was 7. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 30 2 per 1,000, against 282 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 20.2 the corresponding week of last year. The death-rate from the seven chief zyrnotic diseases was equal to 122 per 1.000, against 12-o.the average rate of the four previous weeks,and against 86 the corresponding week of 1802. The ages of mortality were :—Warrington: otol, 15 ; 1 to 5, 5 ; 5 to 15, I; 15 to 25, 2 ; 25 to 60, 3 ; 60 up 4-1 ; total, 30 2-32. The deaths included-10 from diarrhcea ; 1 from scarlet fever: 1 from croup ; I from measles ; 1 from whooping cough ; I from diabetes ; 1 from old age ; 4 from brain disease; , 2 from heart disease ; 1 from liver disease; 1 from drowning. The following_ cases of • infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever, 5; enteric 1. Meteorology . The mean temperature was 62.0 Fah. and therefore 6.4 deg. lower than the preceding week. The maxi mum temperature 78 F., was recorded on the 20th inst., and the minimum temperature 51 F., on the 26th inst. The rainfall measured '59 in. The general direction was N.W.. the reading of the anemometer denoting 726 miles.—JOHN H. e °IMAM, Idedical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>A person who signs himself " A Cricket Enthusiast," forwards on a" letter" but what the object of it is I do not know. It commences by speaking in adulatory terms of cricket, followed on by a treatise on the game, and concludes in the following irreverent and flippant manner. " Hut I'm glad the belly season's over. I'm glad the blank, hot, boiling, summer, which is nice to hear about from poets, but deucedly awful to stand, is gone the way of all things that had a beginning : has gone to its end. And football starts next week ! Hurrah! I can scent the keen air of the frosty winter, hear the lusty shouts of the" gate," and perceive every- thing, in my mind's eye that tends to make the football season the live, robust, healthful thing it is." I don't know why he calls himself a cricket enthusiast. In my opinion he has a greater regard for football than either cricket or—or—for the value of writing paper.</p>
        <p>Poor old first eleven ! What's the matter with you ? Surely you- do not suffer from a bad memory which betrays you into forgetting how to play the game? One is half inclined to think so after the number of matches that have been lost, and the " crushingnesa " as it were of the defeats sustained. Saturday brought another instance this. Preston came to the Arpley ground and won the game by 196 to 52. True they had rather a formidable team, and Warrington were minus the assist- ance of J. J. Crosfield, W. ti. Podmore, and Sugg. But this did not justify Warrington in submitting to such a licking. The game was lost through inferior bowling and bad fielding—and as the Juvenile man adds through Preston scoring too many. I have no doubt that had J. J. Crosfield been present Preston would not have scoreO so extensively, for we all know what peculiar luck Mr. Crosfield has hitherto had in preventing scoring when playing against Preston.</p>
        <p>Ashton was played again and scored three and took no wickets for 22 runs. 11. Smith who had not much luck in the batting did well with the ball taking six wickets for 53 runs. S. M. Crosfield who re-appeared after a continued absence, scored 11 and bowled in- differently. G. Clarke came out with a duck egg, as did also Clark (the pro.) and Tyler.</p>
        <p>Warrington's play all round was poor ;it was weak as the tea we were accustomed to at our early birth- days—but it is far less excusable. lam led to under- stand that there is some inharmonious feeling existing amongst the players. If that is so consistency !cannot be expected.</p>
        <p>The second eleven met Birkenhead Victoria' and did not meet defeat. They came out of tho ordeal with 84 runs Lgainst 68. This was good, considering the calibre of Birkenhead. Reynolds and Hampson batted excep- tionally well for Warrington, whilst Woods and Smith did all tho bowling.</p>
        <p>CYCLING. A good number—or to be correct—a large number of members of the W. C. C. journeyed to their country headquarters, Mickle Trafford, on Saturday. They enjoyed themselves, I have every reason to believe, and indulged in cricket and fishing again.</p>
        <p>By the way the Liverpool Daily Post of Thursday con- tained the following :—`• Lawrence Fletcher, of the Antield Cycling Club, Liverpool, whose machine broke down recently in the attempt to break the bicycle record between Land's End and John o' Groats, started again this week in the effort. He passed through Lock- erbie at an early hour on Wednesday morning, accom- panied by a pacemaker. Fletcher was keeping up a splendid rate of speed, and the roads being in capital order, there is every hope of his accomplishing the feat."</p>
        <p>Well now, all I've got to say about this is that it is very much like some of Artemus Ward's political essays : it is very funny. It was only possible for Mr. Fletcher to follow his route via Warrington, and I learn that he did not call at the usual stopping place,the Norton Arms, where a meal had been ordered fur him, and Wadding- ton of the W.C.C. who had been asked to pace the rider from Whitchurch to Preston, had also heard nothing about him. If he did go through Warrington, surely it must have been late at night, or very, very early in the morning.</p>
        <p>Speaking of Waddington, however, reminds me that he is going to try And break the Cheshire 100 miles re- cord, held by IN. Wright, for 5 hours 59 mica. He is in good form, and lam led to believe that it is highly pro- bable that he will lower Wright's colours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>SWIMMING.</head>
        <p>SWIMMING.</p>
        <p>I have no doubt that the Warrington Swimming C:ub were well pleased with the suczess which the gala meet with on Wednesday evening. I was considerably stir— prised to see such a number of persons present, and was certainly more surprised when I knew that owing to the crowded state of the building at least a hundred persons who came to the door' to lay down their contribution on the altar of swimming," were not allowed that felici- tous privilege.</p>
        <p>The successful manner in which everything was man- aged wou!d, if they were at all given over to pride,make tho officials too conceited to speak to one for a week. The thanks of tbe club are especially due to Mr. S. Wil- son (sec.) and Mr. G. Grounds (treasurer) and Mr. A. 'Bolton. The constant whirl of work in which these three gentlemen were involved entitled them to go on strike fdr a reduction—not in the number of hours—but the number of their duties. In Mr. Bolton the club has a gentleman whose all round knowledge of first class athletics renders his services highly valuable.</p>
        <p>By the way there was one thing which I was not sur- prised about in connection with the swimming and that was the way in which the judge reprimanded the War- rington competitors for their want of care in keeping their places in the water. Hii remarks were very just and well merited. It was quite easy to see that the War- rington swimmers have never taken into consideration that important principle of bath swimming, keeping their proper places.. They were certainly racing in diagonal fashion, and were often- only prevented from colliding with each other through the cries of the spec- tators. As the judge said, they would soon find the disadvantages of such a defect when they came to race in smaller baths, such, for instance, as those at Stock- port and Tyldesley.</p>
        <p>The semi final N.C. Polo Championship between Leaf. at, Manchester, and Cresent, Liverpool, was rather a tame affair; not because the players were uninteresting, but because the superiority ot Leaf-st.' was conspicious. They scored eight goals to nil and the Cresent never ap- peared to have a chance. Leaf-st.„ however, depended upon their two half backs, H. Carlow and A. Smith,with out whom, the team would have been like the "Yeomen of the Guards," without "Jack Point," very much -41. mph.),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>To-day, Saturday, the football season opens, at least so far as Warrington Football Club is concerned. The fixture to-day is with Moseley. The first team is; full-back, Boscow ; three-quarter-backs, Barnes, Evans, fleesom ; half-backs, J. Bate, James Bate; forwards, Jolly, Fair Turner, Dakin, Donohue, Berry, Iligharn, Thornley, Bandies, and Frank Turner.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Warrington Association Football Club, held at the Ring-o-Hells last night, it was resolved that the club should be disbanded, and that the funds should be handed over to the Infirmary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COMNUTTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COMNUTTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Aegess- meat Committee was held yesterday, Mr. Balharry n the chair.</p>
        <p>A TITHE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wm. OWEN (valuer) reported ,that he had had under consideration an appeal made by Mr. William • Bennett, farmer, Poulton, against the Si sessment of his farm. . Mr. Bennett had produced a receipt for rent showing it to be £441 per annum with tithe £27 17s. lid, in addition. He had come ,to. the conclusion that the tithe should not be included in the gross esti- mated rental, as had been done. He read the following question which had been submitted to the Clerk and his answer thereto: Q.—A landlord lets a farm to a tenant at a rental of £3OO out of.which rent the landlord has to pay a tithe of £6O. Can the tenant claim to be assessed I to the poor rate at £240 as the gross estimated rental, taking into consideration the fact that the mode of assessment in the Union is to take the actuil rent paid as the gross estimated rental, and also the fact that the owner of the tithes is separately assessed,* the poor rate on the total amount he received in tithes P The conten- tion of the tenant is that if he is assessed at £3OO and the owner of the tithes is also assessed ou the total amount of tithes he receives that the tithes will be as- sessed and pay rates twice 7—The CLERK'S reply to this question'was that the tenant's contention was right. There was accordingly a reduction made in Mr. Ben- hett's assessment. THE PENRETH RECREATION GROUND. Mr. BeNramtm HOBSON. of Penketh, appeared to appeal against the assessment of the Penketh Recreation Ground, which wa,i £9 gross and a 103. rateable. He contended that the assessment was tar too high. For- merly when the ground was in the jurisdiction of the Recreation Grounds Committee it was not assessed.—The annit : And y.,u wish te get it back to those terms?— Yea,—Mr. A. Jor,okr I dare say you do The CLERK : You appeal to the charity of the Committee then ?—Yea, as the ground is a public one.—Mr. Rita-row pointed out that he had seen cattle grazing on the ground, arm that they must derive something for that.—After a further discussion the Cominitteo refused to reduce the assessment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>IHE EXAMINFR, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 2 1893 CIVILIZATION'S INPERNO• OUR LOCAL CHITROREs.....wARRINGTON. (BY MR. 11. WHITHAM.) I have just reeeß ed several ittLers from friends widely separated in England, but all readers of the " Examiner." The reading of these has caused some little thought. lam charged by one with taking "too sombre a view of life," and that 1 "ought to have a little more of Mark Tapley about me." Another suggests that I give " too dark an aspect to the labour question," and, '' that a man can always get work, here in America, if he wants to work." It is further suggested that I might write of topics that "interest all, rich and poor alike." I may clear the last suggestion out ef the way at once by saying that Warrington, in my opinion, does not need a society pro- per, and, if it did, my pen is not, unfortunately, wielded by a Labby. For the rest I wili try to justify and make good my position. It was gond for Mark Tapley that he could be jolly under all sorts of adverse cbcumstances, even while making a miserable voyage across the Atlantic. Such men are, as Charles Lamb put it, the nurse-children of optimism. I often wonder if it is possible for the angels of the Christian's heaven to know anything of the doings of those other angels who wander about in the Christian hell, the hell as sketched by Dante, by Milton, or, say, by Baxter. Ido not knew this, that the hell I see every day, and of which I read truthful account has a tendency to make one sombre, and to make the Mark Tapley of the novelist, and the M. T., of every day life, two different persons. Let us look at the Mark Tapleys around us. You in Warrington have a few. As em- ployers they spend three or four months every year at Vie seaside, while their workmen stew in the juice of Dial-so and Town's End. As house owners, they eat, drink, and are merry, while the hovels in the town pay them a higher per centage on their investments than the property in Bold-st., or Palmyra. As land owners and brewers,they surround themselves with all the evidences of wealth, buys parsonage, say for Sc. James' Church, and to prove still further their extreme happiness, and disinterestnese apply for a few more licenses (see a re' cent issue of the " Examiner "), so that a British work- man shall have hie beer. This is the British well-to-de M. 'Tapley. There is another, some of us know of, who says, " Oh, well, it's not my business, I'm not going to make my- self miserable with thinking about that sort of stuff,and I'm not going to read about it either?' In the old book a certain personage was asked about his brother. The answer given then, has come down the ages. It is in every man's mouth to day, glib enough, 'Am I my brother's keeper 2" Right glad am I to see the an- swer now being forced, ah, forced. From the pulpit,the preacher is saying yes to this question. The politician is having " Yes," wrung from his heartless carcase, and the philanthropist is abroad demonstrating that there is only one answer to this old, old, question. Yes, I am my brother's keeper, undoubtedly so. Oh, say some of the Ms T.'s " I'm going to mind my own business, look after royself, you know." Well, I think we have had pretty considerable of this," mind my own business," and it seems to me a total failure, whae., say my critical fiends? Now let us look on this side the Atlantic, this "Sweet Beulah Land. In all truth this is a most beau- tiful land. That is; where nature has been left to work out her own sweet will, she, is not altogether "red in tooth and claw." Hawtherne somewhere says '6 That there is but half a lifethe meaner and earthlier half— for those who never find their way into the Hall of Phantasy," My mind often takes me moving through these Halls. Thousands of castles have I built in the air, they have all tippled over, but the building has filled up the higher and less earthly part of life. Here where I live, surrounded by apple trees, ma borne pleasantly situated on the hill side, I often wander, with a congenial writer, through the maddening crowd of the cities. With a "congenial writer," just notice reader nine, that I am like to stray away now and talk about books, novels for instal-ice, I detest the. modern novel, give me good old Sir Walter, I care not much for the rest. But we have a novel that is supposed to be written tor a purpose, like Oliver Twist, a Black HOUSE,. It is possible that the "Mummers," " The Mayor of Caster- bridge," or the "Nether World" have a purpose. It so, well, the last named has accomplished its purpose. I have justfluished it, and Mark 'Tapley nes far away from me as the Poles. Still Gissing is a congenial writer, every word in the " Nether World "is true. True to my own experience. I shall have te comeback a little to your Own Babylen while I give you a long quotation from this congenial writer. " Whit:, what have you been doing to your hair 2" asked Kirkwood. • "lie's dyed it," Mrs. Hewitt explained. " He thought one of the reasons why he couldn't get work was his looking too old." "An' so it was," exclaimed Hewitt, with an angry vehemence which at ones declared his position and re- vealed much of his history. "So it • was ! My hair was a bit turned, an' now-a-days there's no chance for an ••Id man. Ask anyone you like. Why; there's Sam Long couldn't get even a job of gardenin"ca use his hair was a bit turned. ' Dye your hair Jack,' he says ; it's what I've had to do myself,' he says. "They won't have an old man now-a-days at no price." Have you been to see Corder again ? asked Sidney, after reflect- ing anxiously. "No I haven't been to Corder—l beg his pardon; Mister Corder—James Corder Esq. But where do you think I went. this mornitO ? Mrs. Peck. over brought a paper an' showed me an advertisement. Gerbutt, in Goswell-road wanted a man to clean win- dews an' sweep up, and so on, offered fifteen bob a week. Well I went. I got there at half past eight ; an' what do you think I found ? If there was one man standin' at oarbutt's door, there was five hundred. Why, the P'leice had to come an' keep the road clear. Such a lot of poor starving divils you never saw brought together in all your life. I felt like a beast. I wanted to fight, I tell you—to fight till the life was kicked au' throttled oat of me." The above is only out of a novel, and of course, you say, overdrawn. The memory must he dull tat for- gets the reports that have been issued from time to time totiching the dockers. I liave at my elbow Helen Camp- bell'e " Prisoners of Poverty abroad." With Miss Camp- bell for leader we might wander through England's cities and see for mirselves how the nether world lives, but we will forbear that sort of Mark Tapleyism,and hie us back to the States. About the middle of July a panic broke out in this country. It came first with a few rumbling reports that so and.so'e factory had shut deem. These reports soon hadotheir verification in the fact that mills all around began:lB shut down. The largest cotton mills in the world, close to here, employing over 5,000 hands had closed its door for an indefinite time. Another large thread making firm employing over /0,000 had closed its door. A friend writing me from New York stated that over 2,000 union painters alone were walking the streets. I see from to-day's paper that a parade is to come off in that city in which hundreds of thousands of unemployed are to take part. To read of the thousands flocking to Chicago to seek work is like reading something out of Manchausen. In this city of Worcester there is not a factory hut what has either reduced wages or shut the door. The place where my son worked is closed, and he had to go back to a pistol shop for less wage. Where I work at, or did work at, for I have done nothing for two weeks, closed out hundred of men, and gave those that remeined a reduced list. The streets are fairly crowded with men out of work. The is3ue of to-day's Boston Herald " containa some eight or nine columns of reports of visits to the unemployed, and yet one la expected to be a Mark Tapley. It may he said that this is not a nor- mal state of things. Well, I grant that it is worse now, very much worse than it usually is. Still I insist that it is not worse than those who have made a study of the social question expected. That it is altogether abnormal I also deny. During the twelvemonths ending September lit 1892, the appalling fact that twenty-nine thousand seien hundred and twenty warrants for eviction were issued in New York alone should be enough to prove this. One tenth of all who die in that great city are buried in the Potter's field. It is the same in all cities, I have here a report of the exiles of •society •in Boston, a tnick roll and only congenial to one of Morbid tastes. It would take up too much of your space to give extracts. It is enough to say thot the, generality of the cases given are of the sober poor who live in cellars and attics, and for which they pay in rent from sa. per week for one small room, to 10e. and 12s. for two and three rooms. Make no mistake reader, we are not in London or Liverpool, we are in the land of which it used to be sling " that there was room enough for all." Spreading away out West to Colorado the panic takes in all the miners. Thousands of men are being deported from the moun- tains on tramp-trains. These are dividing themselVes between the eastern and western states. California is likely to have a stiff winter. Where my daughter works at they have discharged men sufficient to make a reduc- tion In the pay roll of £20,000 a month. But etibugh, I can fancy some of my readers shivering almost with horrors. As one of my fellovvidlers said to Illfi the other day .• He couldn't read his paper he had shoved it into the stove."</p>
        <p>CHORAL SERVICE. A special choral service was given in the Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening when portions of Men- delssolin's works were rendered. There was a very large congregation. The organist, Mr. W. H. Payton played two organ voluntaries " Andante Religioso Allegtetto " Sonata 4, and " Adagio" Sonata 1, Mendel- -1 ssolin. The choruses "How lovely are the messengers " and" Happy and blest are they" (St. Paul) were ren- dered by the choir, under the leadership of Mr. G. Wal- lington. In the solo and chorus, "My soul thirsteth for God" (42nd Psalm) the solo part was taken by Miss M. Payton, and Mrs. Dobson was the soloist in the solo and chorus ", Hear my Prayer." Mr. A. Griffiths sang the solo" If With all your hearts " (Elijah), and Mr. W. H. Wallingtoh sang the solo, in the solo and chorus" Lord God of Abraham " (Elijah). The whole of the pieces were excellently rendered, and redounded to the credit of every one concerned. A short address was given by the Pastor the Rev. J. Tongs who -conducted the devo- tional part of the service. The collection was in aid of the choir expenseS, and realised ZEI 58:6d. THE RAGGED SCHOOL.—A FRIEND'S DEATH. At the above school, on Sunday, reference was made to the great loss the school had sustained br the death of Mrs. HoWard. She PM an earnest and indefatigable worker In the various branches of the work of the school, and for many years to come her work as a teacher and in connection with the Band of Hope will long be remembered by everyone connected with the school. The deceased had also under her direction a large class of sewing girls, a party of whom, accompanied by Miss Monks and Miss Harding, journeyed to Hill Cliff burial ground (where she was interred last Thursday) on Sun- day afternoon, where, at the graveside of their late friend, they sang several of her favourite hymns. , PROMOTION OF THE REV. F. C. TIMINS. The Rev. F. C. Tim ins, who has been one of the as- sistant clergy at the Parish Church for nearly four years, is about to sever his connection with Warrington, having been presented to the Rectory of Brattleby, in Lincoln- shire. Mr. Timing has greatly endeared himself to tffe parishioners, and though they will be pleased to hear of his preferment tbey wilUdeeply regret his departure.— The Rev. F. L. H. Millard, lecturer at the Training Col- lege has been appointed diocesan inspector in the diocese of Col- lege,</p>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE. The P.S.A. meeting was held In Wycliffe Church on Sunday afternoon,when a large number of men attended. The meeting concluded the first six months of the ,Isociety, which has now a membership of 700, and the ' prizes gained by the necessary number of attendances, number over 400. The chair was occupied by Mr. Bauch, of Warrington,who said he thought be would be doing .a very great wrong to throw any obstacle in the way of the P.S.A. movement. Mr. T. S. Collins, of Liverpool, was the speaker, and he delivered an address en the "Relatiotiship of Christianity to Alcoholic Drinks" in which he considered that Christians neglected their duty who did not do their utmost in stemming the tide of intemperanoe. Mr. G„ U. Yonge sang the solo "Safe in the Arms of Jesus" ad Mr. Hipkiss contributed a clarionet solo, accompanied on the organ by Mr. W. H. Payton. The Rev. J. Yonge and Mr. R. Atkinson took part in the meeting, . THE REV. JOHN YONGE ON THE BACK DOOR QUESTION. At the Wycliffe Chanel on Sunday, the Rev. J. Yonge took tor his text-1 John iii. verse 8," For this purpose the Son of God was manifested, that he might destroy the works of the devil." After speaking of these words in relation to the personal life of men, Mr. Yonge pro- ceeded to refer to them in relation to their wider scope. He spoke of the wider idea of Christiae duty and enter- prise, social and physical, as well as spiritual, which now prevails among the churches, and went on to say —lam hoping, my friends, that this wider conviction of Christian duty which has found expression in the many parts of our own land of recent years, and which has aroused so much Christian activity in many churches, will find lodgement in all our minds, and impel many of us in the coming season at least to some new form of earnest Christian enterprise. I shall hope before long to lay carefully before yon, as a Christian congregation, the claims of the" Social Questions Union," which last winter was inaugurated in this town ; and I shall try my best to enlist your sympathy and active co-operation in its manifold endeavour to improve and uplift the people of Warrington morally, domestically, socially, intellectually. All the churches of this town need to enlist in home missionary work of the widest and broadest description ; not missionary work which aims alone at spiritual results in a distant future, but which aims at speedy and palpable results in a near future. There are " works of the devil " in this town that need to be assailed and destroyed, and which will be more rapidly destroyed by the formation of an earnest Christian public opinion concerning them. There are personal habits, domestic conditions; insanitary conditions, immoral surroundings, which need to be destroyed in the name of Christ, and in direct furtherance of His crusade against the devil. We have hundreds , living around iise for whom it is almost as impossible to live a decent life as it is for any heathens in heathendom. They are the slaves of circumstances, many of which have been created, or at least tolerated, by our Christian neglect, and we must liberate them. They are surrounded by temptations which they have not moral courage, possibly not physical stamina, to overcome. We must do something to put the temptations away from them. Last Thursday I spent the whole of the forenoon in the Borough Police Court, as I have done now on the same occasion nearly every year for many years. I went there, no less as a Christian minister than as a Christian man to help in seeking to destroy some ef the devil's works in this town. Before the actual proceedings comtnencNl which led me there, I was once again witness tithe usual almost daily prosecution of a number of wretched men and women, who had become diordeily through drink. I listened as the presiding magiqrate mingled his infliction of the customary penalty with moral reflections upon the " pity " that people should get into so degraded a condition; and I could not but reflect, as I listened, that the same Justice of the Peace who could so moralize to these wretched victims would bs perfectly ready within an hour to do his utmost to keep at full swing and unfettered freedom, to injure society, the very agencies which filled that felon's dock ! It is a terrible scandal upon our vaunted Christian civilization that so many professing Christian men can be found who will keep open every door for sin ! back as well as front ; and so continually and completely environ men in temptations and facilities to iniquityand misery. I cannot, however, forbear a grateful word of 'earnest acknowledgment to those justices who on Thursday last took so decided a stand in the interests of morality and soberness. Their decision marked, we may hope, the dawn of a better day for the moral condition of this town. They then dealt a blow at "the works of the devil," a blow, which, as Christain people, we may surely hope will he succeeded by yet others, which may help to "destroy "the evil habits, and the miserable homes of mans-, and to help them on to soberness eni goodness. We cannot make men sober against their will, or by Act of Parliament, or act of magistrates. But I believe it to be a positive Christian duty, to which we are called by the Lord ;Jesus Christ, to clear away well conditions of social life : to shut up as many doors as possible which we know to lead men to sin, to render it less possible for men to fall, and more possible for them to stand, and walk erec:, and straight, as good citizens, as worthy members of society, as true disciples of Jesus Christ. For this purpose the Sen of God was manifested that he might destroy the works of the devil. If that was the purpose of His life oueht it not to be more and more the nureeee of our. ?</p>
        <p>• THE REV. A. FREEMAN'S FAREWELL. On Sunday the Rev. A. Freeman preached farewell sermons at the Bold-st. and Bewsev-road Wesleyan Chapels, as his three years ministry in the Warrington circuit has come to a close. Re left Warrington on Thursday for his new circuit at illteley. He preached at Bold-st, in the morning and in the evening at Rewsev- road. There was a very large congregation. His ser- mon on Sunday evening. was to some extent a review of the duties of pastor, and his position toward his congre- gation. He preached from the words, "Therefore, my beloved brethren, be ye steadfast, unmovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, forasumeh as ye know that your labour ig not In vain in the Lord "-1 Cor, xv. 58. After the public service the Sacrament of the Lord's Supper was administered, the number of com- municants being larger than on any previous occasion during the rev, gentleman's pastorate of the church. The hymn "Good bye, till we meet again" (Sankey), was impressively sung, and . before 'Mr. Freeman left the chari4l he shook hands with most of the members of his congregation, who, while expressing their regret to lose him, expressed a hope that he would be as happy and as successful in his new circuit as he had been in Warrington.</p>
        <p>(To be co)ltinued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>Tim LICENSING SEssioNs.—The annual sessions for the Frodshain division of Cheshire were held on Wednesday, before Messrs. C. Reynolds, Thomas Rigby, and Dr. Steele. The annual report of Superintendent Meredith stated that no licensed victuallers or beersellers had been proceeded against, this satisfactory state of affairs having prevailed far two years. Despite the proximity of the Ship Canal works, there was again a decrease of 1 drunkenness. lb(' number of persons proceeded against was 30, of whom 27 were convicted, as compared with 43 persons proceeded against and 39 convicted during the preceding year—a decrease of 13 persons proceeded against. There were in the division 17 licensed vic- tuallers, eight beersellers. and one off-license, total 28, giving an average of ?.58 of the population t each license. On the euggestion of the superintendent, the magistrates ordered that publicans applying for an extension or special license should give two days' notice of their intention in writing. in order that the police might furnish the bench with a report as to the conduct of the house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>OLI) AGE.</head>
        <p>OLI) AGE.</p>
        <p>Father, the sun is getting low, The eventide has come; And weary, halting as I go, I long to be at home. Since thou dost own me as thy child, • A wayward one 'tis true ; Safe guard me through this desert wild Where dangers still pursue. Mr strength decays. 'tis weakness all, .0 take my hand in thine; Fiero from thy side I cannot fall, Upheld by power divine. Those eyes are dim and scarce can trace The goal I long to see; 0 let the brightness of thy face Reveal it all to me. And, when in loneliness and tears, I mourn my comrades dead : Mr father, then thylove appears • Like balm in mercy shed. The darkness deepens, night is nigh ; The grave's long starless night; But will it not be sweet to die, With endless rest Insight. • --</p>
        <p>lONA.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF COMMR,BIONERB AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday next the annual election of five members of the Runcorn Board of Improvement Commissioners will take place at the Market Hall, Bridge-st. The poll wilt be open at nine o'clock, and close at four o'clock. Mr. Frank 'Wiswall, ex- chairman of the Board, is the returning officer, and voters may depend upon having the best arrange- ments possible under the obsolete, not to say dis- graceful mode in which the election has to' be conducted. The contest promises to be the most keen and exciting that has taken place since the "Black list" was defeated some eight years ago. On the present occasion there is no list whatever. Each candidate is working for his own cause, with the exception of Messrs. Hough and Jones, who are joining their interests. The retiring members are 'Wars. S. Taylor, W. Fisher, R. Abel and E. W. Htimplireys, and there is a fifth place vacant through the resignation of Mr. P. Smith. With the exception of Mr. Fisher, the retiring members offer themselves for re-election. In his valedictory ad- dress, Mr. Fisher thanks the ratepayers for their kindness during the twelve years he has had the privilege of representing them on the Board. Mr. Fisher gives no reason lor his retirement from pub- lic affairs, and the ommission of his name from the roll of our local rulers in the future will be a source ef regret to many. There are seven "new" candi- dates in the field, viz., Messrs. E. Itnison, W. Mellor, T. Jones, A. Hough, Walter Collier, Thos. Dean, and It. Roberts. In our advertising columns will bo found the addresses of the various candidates. The. Ratepayers Association announce a public meeting in Bethesda Lecture Hall on Tuesday even- ing:, The whole of the candidates will be invited to speak.</p>
        <p>• • - MEETING OF RATEPAYERS. Under the auspices of the Runcorn Ratepayers . Association, a public meeting was held in the Bethesda ' Lecture Hall on Thursday night: There was a good attendance. Mr. B. Kniveton presided, and on the Platform were Messrs. W. Mellor, W. Collier, R. Roberts, (candidates), and Williams, see. (pro tern). Mr. Williams said Mr. Imison had written stating he was staying in North Wales, and was unable to be present. Be thanked the meinbers for their promised support.—Mr. Leach, a member of the Board of Commissioners had also written a letter expressing regret that he could not take part in the excitement of a public meeting. He wished them all success. They wanted to put on the Board men who were sanitary reformers, their town having the notorie- ty of being the most unhealthy in the kingdom with the abnormal death-rate of 33 per thousand, whereas it , ought to be one of the most healthy towns in England, situated as it was for both sanitary and surface drain- age. Diseases followed close on each other with deadly march, first typhoid fever, then smallpox, Eng- lish cholera, &amp;c. Money, which was spent in battling -with these fell diseases should be spent in providing . baths,pleasure grounds,and other Modern requirements, and insidding the town of insanitary property and injurious nuisances, that were now causing such havoc amongst their families, and swallowing up ratepayers' Money that ought to go for better perposes. He urged ' them to vote for none but sanitaty reformers. The 'Chairman in the course of a speech said the fact of a man being a teetotaller or •otherwise, had nothing to do with this question. The business of the Commissioners was to see that the roads and streets were kept in pro- per condition, and to look after the sanitation of the town. The latter work was of the supremest impor- tance, and it had always been a mystery to him why workingmen took such slight interest in the affairs of , their own town. Their health was their capital. and if , the condition of the town was unsanitary, they bight be ' stricken down with typhoid fever and then whet became of wife and family. Rates should be kept as low as pcssible,if they bad efficient ad ministration, but it was no use cutting down the rates a penny or twopence,and then be ravaged with smallpox and typhoid fever from one ' end of the town to the other. Whatever was spent upon sanitation, if laid out wisely, was well spent money. What they wanted was twenty shillings to the pound. (Applause.) Some of their Commissioners were the most guilty persons in the town with respect to the sanitation of their property. (Applause.) Men who posed to be philanthropists and public spirited Men, had to be threatened with legalproceedings before they would put their property to rights. Shame upon them ! If they were honest men they would either put their pro- . perty right or retire from the Board, and go into that oblivion from which they ought never to have come. (Applause.) He urged the ratepayers to vote for men who would put public welfare before private interest and who would discharge their duty like men. (Applause.) Me. Collier said he felt it was the duty of the rising generation to take a part in local affairs and watch the interests of the people who would have to pay rates, when the older generation, which was well represented on the Beard had passed away. According to the law of averages, it was quite I,kely that he would have to pay the piper 30 years hence, and, probably it Would be quite a stock tale with him to tell how the Commissioners of 1893 made such a fearful bungle of that scheme. (Ap- plause.) Since he had become a candidate he had been given the most feariul warnings. Be must not do this that or the other. Last year there was a tremendous crop of promises—baths, lower rates, beaek sanitation, and a way to Weston Point, and in fact he believed if they had asked fertile Town Hall as a skating rink they could have had it. (Laughter.) The men who made these promises had not the slightest experience, or they would not have done so. By following up the local papersehey could easily find that the , old veterans on tlifk Board, dropped like a ton of bricks du the man when ho brought forward his prospects, or they made him pliant, and experience has shown what 'the result of that was% (Hear, hear.) The " large rate- payer" cry so frequently heard at election times was all rubbish. The interests of the large majority might be identic 41 up to a certain point, but the working man living in his own house would make the best Commissioner, because in the interests of his wife and family would see that the sanitary conditions were kept right. With a roan owning say fifty houses, and who did not live near his property, the case was different. They never found these property owners out Side the Health Committee, they wore always within that charmed circle ; and however lunch the nuisance inspector might feel inclined to do what was right, if he found the property of these men in a bad state of sane, cation, what could lie do? He was between the devil and the deep sea, how could lie go against 'men who were actually his masters. (Hear, hear.) They did not hear of a policeman summoning a. magistrate and that was exactly the position of the nuisance inspector. (Ap- plause.) He was a large ratepayer himself, and the rates were very heavy ; but if he was electod on the Health Committee he should think it no favour if the nuisance inspector did pot make him pay his due. If tie were returned he would pay attention to the sanitary condition of property work which had been abominably neglected in the past. (Applause.) Some persons might object to him on the score of want of experience. If they wanted good workmen they must have appren- tices, and if they returned him he would initiato himself thoroughly into the werk• and when he had thoroughly learnt the ropes they would be sure to hear from him if things were not being done as they ought to be. (Ap- plause.) Mr. Mellor expressed his thanks to the Aseoeiatien for adopting his name as one of the candidates, and if elected he would faithfully carry out needed reforms. tie should make it his aim to conduct the election as pure and straight as possible. Mr. Collier had referred to six yea rs servitude on the Board. He was one of the Individuals that perhaps Mr. Collier referred to. ' Well, he had served his apprenticeship he knew his work, , and if they returned him he would get into harness at ' once. (Apprause.) He should not need to serve an apprenticeship. (Laughter.) With reference to the ' increased rates, Mr. Mellor pointed out that about 18 years ago, the rate was Is 6,1 or is 81 in the £ and the Commissioners allowed everything to go to the dogs. Streets were not made, sanitary conditions were neg- lected, everything was neglected. The people of. 16 years ago had not paid their fair share of the rates of Runcorn. (Hear, hear.) He contended that if there had been a2s rate 18 Fears ago, there would have been a two shilling rate at the present day. (Hear, hear.) The town ouget to be kept up to the mark and every year Pay for itself. Why should the present ratepayers be paying rates that people of sixteen years ago ought to have paid, and many of whom had left the town ? (Applause.) Sanitary matters had been much neglected.' When lie was on the Board, it did not matter what pro- ject he brou'ght forward, it was never taken any notice of. He was just like one or two members at the present time—in a minority. (Hear, hear.) As be street-making he showed that for 30 years the red/lents in many streets had been paying rates without the adva%tages passessed by those who lived la theroughfares adopted as public ones. Many persons also had purchased property in ignorance that they wofild be called upon to incur expenses for street-making, 'which was an injustice. What should be done was that when a street was built on each side itiShould be taken over by the Authority. He was favourable to the provision of baths, believing that the ratepayers would concur with the scheme, even if it meant an increase of Id on the rates. Recreation grounds were much needed, and he would support any feasible project. With regard to the waterworks, all he would say was that, in his opinion, better terms might have been obtained. If returned he would serve then to the best of his ability. (Applause.) The Chairman expressed regret that Mr. Imison could not be present% to explain his views. There could only bO one opinion of Mr. Irnison's capacity for a Commis- sioner. It was a great pity thee he resigned a few years ago. His splendid abilities, upright, honest, straight- forward way of dealing with public questions were the best qualiftcations possible for any man. If Mr. Imiaon were returned he would be of great service . to the town and a credit to) it. Mr. Roberts then delivered an address.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said Mr. Taylor and Mr. Dean had iwlen expected to he present, but unfortunately both gentle- rueu were away from home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>PENKETE</head>
        <p>PENKETE</p>
        <p>Tag Coax, Surtr.E.—During the past week this village has been besieged by quite an army of the colliers from St. Helens and district coal pits. Brass bands, quite frequent, and the latest move on their part is than of a cart pulled by several colliers going round Oa ' tpiuses and farms, the colliers getting all they can in the form of potatoes, vegetables, &amp;c., the carts garnished with large placards, picks, Davy lamps, i‘c. Up to the present no ccmplaints have been heard,, but alarm is Occasionally expressed by timid people, PAROCHIAL TRIP.—On-Thursday the annual parochial trip took place, the destination being Southport. Ti?ii°. ' is one of the most popular trips that goes from the 10 age, and this year eclipsed all previous ones. Over 2.,,., .1 persons assembled at Sankey Station to board the 8 4V train that was to carry them to their destination. morning was gloomy with a light rain, but the weather cleared up nicely, though the air was rather chilly. Th° • party, on their arrival, dispersal thems 'lye; allmt the ' town, the sea shore seeming to be the central point of n knv terest. Dinner was partaken of at 12 30, at the Scas- brick Hotel, about 120 persons being present. Th 3 Beg I , E. S. Jackson and Rev B. Jones presided over the dinnerl: ' the arrangements being in the hands of Messrs. vv. s.keo,l ton and W. Taylor, churchwardens. Tea was provid.. at the same place. The party arrived home about 8.15, after a most enjoyable outing.</p>
