        <p>ELECTION OF COMMISSIONERS. TO THE PROPERTY OWNERS AND RATEPAYERS OF RUNCORN. LADIES AND GENTLEAI EN,— I have pleasure in again offering myself as a candi- date for your sufferages . My labours in your service during the past three years will afford the best and truest inditation of my probable course of action in the future should yon fav- our me with your confidence. I claim to have broken no pledge, to have left no promise unfulfilled, but according to the best of my judgrnent and ability to have faithfully discharged the duties devolving upon me, and I trust I may now receive at your hands a practical recognition of services cheerfully undertaken and conscientiously rendered. I remain, Ladies and Gentlemen, Yours faithfully, B. WYNNE HUMPHREYS.</p>
        <p>p UNC R NI'SP olio :4)31V1 sEg HisiTsT ACT. ELECTION o l F TO THE OWNERS AND OCCUPIERS. LADIES AND GENTLP:MEN. Three years ego I appealed to you for your sufferages at the Commissioners' Election. and you kindly replied by placing me in the honourable position of second on the poll. I trust that my votes at the meetings of the Board as your repretentative have been cast in a way that has given you satisfaction in every respect. My term of office having expired, I have to ask for a renewal of your confidence, and this I beg to do on the lines of my last address, viz., as a.perfectly Independent Candidate. Should you again honour me with a seat on the Board, it will be my object to serve you and watch your interests in the future as in the past, that is. with economy, zeal, and assiduity. I have the honour to be, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your obedient Servant, RICHARD ABEL. Tower House, 0 reenway-road, Runcorn, August, 31st, H 193.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN IMPROVEMENT COMMISSIONERS' SLEC (lON. THURSDAY, SEPTEMBEE 7th, 3893. TO TM' RATEIPAYERS OF RUNCORN. Lamm§ AND GENTLEMEI4, On many occasions I hav e been asked to allow my name to appear among the list of candidates at the forthcom- ing election. I have much pleasure in placing ray services at your disposal. Raving been a member of the Board for a period .Of six years I am thoroughly acquainted with its business and trust thatmy past conduct will be taken as a guieteto my future actions. Experience has taught me the wisdom a making rash promises which are varely fulfilled, and if honoured with your confidence, I will content myself with saying, that I shall always endeavour to study your bestinterests and to support any measures calcallated to secure (ho improvement and prosperity of this important town. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen. Yours most faithfully, WILLIAM MELLOR. VOTE FOR MELLOR.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF COMMISSIONERS. THURSDAY. SEPTEMBER 7TH, 18l.G. TO THE RATEPAYERS OF RUNCORN. LADIES AND GUNTLEMEN, A number of Electors .ay " that I can be useful to yon on the Board of Corn missioners," and at their request I take this opportunity to offer my services. Through your goodwill I have had anne experience of public work in several departments; and beg leave to point to my previous conduct as an augury of what if favoured with a renewal of yOuriconfidence—my future action is likely to be. It is always difficult for outsiders to form a •,correct judge ment upon subjects with which they are not thoroughly familiar, and any attempt at criticism of the Board's past policy might result in an exhibition of ignorance which not doubt all candidates will try to avoid. If elected, no et oft of mine shall be spared to keep the con- fidence gained, and, to deserve it more and more, I am, Ladles and Gentlemen, Your obedient servant, ED WIN IMISON. VOTE roil ImisoN.</p>
        <p>LECTION OF COMMISSIONERS, 1893. TO THE ELECTORS. LADIES AND GENTNEMEN, I beg to (fifer myself as a candidate for one of the vacancies at the coming election. In the vast there have been so many pledges made by gentlemen seeking your suffraces, who Since their election have been Wl:noted with such wretchedly bad m, mories. that I feel it would be out t.f rlace to make any &amp;finite promises, at least until some of the old ones have been fulfilled. If you elect me. I will always consider you: Interests, and strengthen the hands of those who are promoting anything for the good of Runcorn. Out town is now at a crisis, and will be either marred or made during the next few years, and to accomplish the latter I consider it is the duty of every indi-idual Commissioner to question what he does not understand, especially abstruse borrowing schemes, and then our town will he managed (as it ought to he) by eighteen Commissioners instead of about Ewe. Trusting you will favour one with you, confidence and sappori, I am, Ladies and Gentlemen, Yours truly. WAT.THII COLLIEE. Church-street. Runcorn, August 25th. 1893.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF COMMISSIONERS. 1893. TO THE RATEPAYERS OF RUNCORN. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— On the '7th of September you will be calleriamort to elect 'Fire 'Members tp represent you on the Board of 'Com- missioners. Being requested by numerous ratepayers to offer .myself cc a candislate, lave consented to do so. ! Not being the nominee of any particular trade or party,l I shall, if elected have a free hand to do the best I eanfor the general body of ratepayers. Being a large ratepayer. I feel the bnrthen of the increased Improvement Rate, and it would be my study and atm to keep it as low as possible consistent with the efficiency of Sanitary arrangements. The unmade streets, in my opinion Are not getting their fair share of attention in the shave of lighting and cleansing. Baths also are a necessity, and any well-considered scheme for providing them would have my warm support. Recreation grounds wpuld he a great boon to the rising generation, if they could be acquired on easy terms. These and all other questions for the benefit of the Town would receive vny earnest ettentlow. it you should do me the honour of electing Ma. lam, Ladies and Oentlemen. Yours obediently, THOMAS DEAN. pracklev-street, Runcorn. Ang. 28th. 1893.</p>
